{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014VjKxnof72jiZQyFyfKJyH","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tCompleted-GMT: 2026-10-11 20:34:00 GMT\n2\tCompleted-Local: 2026-10-12 03:34:00 Asia/Bangkok\n3\t\n4\t(Host clock as read by `date -u` at completion; as in the previous\n5\treport, this host's clock precedes the brief's Created-GMT.)\n6\t\n7\t# Architect report: revision of the discovery-order numbering note after the codex review\n8\t\n9\tEdited: `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md`\n10\t(rewritten, 724 lines) and the prototype\n11\t`collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj` (rewritten, 751 lines;\n12\trun log at `collab/numbering-probe/run.log`). Nothing staged or\n13\tcommitted. The 6a code was read from branch `srvm-phase6a` (`d77da9f0`)\n14\tthrough the probe project's copies, as before.\n15\t\n16\t## The repair in one paragraph\n17\t\n18\tThe content encoding now numbers the cursor cells *inside* an element\n19\tprovisionally, in the element's own discovery order, relative to the\n20\tenclosing numbering (de Bruijn style: `[X X]` spells `[(u0) (u0)]`,\n21\t`[Y Z]` spells `[(u0) (u1)]`), and the encoding of an element is the\n22\tspelling produced by a *local run* of the same procedure over it (nested\n23\tcontainers sorted, nested ties deferred and re-examined, what it cannot\n24\tresolve spelled `[:tie …]`). There is one worklist per run, so a tie\n25\tdiscovered inside an entered element joins the root worklist and is\n26\tre-examined. The orbit rule is replaced by a **component rule**: at a\n27\tfixpoint, tied elements that share an unnumbered cursor cell form a\n28\tcomponent; a component holding two elements of one group is the D5\n29\trefusal; otherwise components are encoded by a local run over their\n30\telements in group order, sorted, and the least run is entered (equal\n31\tencodings are isomorphic, so host order among them is harmless, proved\n32\tas lemma C). The proof is an isomorphism-invariance theorem with four\n33\tlemmas (§3 of the note).\n34\t\n35\t## Per item\n36\t\n37\t**1. BLOCKING, orbit lemma unsound.** Changed: the spelling (note §2\n38\t\"Spelling\"), D4 (components, component encodings, least-run entry), §3\n39\t(theorem, lemmas A–D). I evaluated the orchestrator's direction and\n40\tadopted it, with one addition it needs to be sound: local first-occurrence\n41\tindices inside an element are not enough when the element itself holds\n42\tnested unordered containers or when tied elements share cells across\n43\tgroups, so the element's key is the output of a full local run (which\n44\tresolves nested ties by the same rules and spells unresolved ones as\n45\t`[:tie]`), and the orbit candidates are components, not elements. The\n46\tnote shows why the weaker version fails: `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` and\n47\t`[#{P Q} #{R S}]` would key equal under single-pass local indices with\n48\t`[:tie]` parts, yet they are not isomorphic, and entering them in either\n49\torder gives different rows; the local run with the component rule keys\n50\tthem differently (prototype fixture `b35b8e12…`). Verified: review A\n51\t`#{[X X] [Y Z]}` and B `{X X, Y Z}` give one root for both enumerations\n52\tand after renaming plus shuffling; the roots `65ba4530…` and `409548a7…`\n53\tequal the digests codex computed for the `X=1, Y=2, Z=3` numbering, an\n54\tindependent cross-check of the emission. Hand derivations in note §6.\n55\t\n56\t**2. BLOCKING, nested tie groups.** Changed: D3 states that entering an\n57\telement inside a group runs D1 on it, so newly discovered groups join\n58\tthe one worklist of the run and are re-examined next round; D4 and D5\n59\trecurse through component local runs; a \"Totality\" paragraph requires a\n60\tbijection onto `1…n` before emission, asserted by the implementation as\n61\ta defect (never a refusal, never a partial carrier). Prototype:\n62\t`discovery-numbering` throws \"numbering is not total\" with the missing\n63\tand extra cells; it fired once during revision on a malformed fixture of\n64\tmine (a census missing the worked frame's pending cursor cell), which is\n65\tthe behaviour wanted. Verified: review C `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}` numbers all\n66\tfour cells, root `18b70de6…` for the state and for its renaming (and 6a\n67\treaches the same root); `[#{#{X Y} #{Z W}} X]` numbers `X cc-1` first and\n68\tresolves the inner sets by the fixpoint and the component rule, root\n69\t`6ea03f9b…`.\n70\t\n71\t**3. HIGH, the refusal class.** Changed: F4 states the predicate in one\n72\tsentence (two elements of one guest set or map with equal keys, linked by\n73\ta chain of shared unnumbered cursor cells at a fixpoint), separates \"not\n74\tcertified by this procedure\" from \"non-interchangeable\" with examples\n75\ton each side, withdraws the rarity claim, and says the real-state rate\n76\tis unmeasured because the repository has no version-3 task corpus (Stage\n77\tE scenarios and the v2 handoff census tests hold cursor cells but no v3\n78\tlift exists before 6b). `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` now numbers (root `92747c0f…`,\n79\tequal to 6a's). Verified by a permissive diagnostic mode in the\n80\tprototype (D5 replaced by host-order entry, 24 shuffles): the loop plus\n81\t2-cycle, the 2-cycle and the 3-cycle give one root (interchangeable,\n82\trefused anyway); the path `#{[A B] [B C]}` gives 2 roots and the\n83\tseven-cell state 4 roots (genuinely need a tie-break). Fixture census:\n84\tof the repository's own fixtures only the two built for the purpose hit\n85\tD5. Random census under a tie-biased generator: 25 of 2000 value states\n86\t(15 witnessed non-interchangeable, 10 not distinguished by 12 shuffles),\n87\t8 of 200 frame-bearing, 2 of 100 with children; the note reports these\n88\tas rates under that distribution, not as a claim about real states.\n89\t\n90\t**4. HIGH, decode.** Changed: §2 \"Validation and decode\" specifies that\n91\tonly a lift-side `:yin.k/non-portable` of kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`\n92\traised by the recomputation is translated to `:yin.k/undecodable` of the\n93\tsame kind (path prefixed by the root id), resource refusals propagate as\n94\tthemselves, no other non-portable kind can arise there because step 5\n95\tran, and the validator order (after step 5, before `validate-frames`)\n96\tstays; A4 carries it into carrier §8 step 6 and the diagnostics\n97\tparagraph. Prototype `decode*` implements the translation and the\n98\tcomparison. Unverified: the prototype's recompute runs after 6a's\n99\t`decode*` returns (so after `validate-frames`), because 6a's `decode*` is\n100\tprivate and monolithic; the note states the required order and the\n101\tacceptance item 16 tests it.\n102\t\n103\t**5. MEDIUM, prototype follows the spec.** Changed: the prototype is\n104\tschema-driven (one `enter-node!`/`spell-node` pair over `row-table`\n105\tdescriptors plus two synthetic kinds, `:entry` and `:component`);\n106\tnumbered cells spell `[:cursor-cell [:n k]]` with `k` an integer and\n107\tlocal ones `[:cursor-cell [:u j body]]`; parked-table values are entered\n108\tthrough their `[:ref :act :tail]` descriptor (the old `:V` bug is gone);\n109\tinstall children are numbered by the same procedure in the prototype's\n110\town emitter; `:window` is entered in numeric register order. New\n111\tfixtures: act window, K-frame window, environment matched to the free\n112\tenvironment, and a parked act at a park site (code from the frame tests'\n113\t`:park` segment), all decodable. Search: 2000 random value states with\n114\tnested sets, sets of pairs including `[X X]`, cursor-cell map keys and\n115\tcells aliased across containers, plus 200 frame-bearing and 100\n116\twith-children states; renaming every cursor cell and shuffling every\n117\tset, map and census: **0 invariance failures, 0 decode failures**,\n118\t2033 decode round trips ok, 232 not decodable through 6a's own\n119\trecompute under its default budget (the probe decodes through 6a's\n120\t`decode*`, which still runs 6a's numbering; a probe limitation, not a\n121\tnumbering outcome), 35 refusals. The decode round trip caught one real\n122\tprototype bug during revision: a memoized local run returned the whole\n123\tnumbering of another element's context, so an unrelated cursor cell was\n124\tadopted and shifted the provisional indices (seed 1016 gave two roots\n125\tover 40 shuffles). The memo now returns the numbering restricted to the\n126\titem's own cells; the note's \"Memoization\" paragraph records the\n127\trequirement. Also fixed in the generator during revision: reference-free\n128\tvectors, and set elements equal under decoded equality, both refused by\n129\tdecode and both generator artifacts.\n130\t\n131\t**6. MEDIUM, \"linear work\".** Changed: §2 \"Work\" states\n132\t`W = O(P · (d + 1) · (n + 1) · log m)` with memoized local runs (`P`\n133\texpanded positions, counted per reference so DAG expansion is bounded by\n134\t`P` rather than the row count; `d` container depth; `n` cursor cells;\n135\t`m` largest container) and `O(P · (n + 1)^(d+1) · log m)` without, and\n136\tmakes `:work` charge one unit per position visited or spelled and one\n137\tper literal byte copied, so every spelling byte is paid for before\n138\tallocation; `:depth`, `:cursor-cells`, `:rows` and `:bytes` stay and\n139\ttogether bound what a spelling can copy; `:search` and `:label-bytes`\n140\tgo. The recommendation line now says \"work bounded and accounted\" instead\n141\tof \"linear work\". A5 updates carrier §10.10's wording accordingly.\n142\tUnverified: the bound is argued, not measured; the prototype does not\n143\tcharge budgets.\n144\t\n145\t**7. MEDIUM, stale wording.** Changed: §0 defines canonical traversal\n146\torder versus host enumeration order; A2 now replaces the whole tail of\n147\tthe Validation paragraph including \"no encounter-order tie-break ever\n148\tdecides validity\" and \"never falls back to encounter order\"; A5 replaces\n149\t§10.10's \"never falls back to encounter order\" and answer 9's\n150\t\"refinement-and-search\"; A8 gives three explicit replacements for\n151\tcontinuation §5.5 (the body-label sentence with its \"step 1\" reference,\n152\tthe \"sorting their elements' encodings (step 2)\" sentence, and a\n153\tqualification of \"never derived from traversal order\" to *host*\n154\ttraversal order); A4 corrects the \"two kinds\" counts in carrier §8 and\n155\t§11 and states the `[:child]` and integer `[:n k]` changes in A1.\n156\t\n157\t**8. LOW.** Changed: the portable-id example holds the id counter equal\n158\t(task B consumes one unrelated fresh name), and the paragraph concedes\n159\tthe review's point that an id recomputed from structure is the\n160\tcanonicalization problem again; engine citations are given against\n161\tbranch `srvm-phase6a` (`gen-id` 227–230, `issue-ref` 311–323,\n162\t`handle-cursor-unminted` 557–568, `gensym` 2312–2318, the `:stream/cursor`\n163\teffect site 2595–2602, read with `git show` on the branch); D1 and the\n164\tF1 table say `:window` is in numeric ascending register order as the\n165\tcarrier specifies.\n166\t\n167\t## Pinned ids, old → new\n168\t\n169\t- Unchanged from the first draft and from 6a: `fab223…`, `c4a7c7…`,\n170\t  `07eda35d…`, `da69c679…`, `49d4478c…`, `536c810b…`, ten-cell root\n171\t  `6e7c7a6b…`.\n172\t- Unchanged from the first draft (differ from 6a): `a3713a5e…`,\n173\t  `4993b5b1…`.\n174\t- First draft gave two roots depending on enumeration → one root:\n175\t  review A `#{[X X] [Y Z]}` `65ba4530…`; review B `{X X, Y Z}` `409548a7…`.\n176\t- First draft refused → numbered: `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` `92747c0f…`.\n177\t- First draft left unnumbered (emitted source ids) → numbered: review C\n178\t  `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}` `18b70de6…`.\n179\t- New pins: `[#{X Y} X]` `7dc3d5c4…`; `[#{#{X Y} #{Z W}} X]` `6ea03f9b…`;\n180\t  `#{[#{X Y} #{X Y}] [#{P Q} #{R S}]}` `b35b8e12…`.\n181\t- Still refused: loop plus 2-cycle (6a's `55ff168e…` stays removed; `L`\n182\t  now gets `cc-1` before the refusal), seven-cell.\n183\t- Every numbering was derived by hand in note §6 and the digest taken\n184\t  from the codec under that numbering; A, B, C and `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]`\n185\t  agree with an independent computation (codex's, or 6a's).\n186\t\n187\t## Unverified or disputed\n188\t\n189\t- No codex counterexample is disputed; all three reproduced on the first\n190\t  prototype and are fixed.\n191\t- The prototype's decode check runs after 6a's frame validation\n192\t  (item 4); the real codec must place it at step 6.\n193\t- Three-host runs, kondo, budgets charging: not run or not implemented\n194\t  (documents and a JVM scratch prototype only).\n195\t- The real-state rate of D5 is unmeasured (no v3 task corpus; item 3).\n196\t- The work bound is argued, not measured (item 6).\n197\t- Uncommitted changes in the p6a worktree, if any, remain invisible.\n198\t\n199\t## Owner questions\n200\t\n201\tUnchanged from the first report, restated in note §9: accept the D5\n202\tclass as documented (recommended yes); refusal kind\n203\t`:unresolved-cursor-cells` rather than `:resource-limit` (recommended).\n204\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7984b61f-639f-4af2-b00a-5b9b7d554f75","uuid":"12883128-018e-4fe5-808c-2a52cc67b4c5","timestamp":"2026-10-12T06:20:07.187Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-numbering/collab/1792150000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-revise.report.md","content":"Completed-GMT: 2026-10-11 20:34:00 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-12 03:34:00 Asia/Bangkok\n\n(Host clock as read by `date -u` at completion; as in the previous\nreport, this host's clock precedes the brief's Created-GMT.)\n\n# Architect report: revision of the discovery-order numbering note after the codex review\n\nEdited: `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md`\n(rewritten, 724 lines) and the prototype\n`collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj` (rewritten, 751 lines;\nrun log at `collab/numbering-probe/run.log`). Nothing staged or\ncommitted. The 6a code was read from branch `srvm-phase6a` (`d77da9f0`)\nthrough the probe project's copies, as before.\n\n## The repair in one paragraph\n\nThe content encoding now numbers the cursor cells *inside* an element\nprovisionally, in the element's own discovery order, relative to the\nenclosing numbering (de Bruijn style: `[X X]` spells `[(u0) (u0)]`,\n`[Y Z]` spells `[(u0) (u1)]`), and the encoding of an element is the\nspelling produced by a *local run* of the same procedure over it (nested\ncontainers sorted, nested ties deferred and re-examined, what it cannot\nresolve spelled `[:tie …]`). There is one worklist per run, so a tie\ndiscovered inside an entered element joins the root worklist and is\nre-examined. The orbit rule is replaced by a **component rule**: at a\nfixpoint, tied elements that share an unnumbered cursor cell form a\ncomponent; a component holding two elements of one group is the D5\nrefusal; otherwise components are encoded by a local run over their\nelements in group order, sorted, and the least run is entered (equal\nencodings are isomorphic, so host order among them is harmless, proved\nas lemma C). The proof is an isomorphism-invariance theorem with four\nlemmas (§3 of the note).\n\n## Per item\n\n**1. BLOCKING, orbit lemma unsound.** Changed: the spelling (note §2\n\"Spelling\"), D4 (components, component encodings, least-run entry), §3\n(theorem, lemmas A–D). I evaluated the orchestrator's direction and\nadopted it, with one addition it needs to be sound: local first-occurrence\nindices inside an element are not enough when the element itself holds\nnested unordered containers or when tied elements share cells across\ngroups, so the element's key is the output of a full local run (which\nresolves nested ties by the same rules and spells unresolved ones as\n`[:tie]`), and the orbit candidates are components, not elements. The\nnote shows why the weaker version fails: `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` and\n`[#{P Q} #{R S}]` would key equal under single-pass local indices with\n`[:tie]` parts, yet they are not isomorphic, and entering them in either\norder gives different rows; the local run with the component rule keys\nthem differently (prototype fixture `b35b8e12…`). Verified: review A\n`#{[X X] [Y Z]}` and B `{X X, Y Z}` give one root for both enumerations\nand after renaming plus shuffling; the roots `65ba4530…` and `409548a7…`\nequal the digests codex computed for the `X=1, Y=2, Z=3` numbering, an\nindependent cross-check of the emission. Hand derivations in note §6.\n\n**2. BLOCKING, nested tie groups.** Changed: D3 states that entering an\nelement inside a group runs D1 on it, so newly discovered groups join\nthe one worklist of the run and are re-examined next round; D4 and D5\nrecurse through component local runs; a \"Totality\" paragraph requires a\nbijection onto `1…n` before emission, asserted by the implementation as\na defect (never a refusal, never a partial carrier). Prototype:\n`discovery-numbering` throws \"numbering is not total\" with the missing\nand extra cells; it fired once during revision on a malformed fixture of\nmine (a census missing the worked frame's pending cursor cell), which is\nthe behaviour wanted. Verified: review C `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}` numbers all\nfour cells, root `18b70de6…` for the state and for its renaming (and 6a\nreaches the same root); `[#{#{X Y} #{Z W}} X]` numbers `X cc-1` first and\nresolves the inner sets by the fixpoint and the component rule, root\n`6ea03f9b…`.\n\n**3. HIGH, the refusal class.** Changed: F4 states the predicate in one\nsentence (two elements of one guest set or map with equal keys, linked by\na chain of shared unnumbered cursor cells at a fixpoint), separates \"not\ncertified by this procedure\" from \"non-interchangeable\" with examples\non each side, withdraws the rarity claim, and says the real-state rate\nis unmeasured because the repository has no version-3 task corpus (Stage\nE scenarios and the v2 handoff census tests hold cursor cells but no v3\nlift exists before 6b). `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` now numbers (root `92747c0f…`,\nequal to 6a's). Verified by a permissive diagnostic mode in the\nprototype (D5 replaced by host-order entry, 24 shuffles): the loop plus\n2-cycle, the 2-cycle and the 3-cycle give one root (interchangeable,\nrefused anyway); the path `#{[A B] [B C]}` gives 2 roots and the\nseven-cell state 4 roots (genuinely need a tie-break). Fixture census:\nof the repository's own fixtures only the two built for the purpose hit\nD5. Random census under a tie-biased generator: 25 of 2000 value states\n(15 witnessed non-interchangeable, 10 not distinguished by 12 shuffles),\n8 of 200 frame-bearing, 2 of 100 with children; the note reports these\nas rates under that distribution, not as a claim about real states.\n\n**4. HIGH, decode.** Changed: §2 \"Validation and decode\" specifies that\nonly a lift-side `:yin.k/non-portable` of kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`\nraised by the recomputation is translated to `:yin.k/undecodable` of the\nsame kind (path prefixed by the root id), resource refusals propagate as\nthemselves, no other non-portable kind can arise there because step 5\nran, and the validator order (after step 5, before `validate-frames`)\nstays; A4 carries it into carrier §8 step 6 and the diagnostics\nparagraph. Prototype `decode*` implements the translation and the\ncomparison. Unverified: the prototype's recompute runs after 6a's\n`decode*` returns (so after `validate-frames`), because 6a's `decode*` is\nprivate and monolithic; the note states the required order and the\nacceptance item 16 tests it.\n\n**5. MEDIUM, prototype follows the spec.** Changed: the prototype is\nschema-driven (one `enter-node!`/`spell-node` pair over `row-table`\ndescriptors plus two synthetic kinds, `:entry` and `:component`);\nnumbered cells spell `[:cursor-cell [:n k]]` with `k` an integer and\nlocal ones `[:cursor-cell [:u j body]]`; parked-table values are entered\nthrough their `[:ref :act :tail]` descriptor (the old `:V` bug is gone);\ninstall children are numbered by the same procedure in the prototype's\nown emitter; `:window` is entered in numeric register order. New\nfixtures: act window, K-frame window, environment matched to the free\nenvironment, and a parked act at a park site (code from the frame tests'\n`:park` segment), all decodable. Search: 2000 random value states with\nnested sets, sets of pairs including `[X X]`, cursor-cell map keys and\ncells aliased across containers, plus 200 frame-bearing and 100\nwith-children states; renaming every cursor cell and shuffling every\nset, map and census: **0 invariance failures, 0 decode failures**,\n2033 decode round trips ok, 232 not decodable through 6a's own\nrecompute under its default budget (the probe decodes through 6a's\n`decode*`, which still runs 6a's numbering; a probe limitation, not a\nnumbering outcome), 35 refusals. The decode round trip caught one real\nprototype bug during revision: a memoized local run returned the whole\nnumbering of another element's context, so an unrelated cursor cell was\nadopted and shifted the provisional indices (seed 1016 gave two roots\nover 40 shuffles). The memo now returns the numbering restricted to the\nitem's own cells; the note's \"Memoization\" paragraph records the\nrequirement. Also fixed in the generator during revision: reference-free\nvectors, and set elements equal under decoded equality, both refused by\ndecode and both generator artifacts.\n\n**6. MEDIUM, \"linear work\".** Changed: §2 \"Work\" states\n`W = O(P · (d + 1) · (n + 1) · log m)` with memoized local runs (`P`\nexpanded positions, counted per reference so DAG expansion is bounded by\n`P` rather than the row count; `d` container depth; `n` cursor cells;\n`m` largest container) and `O(P · (n + 1)^(d+1) · log m)` without, and\nmakes `:work` charge one unit per position visited or spelled and one\nper literal byte copied, so every spelling byte is paid for before\nallocation; `:depth`, `:cursor-cells`, `:rows` and `:bytes` stay and\ntogether bound what a spelling can copy; `:search` and `:label-bytes`\ngo. The recommendation line now says \"work bounded and accounted\" instead\nof \"linear work\". A5 updates carrier §10.10's wording accordingly.\nUnverified: the bound is argued, not measured; the prototype does not\ncharge budgets.\n\n**7. MEDIUM, stale wording.** Changed: §0 defines canonical traversal\norder versus host enumeration order; A2 now replaces the whole tail of\nthe Validation paragraph including \"no encounter-order tie-break ever\ndecides validity\" and \"never falls back to encounter order\"; A5 replaces\n§10.10's \"never falls back to encounter order\" and answer 9's\n\"refinement-and-search\"; A8 gives three explicit replacements for\ncontinuation §5.5 (the body-label sentence with its \"step 1\" reference,\nthe \"sorting their elements' encodings (step 2)\" sentence, and a\nqualification of \"never derived from traversal order\" to *host*\ntraversal order); A4 corrects the \"two kinds\" counts in carrier §8 and\n§11 and states the `[:child]` and integer `[:n k]` changes in A1.\n\n**8. LOW.** Changed: the portable-id example holds the id counter equal\n(task B consumes one unrelated fresh name), and the paragraph concedes\nthe review's point that an id recomputed from structure is the\ncanonicalization problem again; engine citations are given against\nbranch `srvm-phase6a` (`gen-id` 227–230, `issue-ref` 311–323,\n`handle-cursor-unminted` 557–568, `gensym` 2312–2318, the `:stream/cursor`\neffect site 2595–2602, read with `git show` on the branch); D1 and the\nF1 table say `:window` is in numeric ascending register order as the\ncarrier specifies.\n\n## Pinned ids, old → new\n\n- Unchanged from the first draft and from 6a: `fab223…`, `c4a7c7…`,\n  `07eda35d…`, `da69c679…`, `49d4478c…`, `536c810b…`, ten-cell root\n  `6e7c7a6b…`.\n- Unchanged from the first draft (differ from 6a): `a3713a5e…`,\n  `4993b5b1…`.\n- First draft gave two roots depending on enumeration → one root:\n  review A `#{[X X] [Y Z]}` `65ba4530…`; review B `{X X, Y Z}` `409548a7…`.\n- First draft refused → numbered: `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` `92747c0f…`.\n- First draft left unnumbered (emitted source ids) → numbered: review C\n  `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}` `18b70de6…`.\n- New pins: `[#{X Y} X]` `7dc3d5c4…`; `[#{#{X Y} #{Z W}} X]` `6ea03f9b…`;\n  `#{[#{X Y} #{X Y}] [#{P Q} #{R S}]}` `b35b8e12…`.\n- Still refused: loop plus 2-cycle (6a's `55ff168e…` stays removed; `L`\n  now gets `cc-1` before the refusal), seven-cell.\n- Every numbering was derived by hand in note §6 and the digest taken\n  from the codec under that numbering; A, B, C and `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]`\n  agree with an independent computation (codex's, or 6a's).\n\n## Unverified or disputed\n\n- No codex counterexample is disputed; all three reproduced on the first\n  prototype and are fixed.\n- The prototype's decode check runs after 6a's frame validation\n  (item 4); the real codec must place it at step 6.\n- Three-host runs, kondo, budgets charging: not run or not implemented\n  (documents and a JVM scratch prototype only).\n- The real-state rate of D5 is unmeasured (no v3 task corpus; item 3).\n- The work bound is argued, not measured (item 6).\n- Uncommitted changes in the p6a worktree, if any, remain invisible.\n\n## Owner questions\n\nUnchanged from the first report, restated in note §9: accept the D5\nclass as documented (recommended yes); refusal kind\n`:unresolved-cursor-cells` rather than `:resource-limit` (recommended).\n","numLines":204,"startLine":1,"totalLines":204}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_014VjKxnof72jiZQyFyfKJyH","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
