{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011CfwtDF1iS24tAHqLJP2bC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"With counts in hand (prototype core ~330 nonblank lines, ~30 of them D6, diagnostics and measurement), I'll now rewrite the note covering refusal semantics, child translation, the budget-based work bound, A2, estimates, the regression analysis with the D6 repair, and a new \"Replacing 6a\" section.\n\n","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1575,"cache_read_input_tokens":202273,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1575},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7984b61f-639f-4af2-b00a-5b9b7d554f75","uuid":"2bf54d20-40f6-4d9b-aa8c-6d19cdefbbf6","timestamp":"2026-10-12T06:39:30.838Z","request_id":"req_011CfwtDEjbixq5xhEtKhBe9","narration_block_indexes":[0],"thinking_duration_ms":2,"thinking_display":"updates"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011CfwtDF1iS24tAHqLJP2bC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01JsNijEWWzFyCjoUMnYG9vn","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-numbering/docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md","content":"# Cursor-cell numbering by discovery order (companion to the row carrier)\n\nStatus: **Adopted by the owner 2026-10-12 (replaces carrier §6's\nrefinement and search); not yet implemented.** Written 2026-10-12\n(Architect, claude-fable-5-1) for the owner's observation that \"the\nproblem is not that the cursor cell has identity, but that the identity\nis local\", and the orchestrator's proposal to number cursor cells by\n**discovery order** in a canonical traversal. Revised twice the same day\nafter independent reviews (gpt-6.1-sol,\n`collab/1792140000000-…findings.md` and `collab/1792170000000-…findings.md`).\nRound 2 repaired the orbit lemma (`#{[X X] [Y Z]}`) and nested tie groups\n(`#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}`). Round 3 (this text) fixes what the second review\nfound: which local conflicts refuse (§2 D5, witness\n`[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]`), the install-child refusal escaping decode, the\nwork bound (now an explicit budget, with body labels spelled by rank so\nkey bytes no longer grow with a stream descriptor), the A2 wording, the\nsize estimate, and the coverage regression against 6a (2-cycles and\n3-cycles), for which §2 D6 specifies and §6 measures the repair. §8 lists\nwhat an implementation deletes, rewrites and keeps in 6a. Subordinate to\n[`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md)\n(the carrier, body version 3) and\n[`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md`](./yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md);\nvocabulary is UCF §7.5.3 (\"Terminology: cursor, cursor cell, cursor-ref,\ncell\") exactly. Evidence is the carrier draft §5/§6 tables, slice 6a's\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc` and `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc`\n(branch `srvm-phase6a` at `d77da9f0`), and a prototype run against that\ncode (§6). Nothing here is implemented.\n\n**In one line:** number cursor cells in the order a canonical traversal\nmeets them (D1–D2); order the elements of a guest set or map by a key\nthat is the spelling of a speculative local run over the element (so\nalias patterns such as `[X X]` versus `[Y Z]` are part of the key);\ndefer equal keys and re-examine them to a fixpoint (D3); at a fixpoint,\nlink tied elements that share an unnumbered cursor cell into components\nand enter the component of least complete encoding (D4); a component\nholding two equal-keyed elements of one container is the one case this\ncannot certify: refuse it (D5), or resolve it by a bounded residue search\nover that component alone (D6, recommended). Proof in §3, budget in §2\n\"Work\", results in §6, the cut against 6a in §8.\n\n## 0. Vocabulary used below\n\n- **Canonical traversal order** is the order D1 defines: a function of\n  the abstract task alone. **Host enumeration order** (also \"listed\n  order\") is the order in which a host happens to list the elements of a\n  guest set or the entries of a guest map, or the census; it is an\n  accident of the host. The procedure reads it in exactly one place, D4's\n  entry of components of equal encoding, where lemma C shows it cannot\n  change the result.\n- An **element** is a `:set` element or a `:map` entry `[key value]`\n  (the key is a guest value and may be a cursor cell).\n- A **numbering** `N` is a partial map from the task's cursor cells to\n  positive integers, injective, assigned in increasing order; `|N|` is\n  its size. The emitted id of a cell numbered `k` is `:yin.k/cc-k`.\n- A **run** is one execution of the traversal-and-resolution procedure\n  over one item (a row or a value) from a starting numbering. The **root\n  run** is the run over the `:task` row from the empty numbering; its\n  numbers are the output. A **local run** is a run over one item from\n  the enclosing run's current numbering. There are three kinds of local\n  run, and the distinction decides what refuses (D5):\n  - a **key run** is a local run over one element, made to compute the\n    element's sort key; it is *speculative*: it never refuses, and\n    whatever it cannot resolve (a tie, a conflict) is spelled in its\n    key as `[:tie …]`;\n  - a **component run** is a local run over a component's synthetic\n    `[:component …]` row (D4); it must be *complete*, else D5;\n  - a **trial** (D6 only) is a component run that enters one chosen\n    element before its siblings.\n- A **body label** is `[:body [:stream identity descriptor] position]`,\n  a cursor cell's census body as plain data; its **rank** is its index\n  among the census's distinct body labels in bytewise order.\n\n## 1. Findings\n\n**F1. Every non-guest position is ordered.** From the carrier §5 tables\n(and `rows/row-table`, `rows.cljc:37-57`), the positions that can hold a\ncursor-ref (`[:cursor-cell C]`) or a bare `C` slot, and their order:\n\n| Position | Order | Canonical index |\n|---|---|---|\n| `:task` slots 5 `free-env`, 6 `store`, 8 `parked`, 15 `result`; `:wait` state, pending; `:act` env, window, k; `:tail` k; `:kframe` env, window; `:reified` act; `:env` and `:closure` bindings; `:module-store` store; `:pending` `cursor-cell-C`, `response-cursor-cell-C`, `value-V`, `envelope-V` | fixed slot | slot index |\n| `:task` 7 `module-stores`, 12 `installs` | keyed by `M`, by module name | Jing bytes of the key; the child is a task boundary |\n| `:task` 13 `frames`; `:k`; `:vec`, `:list` elements | ordered vector | position `i` |\n| `:window` | keyed by register `N` | `N` ascending, numerically (carrier §5.2) |\n| `:bindings` (environments, closures, the free environment) | keyed by symbol `S` | Jing canonical bytes of `S` (§5 preamble: \"sorted\") |\n| `:store`, `:parked-table` | keyed by `D`, by `P` | bytes of the key; a parked-table value is a structural `:act`/`:tail` reference, entered like any row |\n| `:set` elements | **unordered** | none |\n| `:map` entries | **unordered** (keys are `V`) | none |\n| `:cursor-cells` census | not a position (carrier §6 step 2, §8 step 3) | — |\n\nSo the only unordered positions are guest `:set` elements and guest\n`:map` entries. Both reviews confirmed the inventory complete. The\nexample state in `collab/example-suspended-state.clj` holds its one\ncursor cell (`:cursor-1`) in three ordered positions: the act's\nenvironment under `c`, the window under register 2, and the `:next`\npending's `cursor-cell-C` slot.\n\n**F2. Discovery order subsumes the positions step.** The carrier's §6\nstep 3 (positions) exists to let a cursor cell's number depend on where\nit occurs. A canonical traversal (D1) visits every ordered position in a\nfixed order, so \"the n-th cursor cell met\" already encodes the first\nposition of each cursor cell; later positions cannot change an assigned\nnumber and need not be collected. Refinement (step 4) and search (step 5)\nexisted only to turn position multisets into a total order; discovery\norder is a total order by construction.\n\n**F3. Unordered containers are ordered by a content encoding that\ncarries the alias pattern.** The first draft spelled an unnumbered cursor\ncell by its body alone, so `[X X]` and `[Y Z]` encoded alike. The\nrepaired encoding (§2 \"Spelling\") numbers the cursor cells *inside* an\nelement provisionally, in the element's own discovery order, relative to\nthe enclosing numbering: `[X X]` spells `[(u0) (u0)]`, `[Y Z]` spells\n`[(u0) (u1)]`, so alias topology inside an element is part of its sort\nkey, as a de Bruijn index is part of a term. The key of an element is the\nspelling produced by a *key run* over it, which applies the same rules\nrecursively (nested containers sorted, nested ties deferred and\nre-examined, D4 inside) and spells what it cannot resolve as `[:tie …]`.\nEqual keys form a **tie group**, deferred to the run's worklist and\nre-examined to a fixpoint (D3) as other positions assign numbers. A\nprovisional cell is spelled with its body *rank*, not its body label, so\na key's size depends on the element's structure and not on the length\nof a stream descriptor (§2 \"Work\").\n\n**F4. What refuses, and what is lost against 6a.** What survives a run's\nfixpoint is a worklist of tie groups whose unnumbered cursor cells occur\nonly inside tied elements. Link two tied elements when they share an\nunnumbered cursor cell; a connected set of tied elements is a\n**component**. Two cases:\n- *Certified*: no component holds two elements of one group. Every\n  component is then encoded by a component run over its elements in\n  group order (so a component spanning two groups, `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]`,\n  spells its shared cell once with one index), components are sorted by\n  that encoding, and the least run of equal encodings is entered in host\n  order. Equal encodings here mean *isomorphic* components (§3, lemma C).\n  `#{X Y}`, `#{[X X] [Y Y]}`, `{X 1, Y 1}`, `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}` and\n  `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` are this case.\n- *Conflict*: some component holds two elements of one tie group. Then\n  entering either element first is a choice D1–D4 cannot show to be\n  harmless. **Which runs refuse.** Only the *root run* and *component\n  runs* refuse on a conflict (D5). A *key run* never refuses: it is a\n  speculation made to sort, its unresolved residue is spelled `[:tie …]`\n  in the key, and the enclosing run may resolve the same elements later.\n  The second review's witness is exactly this: in `[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]`\n  the key run over the inner set reaches a conflict (`[A B]` and `[B A]`\n  tie and share both cells), the outer set has one element and is\n  entered, the inner tie is deferred to the root worklist, index 1 meets\n  `A = cc-1`, and the root fixpoint separates `[A B]` (`[(n 1) (u0)]`)\n  from `[B A]` (`[(u0) (n 1)]`), so `B = cc-2`; the lift numbers and the\n  receiver recomputes the same root (`9600fe01…`, the review's digest).\n  The earlier text's \"at any fixpoint of any run\" was wrong and is\n  replaced in D5, lemma D and A1.\n  The predicate, in one sentence: *a state is refused under D5 iff the\n  root run, or a component run it requires, reaches a fixpoint at which\n  two elements of one guest set or map have equal keys and are connected\n  by a chain of shared unnumbered cursor cells through tied elements.*\n\n  **Exactly which structures that is.** A D5 state needs two or more\n  cursor cells with *equal bodies* (same stream identity and descriptor,\n  same position) that are held *only* through guest sets or maps, in\n  elements that each mention more than one of them, with equal keys. The\n  smallest are the directed 2-cycle `#{[A B] [B A]}` and the directed\n  3-cycle `#{[A B] [B C] [C A]}` (both interchangeable: the swap and the\n  rotation are automorphisms, so any entering order gives the same rows,\n  and 6a numbers them); the path `#{[A B] [B C]}` and the seven-cell\n  triangle plus square (`rows_test.cljc:392-404`) are refused and\n  genuinely need a tie-break (§6: two and four distinct roots under\n  host-order entry); the loop plus 2-cycle `#{[L L] [A B] [B A]}`\n  (`rows_test.cljc:312-329`) numbers `L = cc-1` by its key and conflicts\n  on `{A, B}`. One register, environment binding, pending slot or vector\n  position holding any one of the cells breaks the tie for the whole\n  component (`[#{X Y} X]`, the witness above). **Are they common in\n  realistic suspended tasks?** Unmeasured: the repository has no\n  version-3 task corpus (the Stage E scenarios and the v2 handoff census\n  tests hold cursor cells but no v3 lift exists before 6b). The structural\n  argument is that a cursor cell is created into a register\n  (`:stream/cursor` delivers to `rd`, engine `:2595-2602`) and reaches a\n  guest set only by guest code that builds a set of tuples of cursors on\n  one stream at one position and then drops every other reference; of\n  the repository's own fixtures only the two built for the purpose hit\n  D5, and under a tie-biased generator the rate is 35 of 2300 (§6).\n  Because the rate is unmeasured and 6a numbers the 2-cycle and 3-cycle,\n  this note does not leave the regression as a documented limitation:\n  **D6** (§2) is the minimum extra rule that repairs it, a search over\n  the conflicting component only (two trials for a 2-cycle, three for a\n  3-cycle, eleven for the seven-cell state), prototyped and measured in\n  §6: with D6 every one of the 2300 generated states numbers, the 2-cycle\n  and 3-cycle reach 6a's exact roots, and no D1–D4 numbering changes.\n  The owner's choice between D5 (refuse, no search anywhere) and D6\n  (bounded residue search) is §10 question 1; this note recommends D6.\n\n**F5. The scheme is canonical.** Proved in §3: the output (rows or\nrefusal) is a function of the abstract task up to isomorphism (renaming\nof cursor cells, permutation of every unordered container and of the\ncensus) and terminating; the work is accounted by the budget of §2\n\"Work\".\n\n**F6. Prototype evidence (§6).** 2000 random value states (one to six\ncursor cells over two bodies, so ties are common; nested vectors, sets,\nmaps with cursor-cell keys, sets of cursor-cell pairs including aliases\nsuch as `[X X]`, sets of sets, cells aliased across containers), 200\nframe-bearing states (act window, K-frame window, environments, a parked\nstate) and 100 states with install children, each compared with itself\nrenamed and shuffled and decoded through 6a's decoder: 0 invariance\nfailures and 0 decode failures in either mode; D5 refuses 35 of 2300, D6\nrefuses none; the largest `:work` charged is 1470 of 6a's default\n200000 and the largest trial count 13 of 10000. Every pin of rounds 1\nand 2 is unchanged (including under the rank spelling and with D6 on);\nthe second review's digest for the witness is reproduced; the key bytes\nof one state under a 65536-character descriptor fell from 920118 (label\nspelling) to 1396 (rank spelling), the same as under a 128-character\ndescriptor.\n\n**F7. Portable ids minted in the VM are the wrong fix.** §5.\n\n## 2. The rule (replaces carrier §6 \"The procedure\")\n\nInput: the abstract task of carrier §6 (the §5 structure with cursor\ncells as identities, before numbering). Output: a bijection from the\ntask's reached cursor cells to `:yin.k/cc-1 … :yin.k/cc-n`, or a\nrefusal. Install children are numbered as their own tasks by the same\nrule (task boundary, unchanged), in any order, since scopes are disjoint.\n\n**Spelling (the content encoding `CE`).** A run spells the item it has\ntraversed under its final numbering. The root run spells a cursor cell\nnumbered `k` as `[:cursor-cell [:n k]]`, `k` an exact integer; a local\nrun spells a cursor cell it numbered itself (index `j`, counted from the\nenclosing run's local counter) as `[:cursor-cell [:u j r]]`, `r` the\ncell's body rank (an exact integer; rank order equals body-label order,\nso no comparison and no numbering changes against spelling the label,\nand the key's size no longer depends on the descriptor), and a cursor\ncell numbered by an enclosing run as that run spelled it. Nothing else\nchanges from carrier §6 step 2: `[:lit d]` as itself, `[:row B]`\nexpanded in place, structural data as itself, the census as\n`[:cursor-cells]`, an install child as `[:child]` (replacing step 2's\n`[:child <child root id>]`: a child sits only under a name-keyed install\nentry, never inside an unordered container, so its content never decides\nan order, and the encoding stays hash-free). Keyed structural rows are\nspelled in their canonical key order, `:window` in numeric register\norder; a `:set` or `:map` is spelled as the bytewise sorted vector of its\nelements' spellings (an entry as `[:entry K V]`), duplicates retained,\nwhere a tie group the run could not resolve is spelled in place as\n`[:tie K … K]` (its common key, once per element). All comparisons are\nbytewise on canonical CBOR of these synthetic vectors. `CE` is hash-free.\n\n**D1. Traversal.** Depth-first, pre-order, from the run's root item. A\nrow's slots are visited in table order. Ordered repeated slots\n(`frames`, `:vec`, `:list`, `:k`) in order. Keyed rows with structural\nkeys (`:bindings`, `:store`, `:parked-table`, the root's `installs` and\n`module-stores`) in the Jing canonical byte order of the key, the order\n§5 emits; `:window` in ascending numeric register order as the carrier\nspecifies. The census is not entered; an install child's `:task` row is\nnot entered. A `:set`'s elements and a `:map`'s entries are each given a\n**key**: the spelling of a key run over the element under the numbering\nat the moment the container is entered (every element keyed from that\none numbering). Elements are then taken in bytewise key order: a run of\nequal keys is a **tie group**, appended to the run's worklist (deferral\norder is canonical: containers in traversal order, groups within one\ncontainer in key order); every other element is entered in key order,\nan entry's key before its value.\n\n**D2. Meeting.** When the traversal reaches an identity position (a\n`[:cursor-cell C]` value or a `C` slot of a `:pending` row) whose cursor\ncell has no number, the cursor cell receives `|N| + 1` (the root run\ncounts from 1; a local run counts its own cells from the enclosing run's\nlocal counter, so a nested local run never reuses an index of its\nenclosing run). An already numbered cursor cell is passed over. There is\nno first-occurrence spelling: every occurrence emits\n`[:cursor-cell :yin.k/cc-n]` and the census row (carrier §3) stays the\ncontents table.\n\n**D3. Fixpoint.** After the traversal, the worklist's groups are\nre-examined in deferral order: each group's elements are re-keyed under\nthe current numbering and taken as in D1 (singletons entered, equal runs\nre-deferred in order). Repeat while a round numbers at least one cursor\ncell and groups remain. Entering an element inside a group runs D1 on\nit, so a group discovered *inside* an entered element (`#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}`)\njoins the same worklist and is re-examined in the next round; there is\none worklist per run, and the root run's worklist collects deferred\ngroups from every depth.\n\n**D4. Components.** When a fixpoint is reached with groups remaining,\nlink two tied elements (of any groups) that share an unnumbered cursor\ncell; a connected set of tied elements is a **component**. (At a\nfixpoint every unnumbered cursor cell occurs only inside tied elements,\nso all of its occurrences lie in one component.) Encode each component\nby a component run over the synthetic ordered row\n`[:component g₁ i₁ g₂ i₂ …]` in group order, `gᵢ` the group's deferral\nsequence number (a datum) and `iᵢ` the component's one element of that\ngroup, or, when the component holds several elements of group `gᵢ`, a\nsynthetic `:set` of them (the D5/D6 case). A component run must be\ncomplete (no `[:tie]` in its spelling); it is incomplete only when it\nreaches a conflict of its own, which D5 or D6 decides. Sort the\ncomponents by their encodings and **enter the run of least encoding**:\neach of its components has its component run's cursor cells numbered,\nin that run's local-index order, continuing the enclosing run's\nnumbering; components of equal encoding are entered in host order (§3,\nlemma C: equal encodings are isomorphic and the result is the same).\nRemove the entered elements from their groups and return to D3. A group\nwhose elements mention no unnumbered cursor cell holds equal values (the\ncarrier refuses the duplicate at §8 step 5); its elements are entered in\nhost order, which numbers nothing and emits identical rows.\n\n**D5. Conflict, and refusal without D6.** A component **conflicts** when\nit holds two elements of one group. Without D6, a conflict reached by the\nroot run, or by a component run (directly, or through a nested component\nrun of its own), refuses the lift: `:yin.k/non-portable` kind\n`:unresolved-cursor-cells`, `:yin.k/path` the row path of the container\nholding the least conflicting group (for an install child, prefixed\n`[12 <install name> …]`), and `:yin.k/cursor-cells` the sorted unnumbered\ncursor cells of every conflicting component at that fixpoint (so the\ndiagnostic, like the verdict, does not depend on host order). A key run\nnever refuses (§0; F4). A receiver that reaches D5 while recomputing\nrefuses `:yin.k/undecodable` kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`. Neither is\na resource refusal and neither is `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`.\n\n**D6. Residue search (recommended; the repair of F4's regression).** A\nconflict is resolved instead of refused: let `g` be the least conflicting\ngroup of the component and `E` the component's elements of `g`. For each\n`e ∈ E` run a **trial**: a component run over the same `[:component …]`\nrow in which `e` is entered first when its container is keyed, before\nits siblings are keyed (so `e`'s cursor cells take the first local\nindices and its siblings are keyed against them), and which runs to\ncompletion by D1–D6. Each trial charges `:search` one unit. The\ncomponent's encoding is the least trial spelling, and entering the\ncomponent adopts that trial's numbering (D4). Equal least spellings are\nisomorphic and either may be adopted (§3, lemma E). With D6 no\nstructural refusal remains: the only refusals of the procedure are the\nresource refusals of \"Work\" below, and `:unresolved-cursor-cells` is not\ndefined. With D6 in force, 6a's `:search` budget key stays and counts\ntrials; without it, the key is deleted.\n\n**Totality.** The root run ends with an empty worklist or a refusal.\nBefore emission the implementation asserts that the numbering is a\nbijection from the census's cursor cells onto `1 … n`; a violation is a\ndefect (an exception naming the missing or extra cursor cells), never a\nrefusal, and never a partially numbered carrier.\n\n**Memoization (implementation note, not part of the definition).** A\nlocal run's result is a function of its item, of the enclosing numbering\nrestricted to the item's cursor cells, of the enclosing local counter and\n(D6) of the forced element; an implementation may memoize on that key and\nmust return the numbering restricted to the item's own cursor cells (the\nprototype's one bug during round 2 was returning the whole numbering of\nanother element's context, which the decode round trip caught). The memo\nholds only spellings that were paid for under `:work`, so it adds no\nunaccounted bytes. No bound on the number of distinct memo contexts is\nclaimed; the memo is an optimization and the budget is the bound.\n\n**Validation and decode.** Unchanged in form: the receiver decodes, runs\nthe root run, re-emits and compares root ids (`:noncanonical-cursor-cells`\non mismatch). In 6a's `decode*` the recomputation is `emit` itself\n(`rows.cljc:570-575`), which numbers the root *and every install child*\n(`emit-node`, `:185-189`); the **whole** of that call is wrapped so that\nonly a lift-side `:yin.k/non-portable` of kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`\n(D5, from the root or from a child) is translated to `:yin.k/undecodable`\nkind `:unresolved-cursor-cells` with `:yin.k/path` prefixed by the\ncarried root id (a child's path then reads `[<root id> 12 <name> …]`);\na resource refusal (`:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit`) propagates as\nitself; no other non-portable kind can arise there, because step 5 has\nalready checked literals and markers. The second review's escape (an\ninstall child's D5 leaving `decode*` as `:yin.k/non-portable` with path\n`[:child 15]`) came from translating only the root's numbering; the\nprototype now wraps the whole recomputation and the case is in §6. The\nvalidator order stays: step 6 (numbering) after step 5 and before\n`validate-frames` (`rows.cljc:576-578`; test\n`numbering-precedes-frame-defect`, `rows_test.cljc:206`). With D6 the\ntranslation has nothing to translate and is omitted.\n\n**Work.** No polynomial bound is claimed. The second review showed the\nround-2 bound counted positions but not bytes (a 65536-character stream\ndescriptor multiplied key bytes by 170 at fixed `P`, `d`, `n`, `m`) and\nthat memoization's benefit across differing local contexts is unproved.\nThe procedure is instead *accounted*, as 6a already accounts refinement:\nthe `:work` budget is charged one unit per position visited (D1, D3,\nevery local run) and one unit per position spelled plus one unit per\nbyte of literal or structural datum copied into a spelling, before the\ncopy; `:search` (D6) is charged one unit per trial; `:depth` bounds the\nstructural depth as today, `:cursor-cells` bounds `n`, `:rows` the\ncarried rows and `:bytes` the carried bytes. Exceeding any budget is the\nresource refusal of carrier §10.10 (`:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit\n[<key>]`), never a canonicality verdict, exactly as 6a's `:label-bytes`\nwas. The budget charges every allocated spelling byte before allocation,\nso the bytes materialized at any time are bounded by `:work` plus the\ncarried bytes. What the rank spelling guarantees is narrower and exact:\na key's bytes are a function of the element's expanded structure and of\nthe number of distinct bodies, not of the bodies' sizes (§6: 1396 key\nbytes at both 128 and 65536 descriptor characters). The crude bound\nwithout memoization is `O(P · (n + 1)^(d + 1) · log m)` positions and\n`O(B)` bytes per spelling (`P` expanded positions counted per reference,\n`B` expanded bytes, `d` the unordered-container nesting depth, `n` cursor\ncells, `m` the largest container), which the budget enforces rather than\nthe analysis. Measured: every fixture of §6 charges under 1000 `:work`\nunits, the 2300 generated states at most 1470, against 6a's default\n200000; the seven-cell state charges 488 and 11 trials under D6. The\n`:label-bytes` budget disappears with the growing labels.\n\n## 3. Proof of canonicality\n\nCall two configurations (abstract task, partial numbering, worklist)\n**isomorphic** when a bijection `π` of cursor cells carries one to the\nother: the structures agree up to renaming by `π` and up to the host\norder of every unordered container and of the census, numbers agree\n(`N'(π(c)) = N(c)`), and worklists correspond group by group in order.\nThe proof is a simultaneous induction over runs, ordered by (number of\nunnumbered cursor cells of the run's item, nesting depth of the item):\na key run, a component run or a trial is over an item with no more\nunnumbered cells than its enclosing run and strictly fewer nested\nunordered containers, or (a trial) strictly fewer unnumbered cells; so\nthe lemmas below may assume themselves for every local run a run spawns.\n\n**Lemma A (keys are canonical).** The key of an element is the spelling\nof a key run over it, and a key run reads only structure, body ranks\n(the rank of a body is a function of the census up to isomorphism) and\nnumbers; its own listed-order inputs are (i) element order inside nested\ncontainers, which it never uses except through keys, by the induction,\n(ii) the host order of components of equal encoding in its D4 step,\ncovered by lemma C, and (iii) nothing else: a key run that reaches a\nconflict does not choose, it spells the residue `[:tie K … K]`, which is\na function of the group's common key and size. So isomorphic items under\ncorresponding numberings have byte-identical keys, and the sorted order\nof a container's elements is a function of the configuration up to\nisomorphism.\n\n**Lemma B (ties are exactly what keys cannot separate).** By\nconstruction: elements are deferred iff their keys are equal bytes.\n\n**Lemma C (components).** At a fixpoint let `C₁ … Cₘ` be the components\nof one run of equal encodings, each encoding complete, and let\n`U₁ … Uₘ` be their unnumbered cursor cells. The `Uᵢ` are pairwise\ndisjoint and each `Uᵢ` is confined to `Cᵢ` (every occurrence of an\nunnumbered cell lies in a tied element, hence in its own component).\nEqual, complete encodings of `[:component g₁ i₁ …]` rows are a faithful\nspelling (literals as themselves, rows expanded, every cursor cell by\nindex and body rank, groups by sequence number, a group's several\nelements as a sorted set), so they exhibit, for each pair `Cᵢ, Cⱼ`, a\nbijection `Uᵢ → Uⱼ` under which the components are the same structure\ngroup by group. Entering the run in host order `σ` gives the cells of\n`C_{σ(t)}` the numbers `b + (t−1)·|U| + 1 …` by local index; in order\n`σ'` the cells of `C_{σ'(t)}` receive the same numbers. The block-swap\nbijection composed from the pairwise bijections is an isomorphism of the\ntwo resulting configurations: numbers agree by construction, structures\nagree because the swapped components are isomorphic and nothing outside\nthem mentions their cells, and the worklists agree because the same\ngroups remain, with the same elements removed. ∎\n\n**Lemma D (the D5 verdict is invariant).** Whether the root run, or a\ncomponent run it requires, reaches a fixpoint with a conflicting\ncomponent depends only on which tied elements share unnumbered cursor\ncells at that fixpoint, which is a function of the configuration up to\nisomorphism (lemma A gives the ties, the sharing relation is structural);\nand whether a component run is complete is the same question one level\ndown, by the induction. Key runs do not enter the verdict: their\nconflicts are spelled, not refused, and the spelling is canonical (lemma\nA iii).\n\n**Lemma E (D6 trials).** At a conflicting component the set `E` of\nelements of its least conflicting group is a function of the\nconfiguration up to isomorphism (lemma D names the component and its\ngroups canonically). Each trial is a deterministic function of (the\nconfiguration up to isomorphism, the chosen element), by the induction\non the trial run, which has strictly fewer unnumbered cells than the\nenclosing run (its forced element holds at least one, being linked into\nthe component by a shared unnumbered cell). So the multiset of trial\nspellings is canonical and its least member is canonical. Two trials\nwith equal least spellings have produced isomorphic numberings of the\ncomponent (faithful spelling, as in lemma C), so adopting either yields\nisomorphic configurations. ∎\n\n**Theorem.** The procedure's output on isomorphic configurations is the\nsame: both refuse at corresponding points, or both emit byte-identical\nrows. *Proof.* By the induction above. D1 and D2 decisions are functions\nof the configuration up to isomorphism (lemma A); D3 only repeats D1 on\ndeferred groups in a canonical order; D4's choice of the least run is\ncanonical (lemma A on component encodings, which are complete by D5/D6)\nand its only listed-order input is harmless (lemma C); D5 is invariant\n(lemma D); D6 is invariant (lemma E). Emission spells cursor cells by\nnumber only and sorts every emitted unordered slot by canonical bytes\nunder the final numbering (carrier §5), so isomorphic final\nconfigurations emit the same rows. ∎\n\nConsequences:\n\n1. *Function of structure, renaming-invariant, enumeration-invariant*:\n   the theorem with `π` a renaming, a permutation of the containers, or\n   both. No decision reads a source id or a host order except at the\n   point lemma C covers.\n2. *Terminating.* The structural edge family is acyclic (carrier §8 step\n   3), so every traversal is finite. In any run each D3 round that is not\n   the last numbers a cursor cell; each D4 step numbers at least one\n   cursor cell or removes a duplicate group; each local run is over an\n   item with fewer unordered containers nested below it than its\n   spawning run's item, or (a trial) with fewer unnumbered cells, so the\n   induction measure of §3 decreases and there are finitely many local\n   runs. The budget of §2 \"Work\" is what bounds the count.\n3. *Deterministic*, hence two lifts of one state are byte-identical and\n   the receiver's recomputation is well defined.\n4. *Sharing survives*: the numbering is a bijection on reached cursor\n   cells (D2 never renumbers; totality is asserted), every occurrence of\n   one cursor cell spells one number, and two cursor cells at one\n   position get two numbers.\n5. *Cross-engine law*: two engines lifting one canonical frame produce\n   abstract tasks equal up to renaming (the frame is engine-independent,\n   continuation §2.1) and therefore, by the theorem, equal rows.\n6. *D6 moves no D1–D4 id.* Trials run only where D4 found a conflict,\n   which without D6 is a refusal; every numbering D1–D4 assign is\n   identical with and without D6 (§6 checks the pins both ways).\n\n## 4. Amendments\n\n**A1 — carrier §6, from \"The procedure.\" to the end of step 5.** Replace\nwith §2 above (vocabulary, the spelling, D1–D6, totality, work). Keep the\nsection's first paragraph (the counterexample) and amend \"What is\ndefined, and what is not claimed\" to: \"The canonical numbering is defined\nas the output of the discovery-order procedure below, which is a function\nof the state up to renaming of cursor cells and host enumeration of guest\nsets, maps and the census (proof in\n`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md` §3). It is not the\nminimum of the root bytes over all bijections. The one choice the\ntraversal cannot make by itself, between two equal-keyed elements of one\nguest set or map that are linked by shared unnumbered cursor cells at a\nfixpoint of the root run or of a component run, is resolved by a search\nconfined to that component (D6) [or, in the D5 variant: is refused (D5),\nnot searched; the refusal certifies nothing about interchangeability and\ncan be narrowed later without changing any numbering assigned today].\"\nState in step 2's replacement that `[:n k]` and `[:u j r]` carry exact\nCBOR integers, that `r` is the body rank, and that `[:child]` replaces\n`[:child <child root id>]` (reason in §2 \"Spelling\").\n\n**A2 — carrier §6 \"Validation\".** Replace the paragraph's last two\nsentences, from \"Every labeling that yields the selected commitment is\nthereby accepted, and no encounter-order tie-break ever decides validity\"\nto the end, with: \"Every labeling that yields the selected commitment is\nthereby accepted. The emitted commitment and the acceptance decision are\ninvariant under the host's enumeration order of every set, map and\ncensus (proof in the numbering note, §3); the procedure's one use of host\norder, entering components of equal encoding in the order listed, cannot\nchange the rows (lemma C), and a receiver never decides validity by its\nown enumeration order. This is a lower-side structural refusal, not a\nlift kind. A receiver whose work bound (§10.10) is exceeded before the\nprocedure completes refuses with the structured resource refusal before\nany attachment, never labels the state `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`, and\nnever accepts an unfinished check [D5 variant: ; a receiver that reaches\nD5 refuses `:yin.k/undecodable` kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`].\"\n\n**A3 — carrier §5 preamble.** Replace \"The class-erased skeletons of §6\nare used only inside the numbering procedure and never decide the order\nof an emitted row\" with \"The spellings (content encodings) of §6 are\nused only inside the numbering procedure and never decide the order of\nan emitted row\".\n\n**A4 — carrier §8 step 6.** \"Recompute §6 under the work bound, for the\nroot and every install child, and compare (`:yin.k/undecodable`, kind\n`:noncanonical-cursor-cells`; or the resource refusal of §10.10 if the\nbound is exceeded, which propagates untranslated).\" D5 variant: add\n\"`:yin.k/undecodable` kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells` if D5 is reached\nin the root or in a child, translated from the recomputation's lift-side\nrefusal of that kind only, its path prefixed by the carried root id\";\nand in the diagnostics paragraph after step 12 add\n`:unresolved-cursor-cells` to the `:yin.k/undecodable` kinds, change\n\"the two kinds this carrier adds to UCF §7.5.4's set: `:byte-string` and\n`:metadata`\" to \"the three kinds …, `:metadata` and\n`:unresolved-cursor-cells`\", and in §11 \"two lift kinds and two\n`:yin.k/undecodable` kinds are added\" to \"three … and three …\". With D6\nthese counts are unchanged.\n\n**A5 — carrier §10.** Answer 9: \"a renaming-invariant\nrefinement-and-search procedure\" → \"the discovery-order procedure, which\nis invariant under renaming and host enumeration\". Answer 10: \"a bound\non decoded bytes, rows, cursor cells, traversal depth, refinement work\nand search nodes for §6\" → \"a bound on decoded bytes, rows, cursor cells,\ntraversal depth, traversal-and-spelling work and (D6) residue-search\ntrials for §6 (its `:work` unit counts positions and copied bytes, so\nspellings are paid for before they are allocated)\"; and \"never falls\nback to encounter order\" → \"never substitutes host enumeration order for\nthe canonical traversal\". Proposals list: \"as defined by its procedure\"\n→ \"as defined by discovery order [D5 variant: , and the D5 refusal\nclass]\".\n\n**A6 — carrier §3, identity rows.** After \"minted by the lift under §6's\ncanonical numbering\" add \"(the n-th cursor cell met in §6's canonical\ntraversal)\".\n\n**A7 — continuation §2.2.** Replace \"numbered by the draft's §6\nprocedure\" with \"numbered by discovery order (draft §6 as amended by\n`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md`)\".\n\n**A8 — continuation §5.5.** Three replacements. (i) \"Draft §6 is\ninvariant under renaming of the **cursor cells** (its labels never encode\nan incoming cursor-cell id), and the only other identity it consumes is\nthe stream marker in each cursor cell's initial label\n(`[:body <stream-marker-bytes> <position-bytes>]`, draft §6 step 1) and\nin cursor-cell bodies\" → \"Draft §6 is invariant under renaming of the\n**cursor cells** (it reads no incoming cursor-cell id), and the only\nother identity it consumes is the stream marker in each cursor cell's\nbody label (`[:body [:stream identity descriptor] position]`, draft §6\nas amended, spelled by rank inside keys) and in cursor-cell bodies\".\n(ii) \"Unordered containers (`:set`, `:map`, `:store`, `:bindings`, the\nparked table) are encoded by sorting their elements' encodings bytewise\n(draft §6 step 2), never by host traversal order\" → \"Guest sets and maps\nare entered in the bytewise order of their elements' spellings, and\n`:store`, `:bindings` and the parked table in the byte order of their\nstructural keys (draft §6 D1), never in host enumeration order\". (iii)\nLeave \"never derived from traversal order\" but qualify it: \"never\nderived from *host* traversal order (the canonical traversal of draft §6\nD1 is a function of the state and is what the procedure reads)\".\n\n**A9 — continuation §8.1 acceptance.** Item 10 stands; append items 12–22\nof §9 below as the acceptance of the rewritten numbering.\n\n**A10 — UCF §7.5.3.** No change: \"minted by the emitter (stable within\none lift; remapped on lowering) and not content-addressed\" remains true.\n\n## 5. The alternative: portable ids minted in the VM\n\nThe owner asked whether a cursor cell could carry a portable id from\ncreation so the carrier sends it as is. To work across engines the id\nmust be computable by every engine from shared inputs. The candidates\n(branch `srvm-phase6a`, `src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc`) are: the task's id\ncounter (`gen-id`, `:227-230`; `gensym`, `:2312-2318`, which lower adopts\nas `max(local, carried)`), the creation sequence of `:stream/cursor`\nexecutions (`handle-cursor-unminted`'s `seq-n`, `:557-568`, minted at the\n`:stream/cursor` effect, `:2595-2602`, with the id from `gensym` and the\nsealed reference from `issue-ref`, `:311-323`), a hash of (occurrence,\ncounter), or a random id. Every one of them names *history*, not\nstructure:\n\n- *Same structure, same hash fails.* Hold every carried field equal:\n  task A executes `:stream/cursor` three times (ids `cursor-0`,\n  `cursor-1`, `cursor-2`; counter 3) and drops the second; task B executes\n  `:stream/cursor` twice and then consumes one unrelated fresh name\n  (`gensym` for a non-cursor id; counter 3) and keeps both. The two\n  snapshots are structurally equal (two cursor cells with equal bodies at\n  equal positions, the same id counter, the same everything else), yet A\n  carries `{cursor-0, cursor-2}` and B `{cursor-0, cursor-1}`, so the root\n  ids differ. Any id recomputed from the snapshot's *structure* instead\n  is the canonicalization problem of §2 again, not an alternative to it.\n- *The cross-engine harness law* would hold only if both engines agree\n  on the minting history, which makes an execution trace part of the\n  frame's identity; a random or occurrence-bound id breaks it outright\n  (a fork halt has no occurrence; a child has none of its own).\n- *The VM grows a second name.* Lower mints a fresh private resource\n  entry per cursor cell (carrier §8 step 12) and must keep the carried\n  id beside it; a relift after further execution must mint ids that\n  cannot collide with carried ones, which needs an (origin, counter)\n  pair: a global naming scheme for a thing that only needs a name\n  relative to its state.\n\nDiscovery order answers the owner's observation differently: the\nidentity stays local and the carrier gives it a *structural* name, the\nn-th cursor cell met, exactly as a de Bruijn index names a binder by\nposition rather than by a global symbol. Recommend against portable\nids for immutable creation-history ids carried unchanged; that is the\nonly kind proposed.\n\n## 6. Prototype\n\n`collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj` (not repo code; a\nscratch project under `collab/`, never committed) implements D1–D6 as\none schema-driven run procedure (root run, key runs, component runs and\ntrials share the code; a local run differs only in its number tag, in\nspelling residues as `[:tie]` and, for a trial, in its forced element),\nmemoized as §2 describes, charging `:work` and `:search` as §2 \"Work\"\ndescribes, with the totality assertion, an emitter that numbers install\nchildren by the same procedure, a decode path that runs 6a's `decode*`\nwith its numbering check off and then wraps the whole discovery\nrecomputation (root and children) in the narrow translation, and two\nswitches: `:residue-search` (D6; off is the D5 variant) and\n`:permissive` (diagnostic: a conflict is resolved by one host-first\ntrial, to test whether a refused state is in fact interchangeable). Run\nfrom the probe directory (JVM only; `@#'rows/…` reaches 6a's private\nhelpers); the `clojure` launcher was not available to this session, so\nthe run below used the launcher's cached classpath directly:\n\n```\njava @java.args clojure.main -e \"(require 'probe.discovery) (probe.discovery/-main)\"\n```\n\nResults of 2026-10-12, round 3 (full log `collab/numbering-probe/run.log`;\n6a's own tests pass unchanged in the same project):\n\n| State | Discovery order (D5 variant) | With D6 | 6a |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `fixture :s1 :s2 :x :y` (`rows_test.cljc:28`) | `x cc-1, y cc-2`, root `fab223…` | same | same root |\n| review counterexample `'a #{X Y}`, `'b X` | `x cc-1, y cc-2`, root `a3713a5e…` | same | root `2d9873a8…` |\n| symmetric pair `#{X Y}` | component rule, root `07eda35d…` | same | same root |\n| `#{[X X] [Y Y]}` / `{X 1, Y 1}` | roots `da69c679…` / `49d4478c…`; equal after reversal and after renaming plus shuffling | same | same roots |\n| `[x x y]` / `[x y y]` (`rows_test.cljc:159`) | roots `4993b5b1…` / `536c810b…` | same | `71543fe2…` / same |\n| **review A** `#{[X X] [Y Z]}` | `X cc-1, Y cc-2, Z cc-3` for both enumerations, root `65ba4530…` (the review's digest) | same | `387f294a…` |\n| **review B** `{X X, Y Z}` | `X cc-1, Y cc-2, Z cc-3` for both enumerations, root `409548a7…` (the review's digest) | same | `be62ba43…` |\n| **review C** `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}` | total numbering, inner set listed first gets `cc-1, cc-2`; root `18b70de6…` for the state and for its renaming | same | same root |\n| `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` | numbered (two components, each one element per group), root `92747c0f…` | same | same root |\n| `[#{X Y} X]` | `X cc-1, Y cc-2`, root `7dc3d5c4…` | same | `a850fce6…` |\n| `[#{#{X Y} #{Z W}} X]` | `X cc-1, Y cc-2, Z cc-3, W cc-4`, root `6ea03f9b…` | same | `e61d3709…` |\n| `#{[#{X Y} #{X Y}] [#{P Q} #{R S}]}` | keys differ (two cursor cells against four); root `b35b8e12…` | same | refused `[:label-bytes]` |\n| **review r2 witness** `[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]` | key run conflicts, root run numbers `A cc-1, B cc-2`; root `9600fe01…` (the review's digest); 1 root over 24 renamings plus shuffles; decodes | same | `01933121…` |\n| loop plus 2-cycle (`rows_test.cljc:312`) | `L cc-1`, then refused `:unresolved-cursor-cells`, path `[15]`, cells `[A B]` | `L cc-1, A cc-2, B cc-3`, 2 trials, root `858a891e…`, 1 root over 24 | root `55ff168e…` (`L cc-3`) |\n| 2-cycle `#{[A B] [B A]}` | refused; permissive mode gives 1 root over 24 shuffles (interchangeable, not certified) | `A cc-1, B cc-2`, 2 trials, root `0fe898cc…` **= 6a's root** | numbered |\n| 3-cycle `#{[A B] [B C] [C A]}` | refused; permissive 1 root | 3 trials, root `0b181cd4…` **= 6a's root** | numbered |\n| path `#{[A B] [B C]}` | refused; permissive mode gives 2 roots (not interchangeable) | 2 trials, root `726a9fbb…`, 1 root over 24 | `76117652…` |\n| seven-cell triangle plus square (`:392`) | refused; permissive 1 root over 24 (the 4 of round 2 came from listed-order entry) | 11 trials, 488 `:work`, root `d76d787a…`, 1 root over 24; decode through 6a's `decode*` stops at 6a's own `[:label-bytes]` (probe limitation) | refused `[:label-bytes]` |\n| ten cells in a vector (`:331`) | `cc-1 … cc-10` in vector order | same | same root |\n| frame fixture: wait window `x`, K-frame window `y`, environment `y`, parked K-frame window `#{x y}` | `y cc-1` (free environment, slot 5), `x cc-2` (parked table, slot 8), `:test/cursor cc-3`; root `0b9ef61e…`; decodes | same | `fd3513dd…` |\n| frame fixture: wait window `#{x y}`, parked K-frame window `y` | `y cc-1`, `x cc-2`, `:test/cursor cc-3`; root `ff124bfc…`; decodes | same | `e1558410…` |\n| parent with two installs sharing the review-counterexample child | child `X cc-1, Y cc-2`; one child row shared; root `9544fcd7…`; decodes | same | — |\n| **install child holding the 2-cycle** | lift refuses path `[12 a 15]`; a carrier of it built with `emit-numbered` decodes to `:yin.k/undecodable :unresolved-cursor-cells`, path `[<root> 12 a 15]`, cells `[cc-1 cc-2]` | numbers, root `734c33f3…`, decodes | — |\n| budgets | loop plus 2-cycle under `{:work 1}`: `:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit [:work]`; a valid carrier decoded under `{:work 1}` propagates the same | 3-cycle under `{:search 1}`: `:resource-limit [:search]` | — |\n| key bytes, 4 cells over 2 bodies in `#{#{X Y} #{Z W} #{X Z}}` | label spelling: 4364 bytes at a 128-character descriptor, 920118 at 65536; **rank spelling: 1396 at both**; 13 local runs, 78 `:work`, same root either spelling | — | — |\n| 2000 random value states | 1975 numbered, 25 refused (14 witnessed non-interchangeable by 12 shuffles, 11 not distinguished), **0 invariance failures**, 1783 decode round trips ok, 0 decode failures, 192 not decodable through 6a's own recompute budget (probe limitation); max `:work` 1254 | **2000 numbered, 0 refused**, 0 invariance failures, 1808 decoded, 0 failures, 192 at 6a's budget; max trials 13, max `:work` 1470 | — |\n| 200 frame-bearing states | 192 numbered, 8 refused (5 witnessed, 3 not distinguished), 0 failures, 175 decoded, 17 at 6a's budget; max `:work` 767 | 200 numbered, 0 refused, 0 failures, 183 decoded, max trials 5 | — |\n| 100 states with children | 98 numbered, 2 refused (not distinguished), 0 failures, 75 decoded, 23 at 6a's budget; max `:work` 916 | 100 numbered, 0 refused, 0 failures, 76 decoded, max trials 4 | — |\n\nHand derivations, verified against the codec above (the digest comes\nfrom `emit-numbered` under the hand-derived numbering; where 6a or the\nreview reached the same numbering independently the digest agrees).\nFixture `:s1 :s2 :x :y`: the store's `'payload` map is entered by the\nkeys of its entries; whichever of `'one` (`[:cursor-cell x]`), `'both`\n(`#{x y}`) or `'cursors` (`{x :x, y :y}`) sorts first, `x` is met before\n`y`, since `x` is alone under `'one` and its body (`:s1`) ranks before\n`y`'s (`:s2`) in the set and the map; so `x = cc-1`, `y = cc-2`. Review\ncounterexample: `'a` before `'b`; `#{X Y}` ties (`[u0 r]` twice) and is\ndeferred; `'b` meets `X = cc-1`; the fixpoint re-keys the set, `[:n 1]`\nagainst `[:u 0 r]`, and `Y = cc-2`. Review A: keys `[:vec [u0 r] [u0 r]]`\nand `[:vec [u0 r] [u1 r]]` differ at the second cell's index, so `[X X]`\nis entered first: `X = cc-1`, then `Y = cc-2`, `Z = cc-3`. Review B: the\nentries `[:entry [u0 r] [u0 r]]` and `[:entry [u0 r] [u1 r]]` likewise.\nReview C: both inner sets key as `[:set [u0 r] [u1 r]]` (each key run\nresolves its own `{X, Y}` tie by D4 with two one-element components),\nthe outer tie is deferred, the fixpoint changes nothing, the two\ncomponents are isomorphic, and the one listed first gets `cc-1, cc-2`:\neither order emits `#{#{1 2} #{3 4}}`. `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]`: both sets tie\nand defer; components `{X¹, X²}` and `{Y¹, Y²}` (one element per group,\nequal encodings `[:component 1 [u0 r] 2 [u0 r]]`): `X = cc-1`, `Y = cc-2`\nin host order, same rows either way. `[#{X Y} X]`: the set ties, index 1\nmeets `X = cc-1`, the fixpoint splits the set, `Y = cc-2`. **Witness**\n`[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]`: F4 above; the key run's residue `[:set [:tie K\nK]]` is the outer set's only key, so the outer element is entered, the\ninner tie deferred, `A = cc-1` at index 1, and the fixpoint gives\n`B = cc-2`. Loop plus 2-cycle: `[L L]` keys as `[u0 r] [u0 r]`, the two\nothers as `[u0 r] [u1 r]`, so `L = cc-1` by key order; `{[A B], [B A]}`\nties, the fixpoint changes nothing, and the component `{[A B], [B A]}`\nholds two elements of one group: D5 refuses; **D6** runs two trials\nover `[:component 1 [:set [A B] [B A]]]`, one entering `[A B]` first\n(`A = u0, B = u1`, then `[B A]` keys `[u1 u0]` and is entered), one the\nmirror image; the two spellings are equal, the first is adopted:\n`A = cc-2`, `B = cc-3`. 2-cycle alone: the same two trials from the empty\nnumbering, `A = cc-1`, `B = cc-2`, and 6a's search reaches the same\nrows. 3-cycle: three trials, each a rotation of the others, equal\nspellings, `A, B, C = cc-1, cc-2, cc-3` in the first trial's order, again\n6a's rows. Path `#{[A B] [B C]}`: two trials, `[A B]` first gives\n`[:set [u0 u1] [u1 u2]]`, `[B C]` first gives `[:set [u2 u0] [u0 u1]]`;\nthe least is adopted. The example state of\n`collab/example-suspended-state.clj` has one cursor cell, first met in\nthe act's environment under `c`, so `cc-1`; a one-cell state has the\nsame rows under both procedures.\n\nPins: none of the round-1 or round-2 pins change (`fab223…`, `c4a7c7…`,\n`a3713a5e…`, `07eda35d…`, `da69c679…`, `49d4478c…`, `4993b5b1…`,\n`536c810b…`, `65ba4530…`, `409548a7…`, `18b70de6…`, `92747c0f…`,\n`7dc3d5c4…`, `6ea03f9b…`, `b35b8e12…`, the ten-cell root `6e7c7a6b…`),\nwith the rank spelling and with D6 on. New pins: the witness\n`9600fe01…`; under D6 the loop plus 2-cycle `858a891e…` (6a's `55ff168e…`\nstays removed), the 2-cycle `0fe898cc…` and 3-cycle `0b181cd4…` (6a's\nown roots), the path `726a9fbb…`, the seven-cell state `d76d787a…`.\n\n## 7. Recommendation and cost\n\n**Adopt discovery order with D6** (owner decision: adopt; D6 is this\nnote's recommendation within it). 6b has not started and 6a is\nunmerged, so the cost is one implementation slice of `rows.cljc` and its\ntests (§8) plus the amendments of §4. Size, from the prototype and the\nsecond review's count rather than the round-2 guess: the prototype's\nschema-to-procedure region is about 330 nonblank lines, of which about\n30 are D6, the diagnostic mode, tracing and measurement; in place it\nreuses 6a's `descriptors`, `bytes-key`, `compare-bytes`, `spend!`,\n`refuse!` and `emit-node`, so expect **250–300 added or replacement\nlines and 135–150 deleted** (`check-label-bytes!`, labels, `ce`,\n`positions`, `classes`, `refine`, `search`), **about 100–160 net**,\nexcluding tests; the second review's independent estimate was 100–195\nnet. Review cost: one implementation round and one review round; the\nreview reads §3 (five lemmas, one induction) and §8's cut list rather\nthan re-deriving the design, and the generator of §9 item 18 runs in CI.\nWhat the owner gains over 6a: a definition that can be stated and proved\n(the n-th cell met, with one bounded tie-break), no growing labels, a\nsearch confined to the residue (two trials for a 2-cycle; 6a's search\nover the seven-cell state exceeded a megabyte of labels, D6 numbers it in\n11 trials), and 6a's own roots on the 2-cycle and 3-cycle. Risk: a\ncanonicality bug, mitigated by §9 item 18's generator in CI (it caught\nthe prototype's memo bug), by the decode round trip in that generator,\nand by the proof; and the unproved cost bound, which the `:work` and\n`:search` budgets enforce as 6a's budgets did.\n\n**D5 variant (no search at all).** Same slice minus D6 (about 25 lines),\nplus the `:unresolved-cursor-cells` kind, its decode translation and the\ndocumented refusal class of F4; refuses 2-cycles and 3-cycles that 6a\nnumbers. Acceptable if the owner wants no search in the codec; the\nrefusal can be replaced by D6 later without changing any id numbered\nunder D1–D4 (§3 consequence 6).\n\n**Keep 6a.** Zero rounds now, but 6b, the cross-engine harness and every\nreviewer of a pinned id inherit an exhaustive search over the whole\nstate, labels that grow until the seven-cell state exceeds a megabyte,\nand a definition that is \"whatever the procedure outputs\". Not\nrecommended; the owner has decided against it.\n\n**Portable ids.** Changes the VM, the census, the laws and the harness\n(§5); three or more rounds across two designs. Against.\n\n## 8. Replacing 6a: what is deleted, rewritten and kept\n\nAgainst branch `srvm-phase6a` (`d77da9f0`), `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc`\n(line numbers of that file) and `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc`. An\nimplementation brief can be cut from this list.\n\n**rows.cljc, deleted.**\n- `check-label-bytes!` (`:212-230`): the label-size bound; nothing grows\n  any more.\n- Inside `canonical-numbering` (`:236-355`): the label seeding\n  (`:252-255`), `ce-slot`/`ce` (`:257-284`), `positions` (`:285-325`),\n  `classes` (`:326-330`), `refine` (`:331-337`), `search` (`:338-354`),\n  and the `checked-encode`/`checked-key`/`checked-sort` wrappers\n  (`:245-251`). The `[:child (checked-encode id)]` spelling (`:273-275`)\n  goes with `ce`.\n- `default-budget` (`:200-202`): the `:label-bytes` key; the `:search`\n  key too in the D5 variant (with D6 it stays and counts trials).\n\n**rows.cljc, rewritten.**\n- `canonical-numbering` (`:236-355`), same signature and contract\n  (`task`, optional `budget` → `{source-id :yin.k/cc-n}`; empty census →\n  `{}`), now the root run of §2: `descriptors` extended by the two\n  synthetic kinds `:entry` and `:component`; body ranks from the census;\n  `enter`/`unordered`/`fixpoint`/`components`/`component-encoding`/\n  `adopt`/`resolve`/`local-run`/`spell` as in the prototype\n  (`collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj`, sections \"runs\" and\n  \"spelling\"), with `spend!` charged as §2 \"Work\" says; the totality\n  assertion before returning; D5 raising `:yin.k/non-portable\n  :unresolved-cursor-cells` (D5 variant) or D6 charging `:search`.\n  Portable `.cljc` per continuation §12: no `@#'` reflection, `mapv`/\n  `into`/`reduce` (not `for`) over sequences that may exceed 32 elements\n  (the prototype's `components` uses `for`; replace), `contains?` for\n  membership, `(assoc {} …)` never `(assoc nil …)`, atoms are fine on all\n  three hosts.\n- `emit-node` (`:181-197`): thread a row path so a child's D5 refusal\n  reports `[12 <install name> …]` (D5 variant only; otherwise unchanged).\n- `decode*` recompute (`:570-575`): wrap the `emit` call (which numbers\n  the root and every child) so that only `:yin.k/non-portable\n  :unresolved-cursor-cells` becomes `:yin.k/undecodable\n  :unresolved-cursor-cells` with the path prefixed by `root`; a\n  `:resource-limit` propagates (D5 variant only; with D6 the recompute\n  is unchanged apart from the budget keys).\n- Namespace docstring (`:1-17`): \"canonical cursor-cell numbering\" →\n  \"discovery-order cursor-cell numbering (design note §2)\".\n\n**rows.cljc, kept unchanged.** `row-table`, `pending-table`, `node`,\n`refuse!`/`bad!`, the marker checks, `bytes-key`/`compare-bytes`/\n`byte-compare`/`sorted-bytes`, `descriptors`, the census order\n(`:137-156`), `emit-slot`/`emit-node` apart from the path, `spend!`\n(`:205-209`), `emit-numbered` (`:358-363`), `emit` (`:366-372`),\n`structural?`, `literal?`, `check-abstract!`, all of `decode*` before\n`:570` and after `:575` (the order numbering → `validate-frames`,\n`:576-578`, stays), `decode`, `canonical-under`, `frame->rows`,\n`rows->frame`, `validate-frames`, and the `:bytes`, `:rows`, `:depth`,\n`:cursor-cells`, `:work` budget keys with their defaults.\n\n**rows_test.cljc, kept as fixtures and tests.** `task-node`,\n`cursor-cell-body`, `fixture` (`:16-37`), `portable-frame` and the\n`bijection` test, `row-ids-are-jing-default`, `replace-row`,\n`window-sorted-and-unique`, `canonical-under-is-renaming-invariant`\n(`:143-156`), `alias-topology-and-census` (`:159-168`),\n`gates-and-graph-before-hashes`, `numbering-precedes-frame-defect`\n(`:206-213`), `schema-and-literal-canonicality`,\n`child-scope-and-shared-child-dag` (`:240-249`),\n`pending-envelope-and-nil-literal-are-preserved`,\n`task-root-checks-carried-code-and-frame`, `reversed` and\n`unordered-containers-are-order-free` (`:285-304`),\n`census-is-numeric-from-ten-cursor-cells` (`:331-338`), the marker tests\n(`:341-381`), `cursor-cell-and-identity-shaped-literal-are-distinct`\n(`:384-389`), and the fixture `seven-cursor-cell-triangle-square`\n(`:392-397`). Every counterexample of §6 becomes a fixture (items 12–16\nbelow), including the refused ones.\n\n**rows_test.cljc, rewritten.**\n- `refinement-search-on-symmetric-cursor-cells-is-bounded` (`:171-178`)\n  → `symmetric-cursor-cells-number-by-the-component-rule`: same two\n  assertions on equal roots and two numbers; the `{:search 1}`\n  assertions become `{:work 1}` → `:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit`.\n- `canonical-roots-are-pinned` (`:307-328`): `fab223…` and `c4a7c7…`\n  stay; the loop plus 2-cycle block loses the `{:search 2}` assertion and\n  the pin `55ff168e…`; under D6 it pins `858a891e…` with `L = cc-1`\n  (and the renamed task equal); under the D5 variant it asserts the\n  `:unresolved-cursor-cells` refusal with path `[15]` and cells `[A B]`.\n- `intermediate-label-bytes-are-bounded` (`:400-403`) →\n  `seven-cursor-cells-number-by-residue-search` (D6: root `d76d787a…`,\n  11 trials, `{:search 10}` → `:resource-limit [:search]`) or, in the D5\n  variant, `seven-cursor-cells-are-refused` (`:unresolved-cursor-cells`,\n  cells `[a b c d e f g]`).\n- `input-row-bodies-do-not-spend-label-bytes` (`:406-421`) →\n  `large-literals-do-not-spend-work-beyond-their-bytes`, same body: a\n  400000-element literal still emits and decodes under the default\n  budget.\n\n## 9. Acceptance for the rewritten 6a numbering\n\n12. `rows-test/discovery-order-numbering`: for `fixture :s1 :s2 :x :y`,\n    the review counterexample, `[x x y]`, `[x y y]`, `[#{X Y} X]`, the\n    witness `[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]` and the two frame fixtures of §6,\n    `canonical-numbering` returns exactly the maps of §6.\n13. `rows-test/review-counterexample-resolves-by-fixpoint`: `'a #{X Y}`,\n    `'b X` numbers `X cc-1, Y cc-2`; the task renamed and with the set\n    reversed gives the same root; the root is pinned `a3713a5e…`.\n14. `rows-test/component-rule`: `#{X Y}`, `#{[X X] [Y Y]}`, `{X 1, Y 1}`,\n    `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}` and `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` number without refusal;\n    element reversal and renaming give equal roots; pins `07eda35d…`,\n    `da69c679…`, `49d4478c…`, `18b70de6…`, `92747c0f…`.\n15. `rows-test/alias-pattern-is-part-of-the-key`: `#{[X X] [Y Z]}` and\n    `{X X, Y Z}` give one root for both enumerations and after renaming,\n    pinned `65ba4530…` and `409548a7…`; `#{[#{X Y} #{X Y}] [#{P Q} #{R S}]}`\n    numbers and is pinned `b35b8e12…`; the witness `[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]`\n    numbers `A cc-1, B cc-2`, is pinned `9600fe01…` and decodes.\n16. D6: `rows-test/residue-search`: the 2-cycle, the 3-cycle, the path,\n    the loop plus 2-cycle and the seven-cell state number with the pins\n    of §6 (`0fe898cc…`, `0b181cd4…`, `726a9fbb…`, `858a891e…`,\n    `d76d787a…`), renaming plus shuffling gives equal roots, every\n    carrier decodes, and the 3-cycle under `{:search 1}` refuses\n    `:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit [:search]`.\n    D5 variant instead: `rows-test/unresolved-cursor-cells-refuse`: the\n    same five states refuse at `emit` with `:yin.k/non-portable`, kind\n    `:unresolved-cursor-cells`, path `[15]` and the component's sorted\n    cursor cells; a carrier of each built with `emit-numbered` decodes to\n    `:yin.k/undecodable :unresolved-cursor-cells` with the path prefixed\n    by the root, never `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`; a parent whose\n    install `'a` holds the 2-cycle refuses at `emit` with path\n    `[12 a 15]` and its `emit-numbered` carrier decodes to the\n    `:yin.k/undecodable` kind with path `[<root> 12 a 15]`.\n17. `rows-test/canonical-roots-are-pinned`: `fab223…` and `c4a7c7…`\n    unchanged; `a3713a5e…`, `4993b5b1…`, `536c810b…`, `7dc3d5c4…`,\n    `6ea03f9b…`, `9600fe01…` added; `55ff168e…` removed.\n18. `rows-test/counterexample-search`: a seeded generator (one to six\n    cursor cells over two bodies; nested `:vec`, `:set`, `:map` with\n    cursor-cell keys, sets of cursor-cell pairs including `[X X]`, sets of\n    sets, under keyed bindings and the result; unique elements and keys\n    under decoded equality) over at least 2000 states, plus at least 200\n    frame-bearing states (cursor cells in an act window, a K-frame\n    window, environments matched to the free environment, and a parked\n    state at a park site) and at least 100 states with install children:\n    every numbered state's carrier decodes and re-emits to its root, and\n    renaming every cursor cell plus shuffling every set, map and census\n    gives an equal root id; refusals are counted and reported (0 under\n    D6); the largest `:work` and `:search` charged are reported.\n19. `rows-test/numbering-is-total`: a task whose census declares a\n    cursor cell the traversal does not reach raises the totality defect\n    (not a refusal) before any row is emitted.\n20. `numbering-precedes-frame-defect`, `alias-topology-and-census`,\n    `canonical-under-is-renaming-invariant`,\n    `unordered-containers-are-order-free`, `child-scope-and-shared-child-dag`\n    and `census-is-numeric-from-ten-cursor-cells` pass unchanged; the\n    rewritten tests of §8 pass.\n21. `rows-test/work-is-charged-per-byte`: a state whose cursor-cell\n    bodies carry a 65536-character descriptor numbers under the default\n    budget with the same `:work` as under a 128-character descriptor\n    (rank spelling), and `{:work 1}` refuses `:yin.k/unsupported\n    :resource-limit [:work]` at `emit` and, for a valid carrier, at\n    `decode`.\n22. Budgets and docs: `:label-bytes` is gone (and `:search` in the D5\n    variant); A1–A9 applied in the variant chosen; kondo clean;\n    `bb test:sub yin.vm` green on three hosts; Node log shows\n    `Testing yin.vm.ucf.rows-test`.\n\n## 10. Owner questions\n\n1. **D6 or the D5 variant?** Recommended: D6 (a search confined to one\n   conflicting component, measured at 2–13 trials, 6a's roots on the\n   2-cycle and 3-cycle, no structural refusal class to document). The D5\n   variant is the same slice without about 25 lines, with the\n   `:unresolved-cursor-cells` kind and the F4 regression (2-cycles and\n   3-cycles refused) as a documented limitation that D6 can lift later\n   without moving any D1–D4 id.\n2. If the D5 variant: the refusal kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells` as\n   proposed, or the orchestrator's `:resource-limit` (recommended\n   against: it is not a budget, and carrier §10.10 says a resource\n   refusal never stands in for a canonicality verdict)?\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1575,"cache_read_input_tokens":202273,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1575},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7984b61f-639f-4af2-b00a-5b9b7d554f75","uuid":"9c90aa23-73a7-49c5-a30c-272ede35a8cd","timestamp":"2026-10-12T06:44:05.206Z","request_id":"req_011CfwtDEjbixq5xhEtKhBe9","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01JsNijEWWzFyCjoUMnYG9vn":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-numbering/docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md","content":"# Cursor-cell numbering by discovery order (companion to the row carrier)\n\nStatus: **Adopted by the owner 2026-10-12 (replaces carrier §6's\nrefinement and search); not yet implemented.** Written 2026-10-12\n(Architect, claude-fable-5-1) for the owner's observation that \"the\nproblem is not that the cursor cell has identity, but that the identity\nis local\", and the orchestrator's proposal to number cursor cells by\n**discovery order** in a canonical traversal. Revised twice the same day\nafter independent reviews (gpt-6.1-sol,\n`collab/1792140000000-…findings.md` and `collab/1792170000000-…findings.md`).\nRound 2 repaired the orbit lemma (`#{[X X] [Y Z]}`) and nested tie groups\n(`#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}`). Round 3 (this text) fixes what the second review\nfound: which local conflicts refuse (§2 D5, witness\n`[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]`), the install-child refusal escaping decode, the\nwork bound (now an explicit budget, with body labels spelled by rank so\nkey bytes no longer grow with a stream descriptor), the A2 wording, the\nsize estimate, and the coverage regression against 6a (2-cycles and\n3-cycles), for which §2 D6 specifies and §6 measures the repair. §8 lists\nwhat an implementation deletes, rewrites and keeps in 6a. Subordinate to\n[`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md)\n(the carrier, body version 3) and\n[`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md`](./yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md);\nvocabulary is UCF §7.5.3 (\"Terminology: cursor, cursor cell, cursor-ref,\ncell\") exactly. Evidence is the carrier draft §5/§6 tables, slice 6a's\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc` and `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc`\n(branch `srvm-phase6a` at `d77da9f0`), and a prototype run against that\ncode (§6). Nothing here is implemented.\n\n**In one line:** number cursor cells in the order a canonical traversal\nmeets them (D1–D2); order the elements of a guest set or map by a key\nthat is the spelling of a speculative local run over the element (so\nalias patterns such as `[X X]` versus `[Y Z]` are part of the key);\ndefer equal keys and re-examine them to a fixpoint (D3); at a fixpoint,\nlink tied elements that share an unnumbered cursor cell into components\nand enter the component of least complete encoding (D4); a component\nholding two equal-keyed elements of one container is the one case this\ncannot certify: refuse it (D5), or resolve it by a bounded residue search\nover that component alone (D6, recommended). Proof in §3, budget in §2\n\"Work\", results in §6, the cut against 6a in §8.\n\n## 0. Vocabulary used below\n\n- **Canonical traversal order** is the order D1 defines: a function of\n  the abstract task alone. **Host enumeration order** (also \"listed\n  order\") is the order in which a host happens to list the elements of a\n  guest set or the entries of a guest map, or the census; it is an\n  accident of the host. The procedure reads it in exactly one place, D4's\n  entry of components of equal encoding, where lemma C shows it cannot\n  change the result.\n- An **element** is a `:set` element or a `:map` entry `[key value]`\n  (the key is a guest value and may be a cursor cell).\n- A **numbering** `N` is a partial map from the task's cursor cells to\n  positive integers, injective, assigned in increasing order; `|N|` is\n  its size. The emitted id of a cell numbered `k` is `:yin.k/cc-k`.\n- A **run** is one execution of the traversal-and-resolution procedure\n  over one item (a row or a value) from a starting numbering. The **root\n  run** is the run over the `:task` row from the empty numbering; its\n  numbers are the output. A **local run** is a run over one item from\n  the enclosing run's current numbering. There are three kinds of local\n  run, and the distinction decides what refuses (D5):\n  - a **key run** is a local run over one element, made to compute the\n    element's sort key; it is *speculative*: it never refuses, and\n    whatever it cannot resolve (a tie, a conflict) is spelled in its\n    key as `[:tie …]`;\n  - a **component run** is a local run over a component's synthetic\n    `[:component …]` row (D4); it must be *complete*, else D5;\n  - a **trial** (D6 only) is a component run that enters one chosen\n    element before its siblings.\n- A **body label** is `[:body [:stream identity descriptor] position]`,\n  a cursor cell's census body as plain data; its **rank** is its index\n  among the census's distinct body labels in bytewise order.\n\n## 1. Findings\n\n**F1. Every non-guest position is ordered.** From the carrier §5 tables\n(and `rows/row-table`, `rows.cljc:37-57`), the positions that can hold a\ncursor-ref (`[:cursor-cell C]`) or a bare `C` slot, and their order:\n\n| Position | Order | Canonical index |\n|---|---|---|\n| `:task` slots 5 `free-env`, 6 `store`, 8 `parked`, 15 `result`; `:wait` state, pending; `:act` env, window, k; `:tail` k; `:kframe` env, window; `:reified` act; `:env` and `:closure` bindings; `:module-store` store; `:pending` `cursor-cell-C`, `response-cursor-cell-C`, `value-V`, `envelope-V` | fixed slot | slot index |\n| `:task` 7 `module-stores`, 12 `installs` | keyed by `M`, by module name | Jing bytes of the key; the child is a task boundary |\n| `:task` 13 `frames`; `:k`; `:vec`, `:list` elements | ordered vector | position `i` |\n| `:window` | keyed by register `N` | `N` ascending, numerically (carrier §5.2) |\n| `:bindings` (environments, closures, the free environment) | keyed by symbol `S` | Jing canonical bytes of `S` (§5 preamble: \"sorted\") |\n| `:store`, `:parked-table` | keyed by `D`, by `P` | bytes of the key; a parked-table value is a structural `:act`/`:tail` reference, entered like any row |\n| `:set` elements | **unordered** | none |\n| `:map` entries | **unordered** (keys are `V`) | none |\n| `:cursor-cells` census | not a position (carrier §6 step 2, §8 step 3) | — |\n\nSo the only unordered positions are guest `:set` elements and guest\n`:map` entries. Both reviews confirmed the inventory complete. The\nexample state in `collab/example-suspended-state.clj` holds its one\ncursor cell (`:cursor-1`) in three ordered positions: the act's\nenvironment under `c`, the window under register 2, and the `:next`\npending's `cursor-cell-C` slot.\n\n**F2. Discovery order subsumes the positions step.** The carrier's §6\nstep 3 (positions) exists to let a cursor cell's number depend on where\nit occurs. A canonical traversal (D1) visits every ordered position in a\nfixed order, so \"the n-th cursor cell met\" already encodes the first\nposition of each cursor cell; later positions cannot change an assigned\nnumber and need not be collected. Refinement (step 4) and search (step 5)\nexisted only to turn position multisets into a total order; discovery\norder is a total order by construction.\n\n**F3. Unordered containers are ordered by a content encoding that\ncarries the alias pattern.** The first draft spelled an unnumbered cursor\ncell by its body alone, so `[X X]` and `[Y Z]` encoded alike. The\nrepaired encoding (§2 \"Spelling\") numbers the cursor cells *inside* an\nelement provisionally, in the element's own discovery order, relative to\nthe enclosing numbering: `[X X]` spells `[(u0) (u0)]`, `[Y Z]` spells\n`[(u0) (u1)]`, so alias topology inside an element is part of its sort\nkey, as a de Bruijn index is part of a term. The key of an element is the\nspelling produced by a *key run* over it, which applies the same rules\nrecursively (nested containers sorted, nested ties deferred and\nre-examined, D4 inside) and spells what it cannot resolve as `[:tie …]`.\nEqual keys form a **tie group**, deferred to the run's worklist and\nre-examined to a fixpoint (D3) as other positions assign numbers. A\nprovisional cell is spelled with its body *rank*, not its body label, so\na key's size depends on the element's structure and not on the length\nof a stream descriptor (§2 \"Work\").\n\n**F4. What refuses, and what is lost against 6a.** What survives a run's\nfixpoint is a worklist of tie groups whose unnumbered cursor cells occur\nonly inside tied elements. Link two tied elements when they share an\nunnumbered cursor cell; a connected set of tied elements is a\n**component**. Two cases:\n- *Certified*: no component holds two elements of one group. Every\n  component is then encoded by a component run over its elements in\n  group order (so a component spanning two groups, `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]`,\n  spells its shared cell once with one index), components are sorted by\n  that encoding, and the least run of equal encodings is entered in host\n  order. Equal encodings here mean *isomorphic* components (§3, lemma C).\n  `#{X Y}`, `#{[X X] [Y Y]}`, `{X 1, Y 1}`, `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}` and\n  `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` are this case.\n- *Conflict*: some component holds two elements of one tie group. Then\n  entering either element first is a choice D1–D4 cannot show to be\n  harmless. **Which runs refuse.** Only the *root run* and *component\n  runs* refuse on a conflict (D5). A *key run* never refuses: it is a\n  speculation made to sort, its unresolved residue is spelled `[:tie …]`\n  in the key, and the enclosing run may resolve the same elements later.\n  The second review's witness is exactly this: in `[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]`\n  the key run over the inner set reaches a conflict (`[A B]` and `[B A]`\n  tie and share both cells), the outer set has one element and is\n  entered, the inner tie is deferred to the root worklist, index 1 meets\n  `A = cc-1`, and the root fixpoint separates `[A B]` (`[(n 1) (u0)]`)\n  from `[B A]` (`[(u0) (n 1)]`), so `B = cc-2`; the lift numbers and the\n  receiver recomputes the same root (`9600fe01…`, the review's digest).\n  The earlier text's \"at any fixpoint of any run\" was wrong and is\n  replaced in D5, lemma D and A1.\n  The predicate, in one sentence: *a state is refused under D5 iff the\n  root run, or a component run it requires, reaches a fixpoint at which\n  two elements of one guest set or map have equal keys and are connected\n  by a chain of shared unnumbered cursor cells through tied elements.*\n\n  **Exactly which structures that is.** A D5 state needs two or more\n  cursor cells with *equal bodies* (same stream identity and descriptor,\n  same position) that are held *only* through guest sets or maps, in\n  elements that each mention more than one of them, with equal keys. The\n  smallest are the directed 2-cycle `#{[A B] [B A]}` and the directed\n  3-cycle `#{[A B] [B C] [C A]}` (both interchangeable: the swap and the\n  rotation are automorphisms, so any entering order gives the same rows,\n  and 6a numbers them); the path `#{[A B] [B C]}` and the seven-cell\n  triangle plus square (`rows_test.cljc:392-404`) are refused and\n  genuinely need a tie-break (§6: two and four distinct roots under\n  host-order entry); the loop plus 2-cycle `#{[L L] [A B] [B A]}`\n  (`rows_test.cljc:312-329`) numbers `L = cc-1` by its key and conflicts\n  on `{A, B}`. One register, environment binding, pending slot or vector\n  position holding any one of the cells breaks the tie for the whole\n  component (`[#{X Y} X]`, the witness above). **Are they common in\n  realistic suspended tasks?** Unmeasured: the repository has no\n  version-3 task corpus (the Stage E scenarios and the v2 handoff census\n  tests hold cursor cells but no v3 lift exists before 6b). The structural\n  argument is that a cursor cell is created into a register\n  (`:stream/cursor` delivers to `rd`, engine `:2595-2602`) and reaches a\n  guest set only by guest code that builds a set of tuples of cursors on\n  one stream at one position and then drops every other reference; of\n  the repository's own fixtures only the two built for the purpose hit\n  D5, and under a tie-biased generator the rate is 35 of 2300 (§6).\n  Because the rate is unmeasured and 6a numbers the 2-cycle and 3-cycle,\n  this note does not leave the regression as a documented limitation:\n  **D6** (§2) is the minimum extra rule that repairs it, a search over\n  the conflicting component only (two trials for a 2-cycle, three for a\n  3-cycle, eleven for the seven-cell state), prototyped and measured in\n  §6: with D6 every one of the 2300 generated states numbers, the 2-cycle\n  and 3-cycle reach 6a's exact roots, and no D1–D4 numbering changes.\n  The owner's choice between D5 (refuse, no search anywhere) and D6\n  (bounded residue search) is §10 question 1; this note recommends D6.\n\n**F5. The scheme is canonical.** Proved in §3: the output (rows or\nrefusal) is a function of the abstract task up to isomorphism (renaming\nof cursor cells, permutation of every unordered container and of the\ncensus) and terminating; the work is accounted by the budget of §2\n\"Work\".\n\n**F6. Prototype evidence (§6).** 2000 random value states (one to six\ncursor cells over two bodies, so ties are common; nested vectors, sets,\nmaps with cursor-cell keys, sets of cursor-cell pairs including aliases\nsuch as `[X X]`, sets of sets, cells aliased across containers), 200\nframe-bearing states (act window, K-frame window, environments, a parked\nstate) and 100 states with install children, each compared with itself\nrenamed and shuffled and decoded through 6a's decoder: 0 invariance\nfailures and 0 decode failures in either mode; D5 refuses 35 of 2300, D6\nrefuses none; the largest `:work` charged is 1470 of 6a's default\n200000 and the largest trial count 13 of 10000. Every pin of rounds 1\nand 2 is unchanged (including under the rank spelling and with D6 on);\nthe second review's digest for the witness is reproduced; the key bytes\nof one state under a 65536-character descriptor fell from 920118 (label\nspelling) to 1396 (rank spelling), the same as under a 128-character\ndescriptor.\n\n**F7. Portable ids minted in the VM are the wrong fix.** §5.\n\n## 2. The rule (replaces carrier §6 \"The procedure\")\n\nInput: the abstract task of carrier §6 (the §5 structure with cursor\ncells as identities, before numbering). Output: a bijection from the\ntask's reached cursor cells to `:yin.k/cc-1 … :yin.k/cc-n`, or a\nrefusal. Install children are numbered as their own tasks by the same\nrule (task boundary, unchanged), in any order, since scopes are disjoint.\n\n**Spelling (the content encoding `CE`).** A run spells the item it has\ntraversed under its final numbering. The root run spells a cursor cell\nnumbered `k` as `[:cursor-cell [:n k]]`, `k` an exact integer; a local\nrun spells a cursor cell it numbered itself (index `j`, counted from the\nenclosing run's local counter) as `[:cursor-cell [:u j r]]`, `r` the\ncell's body rank (an exact integer; rank order equals body-label order,\nso no comparison and no numbering changes against spelling the label,\nand the key's size no longer depends on the descriptor), and a cursor\ncell numbered by an enclosing run as that run spelled it. Nothing else\nchanges from carrier §6 step 2: `[:lit d]` as itself, `[:row B]`\nexpanded in place, structural data as itself, the census as\n`[:cursor-cells]`, an install child as `[:child]` (replacing step 2's\n`[:child <child root id>]`: a child sits only under a name-keyed install\nentry, never inside an unordered container, so its content never decides\nan order, and the encoding stays hash-free). Keyed structural rows are\nspelled in their canonical key order, `:window` in numeric register\norder; a `:set` or `:map` is spelled as the bytewise sorted vector of its\nelements' spellings (an entry as `[:entry K V]`), duplicates retained,\nwhere a tie group the run could not resolve is spelled in place as\n`[:tie K … K]` (its common key, once per element). All comparisons are\nbytewise on canonical CBOR of these synthetic vectors. `CE` is hash-free.\n\n**D1. Traversal.** Depth-first, pre-order, from the run's root item. A\nrow's slots are visited in table order. Ordered repeated slots\n(`frames`, `:vec`, `:list`, `:k`) in order. Keyed rows with structural\nkeys (`:bindings`, `:store`, `:parked-table`, the root's `installs` and\n`module-stores`) in the Jing canonical byte order of the key, the order\n§5 emits; `:window` in ascending numeric register order as the carrier\nspecifies. The census is not entered; an install child's `:task` row is\nnot entered. A `:set`'s elements and a `:map`'s entries are each given a\n**key**: the spelling of a key run over the element under the numbering\nat the moment the container is entered (every element keyed from that\none numbering). Elements are then taken in bytewise key order: a run of\nequal keys is a **tie group**, appended to the run's worklist (deferral\norder is canonical: containers in traversal order, groups within one\ncontainer in key order); every other element is entered in key order,\nan entry's key before its value.\n\n**D2. Meeting.** When the traversal reaches an identity position (a\n`[:cursor-cell C]` value or a `C` slot of a `:pending` row) whose cursor\ncell has no number, the cursor cell receives `|N| + 1` (the root run\ncounts from 1; a local run counts its own cells from the enclosing run's\nlocal counter, so a nested local run never reuses an index of its\nenclosing run). An already numbered cursor cell is passed over. There is\nno first-occurrence spelling: every occurrence emits\n`[:cursor-cell :yin.k/cc-n]` and the census row (carrier §3) stays the\ncontents table.\n\n**D3. Fixpoint.** After the traversal, the worklist's groups are\nre-examined in deferral order: each group's elements are re-keyed under\nthe current numbering and taken as in D1 (singletons entered, equal runs\nre-deferred in order). Repeat while a round numbers at least one cursor\ncell and groups remain. Entering an element inside a group runs D1 on\nit, so a group discovered *inside* an entered element (`#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}`)\njoins the same worklist and is re-examined in the next round; there is\none worklist per run, and the root run's worklist collects deferred\ngroups from every depth.\n\n**D4. Components.** When a fixpoint is reached with groups remaining,\nlink two tied elements (of any groups) that share an unnumbered cursor\ncell; a connected set of tied elements is a **component**. (At a\nfixpoint every unnumbered cursor cell occurs only inside tied elements,\nso all of its occurrences lie in one component.) Encode each component\nby a component run over the synthetic ordered row\n`[:component g₁ i₁ g₂ i₂ …]` in group order, `gᵢ` the group's deferral\nsequence number (a datum) and `iᵢ` the component's one element of that\ngroup, or, when the component holds several elements of group `gᵢ`, a\nsynthetic `:set` of them (the D5/D6 case). A component run must be\ncomplete (no `[:tie]` in its spelling); it is incomplete only when it\nreaches a conflict of its own, which D5 or D6 decides. Sort the\ncomponents by their encodings and **enter the run of least encoding**:\neach of its components has its component run's cursor cells numbered,\nin that run's local-index order, continuing the enclosing run's\nnumbering; components of equal encoding are entered in host order (§3,\nlemma C: equal encodings are isomorphic and the result is the same).\nRemove the entered elements from their groups and return to D3. A group\nwhose elements mention no unnumbered cursor cell holds equal values (the\ncarrier refuses the duplicate at §8 step 5); its elements are entered in\nhost order, which numbers nothing and emits identical rows.\n\n**D5. Conflict, and refusal without D6.** A component **conflicts** when\nit holds two elements of one group. Without D6, a conflict reached by the\nroot run, or by a component run (directly, or through a nested component\nrun of its own), refuses the lift: `:yin.k/non-portable` kind\n`:unresolved-cursor-cells`, `:yin.k/path` the row path of the container\nholding the least conflicting group (for an install child, prefixed\n`[12 <install name> …]`), and `:yin.k/cursor-cells` the sorted unnumbered\ncursor cells of every conflicting component at that fixpoint (so the\ndiagnostic, like the verdict, does not depend on host order). A key run\nnever refuses (§0; F4). A receiver that reaches D5 while recomputing\nrefuses `:yin.k/undecodable` kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`. Neither is\na resource refusal and neither is `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`.\n\n**D6. Residue search (recommended; the repair of F4's regression).** A\nconflict is resolved instead of refused: let `g` be the least conflicting\ngroup of the component and `E` the component's elements of `g`. For each\n`e ∈ E` run a **trial**: a component run over the same `[:component …]`\nrow in which `e` is entered first when its container is keyed, before\nits siblings are keyed (so `e`'s cursor cells take the first local\nindices and its siblings are keyed against them), and which runs to\ncompletion by D1–D6. Each trial charges `:search` one unit. The\ncomponent's encoding is the least trial spelling, and entering the\ncomponent adopts that trial's numbering (D4). Equal least spellings are\nisomorphic and either may be adopted (§3, lemma E). With D6 no\nstructural refusal remains: the only refusals of the procedure are the\nresource refusals of \"Work\" below, and `:unresolved-cursor-cells` is not\ndefined. With D6 in force, 6a's `:search` budget key stays and counts\ntrials; without it, the key is deleted.\n\n**Totality.** The root run ends with an empty worklist or a refusal.\nBefore emission the implementation asserts that the numbering is a\nbijection from the census's cursor cells onto `1 … n`; a violation is a\ndefect (an exception naming the missing or extra cursor cells), never a\nrefusal, and never a partially numbered carrier.\n\n**Memoization (implementation note, not part of the definition).** A\nlocal run's result is a function of its item, of the enclosing numbering\nrestricted to the item's cursor cells, of the enclosing local counter and\n(D6) of the forced element; an implementation may memoize on that key and\nmust return the numbering restricted to the item's own cursor cells (the\nprototype's one bug during round 2 was returning the whole numbering of\nanother element's context, which the decode round trip caught). The memo\nholds only spellings that were paid for under `:work`, so it adds no\nunaccounted bytes. No bound on the number of distinct memo contexts is\nclaimed; the memo is an optimization and the budget is the bound.\n\n**Validation and decode.** Unchanged in form: the receiver decodes, runs\nthe root run, re-emits and compares root ids (`:noncanonical-cursor-cells`\non mismatch). In 6a's `decode*` the recomputation is `emit` itself\n(`rows.cljc:570-575`), which numbers the root *and every install child*\n(`emit-node`, `:185-189`); the **whole** of that call is wrapped so that\nonly a lift-side `:yin.k/non-portable` of kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`\n(D5, from the root or from a child) is translated to `:yin.k/undecodable`\nkind `:unresolved-cursor-cells` with `:yin.k/path` prefixed by the\ncarried root id (a child's path then reads `[<root id> 12 <name> …]`);\na resource refusal (`:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit`) propagates as\nitself; no other non-portable kind can arise there, because step 5 has\nalready checked literals and markers. The second review's escape (an\ninstall child's D5 leaving `decode*` as `:yin.k/non-portable` with path\n`[:child 15]`) came from translating only the root's numbering; the\nprototype now wraps the whole recomputation and the case is in §6. The\nvalidator order stays: step 6 (numbering) after step 5 and before\n`validate-frames` (`rows.cljc:576-578`; test\n`numbering-precedes-frame-defect`, `rows_test.cljc:206`). With D6 the\ntranslation has nothing to translate and is omitted.\n\n**Work.** No polynomial bound is claimed. The second review showed the\nround-2 bound counted positions but not bytes (a 65536-character stream\ndescriptor multiplied key bytes by 170 at fixed `P`, `d`, `n`, `m`) and\nthat memoization's benefit across differing local contexts is unproved.\nThe procedure is instead *accounted*, as 6a already accounts refinement:\nthe `:work` budget is charged one unit per position visited (D1, D3,\nevery local run) and one unit per position spelled plus one unit per\nbyte of literal or structural datum copied into a spelling, before the\ncopy; `:search` (D6) is charged one unit per trial; `:depth` bounds the\nstructural depth as today, `:cursor-cells` bounds `n`, `:rows` the\ncarried rows and `:bytes` the carried bytes. Exceeding any budget is the\nresource refusal of carrier §10.10 (`:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit\n[<key>]`), never a canonicality verdict, exactly as 6a's `:label-bytes`\nwas. The budget charges every allocated spelling byte before allocation,\nso the bytes materialized at any time are bounded by `:work` plus the\ncarried bytes. What the rank spelling guarantees is narrower and exact:\na key's bytes are a function of the element's expanded structure and of\nthe number of distinct bodies, not of the bodies' sizes (§6: 1396 key\nbytes at both 128 and 65536 descriptor characters). The crude bound\nwithout memoization is `O(P · (n + 1)^(d + 1) · log m)` positions and\n`O(B)` bytes per spelling (`P` expanded positions counted per reference,\n`B` expanded bytes, `d` the unordered-container nesting depth, `n` cursor\ncells, `m` the largest container), which the budget enforces rather than\nthe analysis. Measured: every fixture of §6 charges under 1000 `:work`\nunits, the 2300 generated states at most 1470, against 6a's default\n200000; the seven-cell state charges 488 and 11 trials under D6. The\n`:label-bytes` budget disappears with the growing labels.\n\n## 3. Proof of canonicality\n\nCall two configurations (abstract task, partial numbering, worklist)\n**isomorphic** when a bijection `π` of cursor cells carries one to the\nother: the structures agree up to renaming by `π` and up to the host\norder of every unordered container and of the census, numbers agree\n(`N'(π(c)) = N(c)`), and worklists correspond group by group in order.\nThe proof is a simultaneous induction over runs, ordered by (number of\nunnumbered cursor cells of the run's item, nesting depth of the item):\na key run, a component run or a trial is over an item with no more\nunnumbered cells than its enclosing run and strictly fewer nested\nunordered containers, or (a trial) strictly fewer unnumbered cells; so\nthe lemmas below may assume themselves for every local run a run spawns.\n\n**Lemma A (keys are canonical).** The key of an element is the spelling\nof a key run over it, and a key run reads only structure, body ranks\n(the rank of a body is a function of the census up to isomorphism) and\nnumbers; its own listed-order inputs are (i) element order inside nested\ncontainers, which it never uses except through keys, by the induction,\n(ii) the host order of components of equal encoding in its D4 step,\ncovered by lemma C, and (iii) nothing else: a key run that reaches a\nconflict does not choose, it spells the residue `[:tie K … K]`, which is\na function of the group's common key and size. So isomorphic items under\ncorresponding numberings have byte-identical keys, and the sorted order\nof a container's elements is a function of the configuration up to\nisomorphism.\n\n**Lemma B (ties are exactly what keys cannot separate).** By\nconstruction: elements are deferred iff their keys are equal bytes.\n\n**Lemma C (components).** At a fixpoint let `C₁ … Cₘ` be the components\nof one run of equal encodings, each encoding complete, and let\n`U₁ … Uₘ` be their unnumbered cursor cells. The `Uᵢ` are pairwise\ndisjoint and each `Uᵢ` is confined to `Cᵢ` (every occurrence of an\nunnumbered cell lies in a tied element, hence in its own component).\nEqual, complete encodings of `[:component g₁ i₁ …]` rows are a faithful\nspelling (literals as themselves, rows expanded, every cursor cell by\nindex and body rank, groups by sequence number, a group's several\nelements as a sorted set), so they exhibit, for each pair `Cᵢ, Cⱼ`, a\nbijection `Uᵢ → Uⱼ` under which the components are the same structure\ngroup by group. Entering the run in host order `σ` gives the cells of\n`C_{σ(t)}` the numbers `b + (t−1)·|U| + 1 …` by local index; in order\n`σ'` the cells of `C_{σ'(t)}` receive the same numbers. The block-swap\nbijection composed from the pairwise bijections is an isomorphism of the\ntwo resulting configurations: numbers agree by construction, structures\nagree because the swapped components are isomorphic and nothing outside\nthem mentions their cells, and the worklists agree because the same\ngroups remain, with the same elements removed. ∎\n\n**Lemma D (the D5 verdict is invariant).** Whether the root run, or a\ncomponent run it requires, reaches a fixpoint with a conflicting\ncomponent depends only on which tied elements share unnumbered cursor\ncells at that fixpoint, which is a function of the configuration up to\nisomorphism (lemma A gives the ties, the sharing relation is structural);\nand whether a component run is complete is the same question one level\ndown, by the induction. Key runs do not enter the verdict: their\nconflicts are spelled, not refused, and the spelling is canonical (lemma\nA iii).\n\n**Lemma E (D6 trials).** At a conflicting component the set `E` of\nelements of its least conflicting group is a function of the\nconfiguration up to isomorphism (lemma D names the component and its\ngroups canonically). Each trial is a deterministic function of (the\nconfiguration up to isomorphism, the chosen element), by the induction\non the trial run, which has strictly fewer unnumbered cells than the\nenclosing run (its forced element holds at least one, being linked into\nthe component by a shared unnumbered cell). So the multiset of trial\nspellings is canonical and its least member is canonical. Two trials\nwith equal least spellings have produced isomorphic numberings of the\ncomponent (faithful spelling, as in lemma C), so adopting either yields\nisomorphic configurations. ∎\n\n**Theorem.** The procedure's output on isomorphic configurations is the\nsame: both refuse at corresponding points, or both emit byte-identical\nrows. *Proof.* By the induction above. D1 and D2 decisions are functions\nof the configuration up to isomorphism (lemma A); D3 only repeats D1 on\ndeferred groups in a canonical order; D4's choice of the least run is\ncanonical (lemma A on component encodings, which are complete by D5/D6)\nand its only listed-order input is harmless (lemma C); D5 is invariant\n(lemma D); D6 is invariant (lemma E). Emission spells cursor cells by\nnumber only and sorts every emitted unordered slot by canonical bytes\nunder the final numbering (carrier §5), so isomorphic final\nconfigurations emit the same rows. ∎\n\nConsequences:\n\n1. *Function of structure, renaming-invariant, enumeration-invariant*:\n   the theorem with `π` a renaming, a permutation of the containers, or\n   both. No decision reads a source id or a host order except at the\n   point lemma C covers.\n2. *Terminating.* The structural edge family is acyclic (carrier §8 step\n   3), so every traversal is finite. In any run each D3 round that is not\n   the last numbers a cursor cell; each D4 step numbers at least one\n   cursor cell or removes a duplicate group; each local run is over an\n   item with fewer unordered containers nested below it than its\n   spawning run's item, or (a trial) with fewer unnumbered cells, so the\n   induction measure of §3 decreases and there are finitely many local\n   runs. The budget of §2 \"Work\" is what bounds the count.\n3. *Deterministic*, hence two lifts of one state are byte-identical and\n   the receiver's recomputation is well defined.\n4. *Sharing survives*: the numbering is a bijection on reached cursor\n   cells (D2 never renumbers; totality is asserted), every occurrence of\n   one cursor cell spells one number, and two cursor cells at one\n   position get two numbers.\n5. *Cross-engine law*: two engines lifting one canonical frame produce\n   abstract tasks equal up to renaming (the frame is engine-independent,\n   continuation §2.1) and therefore, by the theorem, equal rows.\n6. *D6 moves no D1–D4 id.* Trials run only where D4 found a conflict,\n   which without D6 is a refusal; every numbering D1–D4 assign is\n   identical with and without D6 (§6 checks the pins both ways).\n\n## 4. Amendments\n\n**A1 — carrier §6, from \"The procedure.\" to the end of step 5.** Replace\nwith §2 above (vocabulary, the spelling, D1–D6, totality, work). Keep the\nsection's first paragraph (the counterexample) and amend \"What is\ndefined, and what is not claimed\" to: \"The canonical numbering is defined\nas the output of the discovery-order procedure below, which is a function\nof the state up to renaming of cursor cells and host enumeration of guest\nsets, maps and the census (proof in\n`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md` §3). It is not the\nminimum of the root bytes over all bijections. The one choice the\ntraversal cannot make by itself, between two equal-keyed elements of one\nguest set or map that are linked by shared unnumbered cursor cells at a\nfixpoint of the root run or of a component run, is resolved by a search\nconfined to that component (D6) [or, in the D5 variant: is refused (D5),\nnot searched; the refusal certifies nothing about interchangeability and\ncan be narrowed later without changing any numbering assigned today].\"\nState in step 2's replacement that `[:n k]` and `[:u j r]` carry exact\nCBOR integers, that `r` is the body rank, and that `[:child]` replaces\n`[:child <child root id>]` (reason in §2 \"Spelling\").\n\n**A2 — carrier §6 \"Validation\".** Replace the paragraph's last two\nsentences, from \"Every labeling that yields the selected commitment is\nthereby accepted, and no encounter-order tie-break ever decides validity\"\nto the end, with: \"Every labeling that yields the selected commitment is\nthereby accepted. The emitted commitment and the acceptance decision are\ninvariant under the host's enumeration order of every set, map and\ncensus (proof in the numbering note, §3); the procedure's one use of host\norder, entering components of equal encoding in the order listed, cannot\nchange the rows (lemma C), and a receiver never decides validity by its\nown enumeration order. This is a lower-side structural refusal, not a\nlift kind. A receiver whose work bound (§10.10) is exceeded before the\nprocedure completes refuses with the structured resource refusal before\nany attachment, never labels the state `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`, and\nnever accepts an unfinished check [D5 variant: ; a receiver that reaches\nD5 refuses `:yin.k/undecodable` kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`].\"\n\n**A3 — carrier §5 preamble.** Replace \"The class-erased skeletons of §6\nare used only inside the numbering procedure and never decide the order\nof an emitted row\" with \"The spellings (content encodings) of §6 are\nused only inside the numbering procedure and never decide the order of\nan emitted row\".\n\n**A4 — carrier §8 step 6.** \"Recompute §6 under the work bound, for the\nroot and every install child, and compare (`:yin.k/undecodable`, kind\n`:noncanonical-cursor-cells`; or the resource refusal of §10.10 if the\nbound is exceeded, which propagates untranslated).\" D5 variant: add\n\"`:yin.k/undecodable` kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells` if D5 is reached\nin the root or in a child, translated from the recomputation's lift-side\nrefusal of that kind only, its path prefixed by the carried root id\";\nand in the diagnostics paragraph after step 12 add\n`:unresolved-cursor-cells` to the `:yin.k/undecodable` kinds, change\n\"the two kinds this carrier adds to UCF §7.5.4's set: `:byte-string` and\n`:metadata`\" to \"the three kinds …, `:metadata` and\n`:unresolved-cursor-cells`\", and in §11 \"two lift kinds and two\n`:yin.k/undecodable` kinds are added\" to \"three … and three …\". With D6\nthese counts are unchanged.\n\n**A5 — carrier §10.** Answer 9: \"a renaming-invariant\nrefinement-and-search procedure\" → \"the discovery-order procedure, which\nis invariant under renaming and host enumeration\". Answer 10: \"a bound\non decoded bytes, rows, cursor cells, traversal depth, refinement work\nand search nodes for §6\" → \"a bound on decoded bytes, rows, cursor cells,\ntraversal depth, traversal-and-spelling work and (D6) residue-search\ntrials for §6 (its `:work` unit counts positions and copied bytes, so\nspellings are paid for before they are allocated)\"; and \"never falls\nback to encounter order\" → \"never substitutes host enumeration order for\nthe canonical traversal\". Proposals list: \"as defined by its procedure\"\n→ \"as defined by discovery order [D5 variant: , and the D5 refusal\nclass]\".\n\n**A6 — carrier §3, identity rows.** After \"minted by the lift under §6's\ncanonical numbering\" add \"(the n-th cursor cell met in §6's canonical\ntraversal)\".\n\n**A7 — continuation §2.2.** Replace \"numbered by the draft's §6\nprocedure\" with \"numbered by discovery order (draft §6 as amended by\n`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md`)\".\n\n**A8 — continuation §5.5.** Three replacements. (i) \"Draft §6 is\ninvariant under renaming of the **cursor cells** (its labels never encode\nan incoming cursor-cell id), and the only other identity it consumes is\nthe stream marker in each cursor cell's initial label\n(`[:body <stream-marker-bytes> <position-bytes>]`, draft §6 step 1) and\nin cursor-cell bodies\" → \"Draft §6 is invariant under renaming of the\n**cursor cells** (it reads no incoming cursor-cell id), and the only\nother identity it consumes is the stream marker in each cursor cell's\nbody label (`[:body [:stream identity descriptor] position]`, draft §6\nas amended, spelled by rank inside keys) and in cursor-cell bodies\".\n(ii) \"Unordered containers (`:set`, `:map`, `:store`, `:bindings`, the\nparked table) are encoded by sorting their elements' encodings bytewise\n(draft §6 step 2), never by host traversal order\" → \"Guest sets and maps\nare entered in the bytewise order of their elements' spellings, and\n`:store`, `:bindings` and the parked table in the byte order of their\nstructural keys (draft §6 D1), never in host enumeration order\". (iii)\nLeave \"never derived from traversal order\" but qualify it: \"never\nderived from *host* traversal order (the canonical traversal of draft §6\nD1 is a function of the state and is what the procedure reads)\".\n\n**A9 — continuation §8.1 acceptance.** Item 10 stands; append items 12–22\nof §9 below as the acceptance of the rewritten numbering.\n\n**A10 — UCF §7.5.3.** No change: \"minted by the emitter (stable within\none lift; remapped on lowering) and not content-addressed\" remains true.\n\n## 5. The alternative: portable ids minted in the VM\n\nThe owner asked whether a cursor cell could carry a portable id from\ncreation so the carrier sends it as is. To work across engines the id\nmust be computable by every engine from shared inputs. The candidates\n(branch `srvm-phase6a`, `src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc`) are: the task's id\ncounter (`gen-id`, `:227-230`; `gensym`, `:2312-2318`, which lower adopts\nas `max(local, carried)`), the creation sequence of `:stream/cursor`\nexecutions (`handle-cursor-unminted`'s `seq-n`, `:557-568`, minted at the\n`:stream/cursor` effect, `:2595-2602`, with the id from `gensym` and the\nsealed reference from `issue-ref`, `:311-323`), a hash of (occurrence,\ncounter), or a random id. Every one of them names *history*, not\nstructure:\n\n- *Same structure, same hash fails.* Hold every carried field equal:\n  task A executes `:stream/cursor` three times (ids `cursor-0`,\n  `cursor-1`, `cursor-2`; counter 3) and drops the second; task B executes\n  `:stream/cursor` twice and then consumes one unrelated fresh name\n  (`gensym` for a non-cursor id; counter 3) and keeps both. The two\n  snapshots are structurally equal (two cursor cells with equal bodies at\n  equal positions, the same id counter, the same everything else), yet A\n  carries `{cursor-0, cursor-2}` and B `{cursor-0, cursor-1}`, so the root\n  ids differ. Any id recomputed from the snapshot's *structure* instead\n  is the canonicalization problem of §2 again, not an alternative to it.\n- *The cross-engine harness law* would hold only if both engines agree\n  on the minting history, which makes an execution trace part of the\n  frame's identity; a random or occurrence-bound id breaks it outright\n  (a fork halt has no occurrence; a child has none of its own).\n- *The VM grows a second name.* Lower mints a fresh private resource\n  entry per cursor cell (carrier §8 step 12) and must keep the carried\n  id beside it; a relift after further execution must mint ids that\n  cannot collide with carried ones, which needs an (origin, counter)\n  pair: a global naming scheme for a thing that only needs a name\n  relative to its state.\n\nDiscovery order answers the owner's observation differently: the\nidentity stays local and the carrier gives it a *structural* name, the\nn-th cursor cell met, exactly as a de Bruijn index names a binder by\nposition rather than by a global symbol. Recommend against portable\nids for immutable creation-history ids carried unchanged; that is the\nonly kind proposed.\n\n## 6. Prototype\n\n`collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj` (not repo code; a\nscratch project under `collab/`, never committed) implements D1–D6 as\none schema-driven run procedure (root run, key runs, component runs and\ntrials share the code; a local run differs only in its number tag, in\nspelling residues as `[:tie]` and, for a trial, in its forced element),\nmemoized as §2 describes, charging `:work` and `:search` as §2 \"Work\"\ndescribes, with the totality assertion, an emitter that numbers install\nchildren by the same procedure, a decode path that runs 6a's `decode*`\nwith its numbering check off and then wraps the whole discovery\nrecomputation (root and children) in the narrow translation, and two\nswitches: `:residue-search` (D6; off is the D5 variant) and\n`:permissive` (diagnostic: a conflict is resolved by one host-first\ntrial, to test whether a refused state is in fact interchangeable). Run\nfrom the probe directory (JVM only; `@#'rows/…` reaches 6a's private\nhelpers); the `clojure` launcher was not available to this session, so\nthe run below used the launcher's cached classpath directly:\n\n```\njava @java.args clojure.main -e \"(require 'probe.discovery) (probe.discovery/-main)\"\n```\n\nResults of 2026-10-12, round 3 (full log `collab/numbering-probe/run.log`;\n6a's own tests pass unchanged in the same project):\n\n| State | Discovery order (D5 variant) | With D6 | 6a |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `fixture :s1 :s2 :x :y` (`rows_test.cljc:28`) | `x cc-1, y cc-2`, root `fab223…` | same | same root |\n| review counterexample `'a #{X Y}`, `'b X` | `x cc-1, y cc-2`, root `a3713a5e…` | same | root `2d9873a8…` |\n| symmetric pair `#{X Y}` | component rule, root `07eda35d…` | same | same root |\n| `#{[X X] [Y Y]}` / `{X 1, Y 1}` | roots `da69c679…` / `49d4478c…`; equal after reversal and after renaming plus shuffling | same | same roots |\n| `[x x y]` / `[x y y]` (`rows_test.cljc:159`) | roots `4993b5b1…` / `536c810b…` | same | `71543fe2…` / same |\n| **review A** `#{[X X] [Y Z]}` | `X cc-1, Y cc-2, Z cc-3` for both enumerations, root `65ba4530…` (the review's digest) | same | `387f294a…` |\n| **review B** `{X X, Y Z}` | `X cc-1, Y cc-2, Z cc-3` for both enumerations, root `409548a7…` (the review's digest) | same | `be62ba43…` |\n| **review C** `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}` | total numbering, inner set listed first gets `cc-1, cc-2`; root `18b70de6…` for the state and for its renaming | same | same root |\n| `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` | numbered (two components, each one element per group), root `92747c0f…` | same | same root |\n| `[#{X Y} X]` | `X cc-1, Y cc-2`, root `7dc3d5c4…` | same | `a850fce6…` |\n| `[#{#{X Y} #{Z W}} X]` | `X cc-1, Y cc-2, Z cc-3, W cc-4`, root `6ea03f9b…` | same | `e61d3709…` |\n| `#{[#{X Y} #{X Y}] [#{P Q} #{R S}]}` | keys differ (two cursor cells against four); root `b35b8e12…` | same | refused `[:label-bytes]` |\n| **review r2 witness** `[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]` | key run conflicts, root run numbers `A cc-1, B cc-2`; root `9600fe01…` (the review's digest); 1 root over 24 renamings plus shuffles; decodes | same | `01933121…` |\n| loop plus 2-cycle (`rows_test.cljc:312`) | `L cc-1`, then refused `:unresolved-cursor-cells`, path `[15]`, cells `[A B]` | `L cc-1, A cc-2, B cc-3`, 2 trials, root `858a891e…`, 1 root over 24 | root `55ff168e…` (`L cc-3`) |\n| 2-cycle `#{[A B] [B A]}` | refused; permissive mode gives 1 root over 24 shuffles (interchangeable, not certified) | `A cc-1, B cc-2`, 2 trials, root `0fe898cc…` **= 6a's root** | numbered |\n| 3-cycle `#{[A B] [B C] [C A]}` | refused; permissive 1 root | 3 trials, root `0b181cd4…` **= 6a's root** | numbered |\n| path `#{[A B] [B C]}` | refused; permissive mode gives 2 roots (not interchangeable) | 2 trials, root `726a9fbb…`, 1 root over 24 | `76117652…` |\n| seven-cell triangle plus square (`:392`) | refused; permissive 1 root over 24 (the 4 of round 2 came from listed-order entry) | 11 trials, 488 `:work`, root `d76d787a…`, 1 root over 24; decode through 6a's `decode*` stops at 6a's own `[:label-bytes]` (probe limitation) | refused `[:label-bytes]` |\n| ten cells in a vector (`:331`) | `cc-1 … cc-10` in vector order | same | same root |\n| frame fixture: wait window `x`, K-frame window `y`, environment `y`, parked K-frame window `#{x y}` | `y cc-1` (free environment, slot 5), `x cc-2` (parked table, slot 8), `:test/cursor cc-3`; root `0b9ef61e…`; decodes | same | `fd3513dd…` |\n| frame fixture: wait window `#{x y}`, parked K-frame window `y` | `y cc-1`, `x cc-2`, `:test/cursor cc-3`; root `ff124bfc…`; decodes | same | `e1558410…` |\n| parent with two installs sharing the review-counterexample child | child `X cc-1, Y cc-2`; one child row shared; root `9544fcd7…`; decodes | same | — |\n| **install child holding the 2-cycle** | lift refuses path `[12 a 15]`; a carrier of it built with `emit-numbered` decodes to `:yin.k/undecodable :unresolved-cursor-cells`, path `[<root> 12 a 15]`, cells `[cc-1 cc-2]` | numbers, root `734c33f3…`, decodes | — |\n| budgets | loop plus 2-cycle under `{:work 1}`: `:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit [:work]`; a valid carrier decoded under `{:work 1}` propagates the same | 3-cycle under `{:search 1}`: `:resource-limit [:search]` | — |\n| key bytes, 4 cells over 2 bodies in `#{#{X Y} #{Z W} #{X Z}}` | label spelling: 4364 bytes at a 128-character descriptor, 920118 at 65536; **rank spelling: 1396 at both**; 13 local runs, 78 `:work`, same root either spelling | — | — |\n| 2000 random value states | 1975 numbered, 25 refused (14 witnessed non-interchangeable by 12 shuffles, 11 not distinguished), **0 invariance failures**, 1783 decode round trips ok, 0 decode failures, 192 not decodable through 6a's own recompute budget (probe limitation); max `:work` 1254 | **2000 numbered, 0 refused**, 0 invariance failures, 1808 decoded, 0 failures, 192 at 6a's budget; max trials 13, max `:work` 1470 | — |\n| 200 frame-bearing states | 192 numbered, 8 refused (5 witnessed, 3 not distinguished), 0 failures, 175 decoded, 17 at 6a's budget; max `:work` 767 | 200 numbered, 0 refused, 0 failures, 183 decoded, max trials 5 | — |\n| 100 states with children | 98 numbered, 2 refused (not distinguished), 0 failures, 75 decoded, 23 at 6a's budget; max `:work` 916 | 100 numbered, 0 refused, 0 failures, 76 decoded, max trials 4 | — |\n\nHand derivations, verified against the codec above (the digest comes\nfrom `emit-numbered` under the hand-derived numbering; where 6a or the\nreview reached the same numbering independently the digest agrees).\nFixture `:s1 :s2 :x :y`: the store's `'payload` map is entered by the\nkeys of its entries; whichever of `'one` (`[:cursor-cell x]`), `'both`\n(`#{x y}`) or `'cursors` (`{x :x, y :y}`) sorts first, `x` is met before\n`y`, since `x` is alone under `'one` and its body (`:s1`) ranks before\n`y`'s (`:s2`) in the set and the map; so `x = cc-1`, `y = cc-2`. Review\ncounterexample: `'a` before `'b`; `#{X Y}` ties (`[u0 r]` twice) and is\ndeferred; `'b` meets `X = cc-1`; the fixpoint re-keys the set, `[:n 1]`\nagainst `[:u 0 r]`, and `Y = cc-2`. Review A: keys `[:vec [u0 r] [u0 r]]`\nand `[:vec [u0 r] [u1 r]]` differ at the second cell's index, so `[X X]`\nis entered first: `X = cc-1`, then `Y = cc-2`, `Z = cc-3`. Review B: the\nentries `[:entry [u0 r] [u0 r]]` and `[:entry [u0 r] [u1 r]]` likewise.\nReview C: both inner sets key as `[:set [u0 r] [u1 r]]` (each key run\nresolves its own `{X, Y}` tie by D4 with two one-element components),\nthe outer tie is deferred, the fixpoint changes nothing, the two\ncomponents are isomorphic, and the one listed first gets `cc-1, cc-2`:\neither order emits `#{#{1 2} #{3 4}}`. `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]`: both sets tie\nand defer; components `{X¹, X²}` and `{Y¹, Y²}` (one element per group,\nequal encodings `[:component 1 [u0 r] 2 [u0 r]]`): `X = cc-1`, `Y = cc-2`\nin host order, same rows either way. `[#{X Y} X]`: the set ties, index 1\nmeets `X = cc-1`, the fixpoint splits the set, `Y = cc-2`. **Witness**\n`[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]`: F4 above; the key run's residue `[:set [:tie K\nK]]` is the outer set's only key, so the outer element is entered, the\ninner tie deferred, `A = cc-1` at index 1, and the fixpoint gives\n`B = cc-2`. Loop plus 2-cycle: `[L L]` keys as `[u0 r] [u0 r]`, the two\nothers as `[u0 r] [u1 r]`, so `L = cc-1` by key order; `{[A B], [B A]}`\nties, the fixpoint changes nothing, and the component `{[A B], [B A]}`\nholds two elements of one group: D5 refuses; **D6** runs two trials\nover `[:component 1 [:set [A B] [B A]]]`, one entering `[A B]` first\n(`A = u0, B = u1`, then `[B A]` keys `[u1 u0]` and is entered), one the\nmirror image; the two spellings are equal, the first is adopted:\n`A = cc-2`, `B = cc-3`. 2-cycle alone: the same two trials from the empty\nnumbering, `A = cc-1`, `B = cc-2`, and 6a's search reaches the same\nrows. 3-cycle: three trials, each a rotation of the others, equal\nspellings, `A, B, C = cc-1, cc-2, cc-3` in the first trial's order, again\n6a's rows. Path `#{[A B] [B C]}`: two trials, `[A B]` first gives\n`[:set [u0 u1] [u1 u2]]`, `[B C]` first gives `[:set [u2 u0] [u0 u1]]`;\nthe least is adopted. The example state of\n`collab/example-suspended-state.clj` has one cursor cell, first met in\nthe act's environment under `c`, so `cc-1`; a one-cell state has the\nsame rows under both procedures.\n\nPins: none of the round-1 or round-2 pins change (`fab223…`, `c4a7c7…`,\n`a3713a5e…`, `07eda35d…`, `da69c679…`, `49d4478c…`, `4993b5b1…`,\n`536c810b…`, `65ba4530…`, `409548a7…`, `18b70de6…`, `92747c0f…`,\n`7dc3d5c4…`, `6ea03f9b…`, `b35b8e12…`, the ten-cell root `6e7c7a6b…`),\nwith the rank spelling and with D6 on. New pins: the witness\n`9600fe01…`; under D6 the loop plus 2-cycle `858a891e…` (6a's `55ff168e…`\nstays removed), the 2-cycle `0fe898cc…` and 3-cycle `0b181cd4…` (6a's\nown roots), the path `726a9fbb…`, the seven-cell state `d76d787a…`.\n\n## 7. Recommendation and cost\n\n**Adopt discovery order with D6** (owner decision: adopt; D6 is this\nnote's recommendation within it). 6b has not started and 6a is\nunmerged, so the cost is one implementation slice of `rows.cljc` and its\ntests (§8) plus the amendments of §4. Size, from the prototype and the\nsecond review's count rather than the round-2 guess: the prototype's\nschema-to-procedure region is about 330 nonblank lines, of which about\n30 are D6, the diagnostic mode, tracing and measurement; in place it\nreuses 6a's `descriptors`, `bytes-key`, `compare-bytes`, `spend!`,\n`refuse!` and `emit-node`, so expect **250–300 added or replacement\nlines and 135–150 deleted** (`check-label-bytes!`, labels, `ce`,\n`positions`, `classes`, `refine`, `search`), **about 100–160 net**,\nexcluding tests; the second review's independent estimate was 100–195\nnet. Review cost: one implementation round and one review round; the\nreview reads §3 (five lemmas, one induction) and §8's cut list rather\nthan re-deriving the design, and the generator of §9 item 18 runs in CI.\nWhat the owner gains over 6a: a definition that can be stated and proved\n(the n-th cell met, with one bounded tie-break), no growing labels, a\nsearch confined to the residue (two trials for a 2-cycle; 6a's search\nover the seven-cell state exceeded a megabyte of labels, D6 numbers it in\n11 trials), and 6a's own roots on the 2-cycle and 3-cycle. Risk: a\ncanonicality bug, mitigated by §9 item 18's generator in CI (it caught\nthe prototype's memo bug), by the decode round trip in that generator,\nand by the proof; and the unproved cost bound, which the `:work` and\n`:search` budgets enforce as 6a's budgets did.\n\n**D5 variant (no search at all).** Same slice minus D6 (about 25 lines),\nplus the `:unresolved-cursor-cells` kind, its decode translation and the\ndocumented refusal class of F4; refuses 2-cycles and 3-cycles that 6a\nnumbers. Acceptable if the owner wants no search in the codec; the\nrefusal can be replaced by D6 later without changing any id numbered\nunder D1–D4 (§3 consequence 6).\n\n**Keep 6a.** Zero rounds now, but 6b, the cross-engine harness and every\nreviewer of a pinned id inherit an exhaustive search over the whole\nstate, labels that grow until the seven-cell state exceeds a megabyte,\nand a definition that is \"whatever the procedure outputs\". Not\nrecommended; the owner has decided against it.\n\n**Portable ids.** Changes the VM, the census, the laws and the harness\n(§5); three or more rounds across two designs. Against.\n\n## 8. Replacing 6a: what is deleted, rewritten and kept\n\nAgainst branch `srvm-phase6a` (`d77da9f0`), `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc`\n(line numbers of that file) and `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc`. An\nimplementation brief can be cut from this list.\n\n**rows.cljc, deleted.**\n- `check-label-bytes!` (`:212-230`): the label-size bound; nothing grows\n  any more.\n- Inside `canonical-numbering` (`:236-355`): the label seeding\n  (`:252-255`), `ce-slot`/`ce` (`:257-284`), `positions` (`:285-325`),\n  `classes` (`:326-330`), `refine` (`:331-337`), `search` (`:338-354`),\n  and the `checked-encode`/`checked-key`/`checked-sort` wrappers\n  (`:245-251`). The `[:child (checked-encode id)]` spelling (`:273-275`)\n  goes with `ce`.\n- `default-budget` (`:200-202`): the `:label-bytes` key; the `:search`\n  key too in the D5 variant (with D6 it stays and counts trials).\n\n**rows.cljc, rewritten.**\n- `canonical-numbering` (`:236-355`), same signature and contract\n  (`task`, optional `budget` → `{source-id :yin.k/cc-n}`; empty census →\n  `{}`), now the root run of §2: `descriptors` extended by the two\n  synthetic kinds `:entry` and `:component`; body ranks from the census;\n  `enter`/`unordered`/`fixpoint`/`components`/`component-encoding`/\n  `adopt`/`resolve`/`local-run`/`spell` as in the prototype\n  (`collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj`, sections \"runs\" and\n  \"spelling\"), with `spend!` charged as §2 \"Work\" says; the totality\n  assertion before returning; D5 raising `:yin.k/non-portable\n  :unresolved-cursor-cells` (D5 variant) or D6 charging `:search`.\n  Portable `.cljc` per continuation §12: no `@#'` reflection, `mapv`/\n  `into`/`reduce` (not `for`) over sequences that may exceed 32 elements\n  (the prototype's `components` uses `for`; replace), `contains?` for\n  membership, `(assoc {} …)` never `(assoc nil …)`, atoms are fine on all\n  three hosts.\n- `emit-node` (`:181-197`): thread a row path so a child's D5 refusal\n  reports `[12 <install name> …]` (D5 variant only; otherwise unchanged).\n- `decode*` recompute (`:570-575`): wrap the `emit` call (which numbers\n  the root and every child) so that only `:yin.k/non-portable\n  :unresolved-cursor-cells` becomes `:yin.k/undecodable\n  :unresolved-cursor-cells` with the path prefixed by `root`; a\n  `:resource-limit` propagates (D5 variant only; with D6 the recompute\n  is unchanged apart from the budget keys).\n- Namespace docstring (`:1-17`): \"canonical cursor-cell numbering\" →\n  \"discovery-order cursor-cell numbering (design note §2)\".\n\n**rows.cljc, kept unchanged.** `row-table`, `pending-table`, `node`,\n`refuse!`/`bad!`, the marker checks, `bytes-key`/`compare-bytes`/\n`byte-compare`/`sorted-bytes`, `descriptors`, the census order\n(`:137-156`), `emit-slot`/`emit-node` apart from the path, `spend!`\n(`:205-209`), `emit-numbered` (`:358-363`), `emit` (`:366-372`),\n`structural?`, `literal?`, `check-abstract!`, all of `decode*` before\n`:570` and after `:575` (the order numbering → `validate-frames`,\n`:576-578`, stays), `decode`, `canonical-under`, `frame->rows`,\n`rows->frame`, `validate-frames`, and the `:bytes`, `:rows`, `:depth`,\n`:cursor-cells`, `:work` budget keys with their defaults.\n\n**rows_test.cljc, kept as fixtures and tests.** `task-node`,\n`cursor-cell-body`, `fixture` (`:16-37`), `portable-frame` and the\n`bijection` test, `row-ids-are-jing-default`, `replace-row`,\n`window-sorted-and-unique`, `canonical-under-is-renaming-invariant`\n(`:143-156`), `alias-topology-and-census` (`:159-168`),\n`gates-and-graph-before-hashes`, `numbering-precedes-frame-defect`\n(`:206-213`), `schema-and-literal-canonicality`,\n`child-scope-and-shared-child-dag` (`:240-249`),\n`pending-envelope-and-nil-literal-are-preserved`,\n`task-root-checks-carried-code-and-frame`, `reversed` and\n`unordered-containers-are-order-free` (`:285-304`),\n`census-is-numeric-from-ten-cursor-cells` (`:331-338`), the marker tests\n(`:341-381`), `cursor-cell-and-identity-shaped-literal-are-distinct`\n(`:384-389`), and the fixture `seven-cursor-cell-triangle-square`\n(`:392-397`). Every counterexample of §6 becomes a fixture (items 12–16\nbelow), including the refused ones.\n\n**rows_test.cljc, rewritten.**\n- `refinement-search-on-symmetric-cursor-cells-is-bounded` (`:171-178`)\n  → `symmetric-cursor-cells-number-by-the-component-rule`: same two\n  assertions on equal roots and two numbers; the `{:search 1}`\n  assertions become `{:work 1}` → `:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit`.\n- `canonical-roots-are-pinned` (`:307-328`): `fab223…` and `c4a7c7…`\n  stay; the loop plus 2-cycle block loses the `{:search 2}` assertion and\n  the pin `55ff168e…`; under D6 it pins `858a891e…` with `L = cc-1`\n  (and the renamed task equal); under the D5 variant it asserts the\n  `:unresolved-cursor-cells` refusal with path `[15]` and cells `[A B]`.\n- `intermediate-label-bytes-are-bounded` (`:400-403`) →\n  `seven-cursor-cells-number-by-residue-search` (D6: root `d76d787a…`,\n  11 trials, `{:search 10}` → `:resource-limit [:search]`) or, in the D5\n  variant, `seven-cursor-cells-are-refused` (`:unresolved-cursor-cells`,\n  cells `[a b c d e f g]`).\n- `input-row-bodies-do-not-spend-label-bytes` (`:406-421`) →\n  `large-literals-do-not-spend-work-beyond-their-bytes`, same body: a\n  400000-element literal still emits and decodes under the default\n  budget.\n\n## 9. Acceptance for the rewritten 6a numbering\n\n12. `rows-test/discovery-order-numbering`: for `fixture :s1 :s2 :x :y`,\n    the review counterexample, `[x x y]`, `[x y y]`, `[#{X Y} X]`, the\n    witness `[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]` and the two frame fixtures of §6,\n    `canonical-numbering` returns exactly the maps of §6.\n13. `rows-test/review-counterexample-resolves-by-fixpoint`: `'a #{X Y}`,\n    `'b X` numbers `X cc-1, Y cc-2`; the task renamed and with the set\n    reversed gives the same root; the root is pinned `a3713a5e…`.\n14. `rows-test/component-rule`: `#{X Y}`, `#{[X X] [Y Y]}`, `{X 1, Y 1}`,\n    `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}` and `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` number without refusal;\n    element reversal and renaming give equal roots; pins `07eda35d…`,\n    `da69c679…`, `49d4478c…`, `18b70de6…`, `92747c0f…`.\n15. `rows-test/alias-pattern-is-part-of-the-key`: `#{[X X] [Y Z]}` and\n    `{X X, Y Z}` give one root for both enumerations and after renaming,\n    pinned `65ba4530…` and `409548a7…`; `#{[#{X Y} #{X Y}] [#{P Q} #{R S}]}`\n    numbers and is pinned `b35b8e12…`; the witness `[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]`\n    numbers `A cc-1, B cc-2`, is pinned `9600fe01…` and decodes.\n16. D6: `rows-test/residue-search`: the 2-cycle, the 3-cycle, the path,\n    the loop plus 2-cycle and the seven-cell state number with the pins\n    of §6 (`0fe898cc…`, `0b181cd4…`, `726a9fbb…`, `858a891e…`,\n    `d76d787a…`), renaming plus shuffling gives equal roots, every\n    carrier decodes, and the 3-cycle under `{:search 1}` refuses\n    `:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit [:search]`.\n    D5 variant instead: `rows-test/unresolved-cursor-cells-refuse`: the\n    same five states refuse at `emit` with `:yin.k/non-portable`, kind\n    `:unresolved-cursor-cells`, path `[15]` and the component's sorted\n    cursor cells; a carrier of each built with `emit-numbered` decodes to\n    `:yin.k/undecodable :unresolved-cursor-cells` with the path prefixed\n    by the root, never `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`; a parent whose\n    install `'a` holds the 2-cycle refuses at `emit` with path\n    `[12 a 15]` and its `emit-numbered` carrier decodes to the\n    `:yin.k/undecodable` kind with path `[<root> 12 a 15]`.\n17. `rows-test/canonical-roots-are-pinned`: `fab223…` and `c4a7c7…`\n    unchanged; `a3713a5e…`, `4993b5b1…`, `536c810b…`, `7dc3d5c4…`,\n    `6ea03f9b…`, `9600fe01…` added; `55ff168e…` removed.\n18. `rows-test/counterexample-search`: a seeded generator (one to six\n    cursor cells over two bodies; nested `:vec`, `:set`, `:map` with\n    cursor-cell keys, sets of cursor-cell pairs including `[X X]`, sets of\n    sets, under keyed bindings and the result; unique elements and keys\n    under decoded equality) over at least 2000 states, plus at least 200\n    frame-bearing states (cursor cells in an act window, a K-frame\n    window, environments matched to the free environment, and a parked\n    state at a park site) and at least 100 states with install children:\n    every numbered state's carrier decodes and re-emits to its root, and\n    renaming every cursor cell plus shuffling every set, map and census\n    gives an equal root id; refusals are counted and reported (0 under\n    D6); the largest `:work` and `:search` charged are reported.\n19. `rows-test/numbering-is-total`: a task whose census declares a\n    cursor cell the traversal does not reach raises the totality defect\n    (not a refusal) before any row is emitted.\n20. `numbering-precedes-frame-defect`, `alias-topology-and-census`,\n    `canonical-under-is-renaming-invariant`,\n    `unordered-containers-are-order-free`, `child-scope-and-shared-child-dag`\n    and `census-is-numeric-from-ten-cursor-cells` pass unchanged; the\n    rewritten tests of §8 pass.\n21. `rows-test/work-is-charged-per-byte`: a state whose cursor-cell\n    bodies carry a 65536-character descriptor numbers under the default\n    budget with the same `:work` as under a 128-character descriptor\n    (rank spelling), and `{:work 1}` refuses `:yin.k/unsupported\n    :resource-limit [:work]` at `emit` and, for a valid carrier, at\n    `decode`.\n22. Budgets and docs: `:label-bytes` is gone (and `:search` in the D5\n    variant); A1–A9 applied in the variant chosen; kondo clean;\n    `bb test:sub yin.vm` green on three hosts; Node log shows\n    `Testing yin.vm.ucf.rows-test`.\n\n## 10. Owner questions\n\n1. **D6 or the D5 variant?** Recommended: D6 (a search confined to one\n   conflicting component, measured at 2–13 trials, 6a's roots on the\n   2-cycle and 3-cycle, no structural refusal class to document). The D5\n   variant is the same slice without about 25 lines, with the\n   `:unresolved-cursor-cells` kind and the F4 regression (2-cycles and\n   3-cycles refused) as a documented limitation that D6 can lift later\n   without moving any D1–D4 id.\n2. If the D5 variant: the refusal kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells` as\n   proposed, or the orchestrator's `:resource-limit` (recommended\n   against: it is not a budget, and carrier §10.10 says a resource\n   refusal never stands in for a canonicality verdict)?\n"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JsNijEWWzFyCjoUMnYG9vn","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-numbering/docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7984b61f-639f-4af2-b00a-5b9b7d554f75","uuid":"af30835b-7f30-43b5-b6eb-bda73eb8ddd4","timestamp":"2026-10-12T06:44:05.301Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"update","filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-numbering/docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md","content":"# Cursor-cell numbering by discovery order (companion to the row carrier)\n\nStatus: **Adopted by the owner 2026-10-12 (replaces carrier §6's\nrefinement and search); not yet implemented.** Written 2026-10-12\n(Architect, claude-fable-5-1) for the owner's observation that \"the\nproblem is not that the cursor cell has identity, but that the identity\nis local\", and the orchestrator's proposal to number cursor cells by\n**discovery order** in a canonical traversal. Revised twice the same day\nafter independent reviews (gpt-6.1-sol,\n`collab/1792140000000-…findings.md` and `collab/1792170000000-…findings.md`).\nRound 2 repaired the orbit lemma (`#{[X X] [Y Z]}`) and nested tie groups\n(`#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}`). Round 3 (this text) fixes what the second review\nfound: which local conflicts refuse (§2 D5, witness\n`[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]`), the install-child refusal escaping decode, the\nwork bound (now an explicit budget, with body labels spelled by rank so\nkey bytes no longer grow with a stream descriptor), the A2 wording, the\nsize estimate, and the coverage regression against 6a (2-cycles and\n3-cycles), for which §2 D6 specifies and §6 measures the repair. §8 lists\nwhat an implementation deletes, rewrites and keeps in 6a. Subordinate to\n[`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md)\n(the carrier, body version 3) and\n[`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md`](./yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md);\nvocabulary is UCF §7.5.3 (\"Terminology: cursor, cursor cell, cursor-ref,\ncell\") exactly. Evidence is the carrier draft §5/§6 tables, slice 6a's\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc` and `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc`\n(branch `srvm-phase6a` at `d77da9f0`), and a prototype run against that\ncode (§6). Nothing here is implemented.\n\n**In one line:** number cursor cells in the order a canonical traversal\nmeets them (D1–D2); order the elements of a guest set or map by a key\nthat is the spelling of a speculative local run over the element (so\nalias patterns such as `[X X]` versus `[Y Z]` are part of the key);\ndefer equal keys and re-examine them to a fixpoint (D3); at a fixpoint,\nlink tied elements that share an unnumbered cursor cell into components\nand enter the component of least complete encoding (D4); a component\nholding two equal-keyed elements of one container is the one case this\ncannot certify: refuse it (D5), or resolve it by a bounded residue search\nover that component alone (D6, recommended). Proof in §3, budget in §2\n\"Work\", results in §6, the cut against 6a in §8.\n\n## 0. Vocabulary used below\n\n- **Canonical traversal order** is the order D1 defines: a function of\n  the abstract task alone. **Host enumeration order** (also \"listed\n  order\") is the order in which a host happens to list the elements of a\n  guest set or the entries of a guest map, or the census; it is an\n  accident of the host. The procedure reads it in exactly one place, D4's\n  entry of components of equal encoding, where lemma C shows it cannot\n  change the result.\n- An **element** is a `:set` element or a `:map` entry `[key value]`\n  (the key is a guest value and may be a cursor cell).\n- A **numbering** `N` is a partial map from the task's cursor cells to\n  positive integers, injective, assigned in increasing order; `|N|` is\n  its size. The emitted id of a cell numbered `k` is `:yin.k/cc-k`.\n- A **run** is one execution of the traversal-and-resolution procedure\n  over one item (a row or a value) from a starting numbering. The **root\n  run** is the run over the `:task` row from the empty numbering; its\n  numbers are the output. A **local run** is a run over one item from\n  the enclosing run's current numbering. There are three kinds of local\n  run, and the distinction decides what refuses (D5):\n  - a **key run** is a local run over one element, made to compute the\n    element's sort key; it is *speculative*: it never refuses, and\n    whatever it cannot resolve (a tie, a conflict) is spelled in its\n    key as `[:tie …]`;\n  - a **component run** is a local run over a component's synthetic\n    `[:component …]` row (D4); it must be *complete*, else D5;\n  - a **trial** (D6 only) is a component run that enters one chosen\n    element before its siblings.\n- A **body label** is `[:body [:stream identity descriptor] position]`,\n  a cursor cell's census body as plain data; its **rank** is its index\n  among the census's distinct body labels in bytewise order.\n\n## 1. Findings\n\n**F1. Every non-guest position is ordered.** From the carrier §5 tables\n(and `rows/row-table`, `rows.cljc:37-57`), the positions that can hold a\ncursor-ref (`[:cursor-cell C]`) or a bare `C` slot, and their order:\n\n| Position | Order | Canonical index |\n|---|---|---|\n| `:task` slots 5 `free-env`, 6 `store`, 8 `parked`, 15 `result`; `:wait` state, pending; `:act` env, window, k; `:tail` k; `:kframe` env, window; `:reified` act; `:env` and `:closure` bindings; `:module-store` store; `:pending` `cursor-cell-C`, `response-cursor-cell-C`, `value-V`, `envelope-V` | fixed slot | slot index |\n| `:task` 7 `module-stores`, 12 `installs` | keyed by `M`, by module name | Jing bytes of the key; the child is a task boundary |\n| `:task` 13 `frames`; `:k`; `:vec`, `:list` elements | ordered vector | position `i` |\n| `:window` | keyed by register `N` | `N` ascending, numerically (carrier §5.2) |\n| `:bindings` (environments, closures, the free environment) | keyed by symbol `S` | Jing canonical bytes of `S` (§5 preamble: \"sorted\") |\n| `:store`, `:parked-table` | keyed by `D`, by `P` | bytes of the key; a parked-table value is a structural `:act`/`:tail` reference, entered like any row |\n| `:set` elements | **unordered** | none |\n| `:map` entries | **unordered** (keys are `V`) | none |\n| `:cursor-cells` census | not a position (carrier §6 step 2, §8 step 3) | — |\n\nSo the only unordered positions are guest `:set` elements and guest\n`:map` entries. Both reviews confirmed the inventory complete. The\nexample state in `collab/example-suspended-state.clj` holds its one\ncursor cell (`:cursor-1`) in three ordered positions: the act's\nenvironment under `c`, the window under register 2, and the `:next`\npending's `cursor-cell-C` slot.\n\n**F2. Discovery order subsumes the positions step.** The carrier's §6\nstep 3 (positions) exists to let a cursor cell's number depend on where\nit occurs. A canonical traversal (D1) visits every ordered position in a\nfixed order, so \"the n-th cursor cell met\" already encodes the first\nposition of each cursor cell; later positions cannot change an assigned\nnumber and need not be collected. Refinement (step 4) and search (step 5)\nexisted only to turn position multisets into a total order; discovery\norder is a total order by construction.\n\n**F3. Unordered containers are ordered by a content encoding that\ncarries the alias pattern.** The first draft spelled an unnumbered cursor\ncell by its body alone, so `[X X]` and `[Y Z]` encoded alike. The\nrepaired encoding (§2 \"Spelling\") numbers the cursor cells *inside* an\nelement provisionally, in the element's own discovery order, relative to\nthe enclosing numbering: `[X X]` spells `[(u0) (u0)]`, `[Y Z]` spells\n`[(u0) (u1)]`, so alias topology inside an element is part of its sort\nkey, as a de Bruijn index is part of a term. The key of an element is the\nspelling produced by a *key run* over it, which applies the same rules\nrecursively (nested containers sorted, nested ties deferred and\nre-examined, D4 inside) and spells what it cannot resolve as `[:tie …]`.\nEqual keys form a **tie group**, deferred to the run's worklist and\nre-examined to a fixpoint (D3) as other positions assign numbers. A\nprovisional cell is spelled with its body *rank*, not its body label, so\na key's size depends on the element's structure and not on the length\nof a stream descriptor (§2 \"Work\").\n\n**F4. What refuses, and what is lost against 6a.** What survives a run's\nfixpoint is a worklist of tie groups whose unnumbered cursor cells occur\nonly inside tied elements. Link two tied elements when they share an\nunnumbered cursor cell; a connected set of tied elements is a\n**component**. Two cases:\n- *Certified*: no component holds two elements of one group. Every\n  component is then encoded by a component run over its elements in\n  group order (so a component spanning two groups, `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]`,\n  spells its shared cell once with one index), components are sorted by\n  that encoding, and the least run of equal encodings is entered in host\n  order. Equal encodings here mean *isomorphic* components (§3, lemma C).\n  `#{X Y}`, `#{[X X] [Y Y]}`, `{X 1, Y 1}`, `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}` and\n  `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` are this case.\n- *Conflict*: some component holds two elements of one tie group. Then\n  entering either element first is a choice D1–D4 cannot show to be\n  harmless. **Which runs refuse.** Only the *root run* and *component\n  runs* refuse on a conflict (D5). A *key run* never refuses: it is a\n  speculation made to sort, its unresolved residue is spelled `[:tie …]`\n  in the key, and the enclosing run may resolve the same elements later.\n  The second review's witness is exactly this: in `[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]`\n  the key run over the inner set reaches a conflict (`[A B]` and `[B A]`\n  tie and share both cells), the outer set has one element and is\n  entered, the inner tie is deferred to the root worklist, index 1 meets\n  `A = cc-1`, and the root fixpoint separates `[A B]` (`[(n 1) (u0)]`)\n  from `[B A]` (`[(u0) (n 1)]`), so `B = cc-2`; the lift numbers and the\n  receiver recomputes the same root (`9600fe01…`, the review's digest).\n  The earlier text's \"at any fixpoint of any run\" was wrong and is\n  replaced in D5, lemma D and A1.\n  The predicate, in one sentence: *a state is refused under D5 iff the\n  root run, or a component run it requires, reaches a fixpoint at which\n  two elements of one guest set or map have equal keys and are connected\n  by a chain of shared unnumbered cursor cells through tied elements.*\n\n  **Exactly which structures that is.** A D5 state needs two or more\n  cursor cells with *equal bodies* (same stream identity and descriptor,\n  same position) that are held *only* through guest sets or maps, in\n  elements that each mention more than one of them, with equal keys. The\n  smallest are the directed 2-cycle `#{[A B] [B A]}` and the directed\n  3-cycle `#{[A B] [B C] [C A]}` (both interchangeable: the swap and the\n  rotation are automorphisms, so any entering order gives the same rows,\n  and 6a numbers them); the path `#{[A B] [B C]}` and the seven-cell\n  triangle plus square (`rows_test.cljc:392-404`) are refused and\n  genuinely need a tie-break (§6: two and four distinct roots under\n  host-order entry); the loop plus 2-cycle `#{[L L] [A B] [B A]}`\n  (`rows_test.cljc:312-329`) numbers `L = cc-1` by its key and conflicts\n  on `{A, B}`. One register, environment binding, pending slot or vector\n  position holding any one of the cells breaks the tie for the whole\n  component (`[#{X Y} X]`, the witness above). **Are they common in\n  realistic suspended tasks?** Unmeasured: the repository has no\n  version-3 task corpus (the Stage E scenarios and the v2 handoff census\n  tests hold cursor cells but no v3 lift exists before 6b). The structural\n  argument is that a cursor cell is created into a register\n  (`:stream/cursor` delivers to `rd`, engine `:2595-2602`) and reaches a\n  guest set only by guest code that builds a set of tuples of cursors on\n  one stream at one position and then drops every other reference; of\n  the repository's own fixtures only the two built for the purpose hit\n  D5, and under a tie-biased generator the rate is 35 of 2300 (§6).\n  Because the rate is unmeasured and 6a numbers the 2-cycle and 3-cycle,\n  this note does not leave the regression as a documented limitation:\n  **D6** (§2) is the minimum extra rule that repairs it, a search over\n  the conflicting component only (two trials for a 2-cycle, three for a\n  3-cycle, eleven for the seven-cell state), prototyped and measured in\n  §6: with D6 every one of the 2300 generated states numbers, the 2-cycle\n  and 3-cycle reach 6a's exact roots, and no D1–D4 numbering changes.\n  The owner's choice between D5 (refuse, no search anywhere) and D6\n  (bounded residue search) is §10 question 1; this note recommends D6.\n\n**F5. The scheme is canonical.** Proved in §3: the output (rows or\nrefusal) is a function of the abstract task up to isomorphism (renaming\nof cursor cells, permutation of every unordered container and of the\ncensus) and terminating; the work is accounted by the budget of §2\n\"Work\".\n\n**F6. Prototype evidence (§6).** 2000 random value states (one to six\ncursor cells over two bodies, so ties are common; nested vectors, sets,\nmaps with cursor-cell keys, sets of cursor-cell pairs including aliases\nsuch as `[X X]`, sets of sets, cells aliased across containers), 200\nframe-bearing states (act window, K-frame window, environments, a parked\nstate) and 100 states with install children, each compared with itself\nrenamed and shuffled and decoded through 6a's decoder: 0 invariance\nfailures and 0 decode failures in either mode; D5 refuses 35 of 2300, D6\nrefuses none; the largest `:work` charged is 1470 of 6a's default\n200000 and the largest trial count 13 of 10000. Every pin of rounds 1\nand 2 is unchanged (including under the rank spelling and with D6 on);\nthe second review's digest for the witness is reproduced; the key bytes\nof one state under a 65536-character descriptor fell from 920118 (label\nspelling) to 1396 (rank spelling), the same as under a 128-character\ndescriptor.\n\n**F7. Portable ids minted in the VM are the wrong fix.** §5.\n\n## 2. The rule (replaces carrier §6 \"The procedure\")\n\nInput: the abstract task of carrier §6 (the §5 structure with cursor\ncells as identities, before numbering). Output: a bijection from the\ntask's reached cursor cells to `:yin.k/cc-1 … :yin.k/cc-n`, or a\nrefusal. Install children are numbered as their own tasks by the same\nrule (task boundary, unchanged), in any order, since scopes are disjoint.\n\n**Spelling (the content encoding `CE`).** A run spells the item it has\ntraversed under its final numbering. The root run spells a cursor cell\nnumbered `k` as `[:cursor-cell [:n k]]`, `k` an exact integer; a local\nrun spells a cursor cell it numbered itself (index `j`, counted from the\nenclosing run's local counter) as `[:cursor-cell [:u j r]]`, `r` the\ncell's body rank (an exact integer; rank order equals body-label order,\nso no comparison and no numbering changes against spelling the label,\nand the key's size no longer depends on the descriptor), and a cursor\ncell numbered by an enclosing run as that run spelled it. Nothing else\nchanges from carrier §6 step 2: `[:lit d]` as itself, `[:row B]`\nexpanded in place, structural data as itself, the census as\n`[:cursor-cells]`, an install child as `[:child]` (replacing step 2's\n`[:child <child root id>]`: a child sits only under a name-keyed install\nentry, never inside an unordered container, so its content never decides\nan order, and the encoding stays hash-free). Keyed structural rows are\nspelled in their canonical key order, `:window` in numeric register\norder; a `:set` or `:map` is spelled as the bytewise sorted vector of its\nelements' spellings (an entry as `[:entry K V]`), duplicates retained,\nwhere a tie group the run could not resolve is spelled in place as\n`[:tie K … K]` (its common key, once per element). All comparisons are\nbytewise on canonical CBOR of these synthetic vectors. `CE` is hash-free.\n\n**D1. Traversal.** Depth-first, pre-order, from the run's root item. A\nrow's slots are visited in table order. Ordered repeated slots\n(`frames`, `:vec`, `:list`, `:k`) in order. Keyed rows with structural\nkeys (`:bindings`, `:store`, `:parked-table`, the root's `installs` and\n`module-stores`) in the Jing canonical byte order of the key, the order\n§5 emits; `:window` in ascending numeric register order as the carrier\nspecifies. The census is not entered; an install child's `:task` row is\nnot entered. A `:set`'s elements and a `:map`'s entries are each given a\n**key**: the spelling of a key run over the element under the numbering\nat the moment the container is entered (every element keyed from that\none numbering). Elements are then taken in bytewise key order: a run of\nequal keys is a **tie group**, appended to the run's worklist (deferral\norder is canonical: containers in traversal order, groups within one\ncontainer in key order); every other element is entered in key order,\nan entry's key before its value.\n\n**D2. Meeting.** When the traversal reaches an identity position (a\n`[:cursor-cell C]` value or a `C` slot of a `:pending` row) whose cursor\ncell has no number, the cursor cell receives `|N| + 1` (the root run\ncounts from 1; a local run counts its own cells from the enclosing run's\nlocal counter, so a nested local run never reuses an index of its\nenclosing run). An already numbered cursor cell is passed over. There is\nno first-occurrence spelling: every occurrence emits\n`[:cursor-cell :yin.k/cc-n]` and the census row (carrier §3) stays the\ncontents table.\n\n**D3. Fixpoint.** After the traversal, the worklist's groups are\nre-examined in deferral order: each group's elements are re-keyed under\nthe current numbering and taken as in D1 (singletons entered, equal runs\nre-deferred in order). Repeat while a round numbers at least one cursor\ncell and groups remain. Entering an element inside a group runs D1 on\nit, so a group discovered *inside* an entered element (`#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}`)\njoins the same worklist and is re-examined in the next round; there is\none worklist per run, and the root run's worklist collects deferred\ngroups from every depth.\n\n**D4. Components.** When a fixpoint is reached with groups remaining,\nlink two tied elements (of any groups) that share an unnumbered cursor\ncell; a connected set of tied elements is a **component**. (At a\nfixpoint every unnumbered cursor cell occurs only inside tied elements,\nso all of its occurrences lie in one component.) Encode each component\nby a component run over the synthetic ordered row\n`[:component g₁ i₁ g₂ i₂ …]` in group order, `gᵢ` the group's deferral\nsequence number (a datum) and `iᵢ` the component's one element of that\ngroup, or, when the component holds several elements of group `gᵢ`, a\nsynthetic `:set` of them (the D5/D6 case). A component run must be\ncomplete (no `[:tie]` in its spelling); it is incomplete only when it\nreaches a conflict of its own, which D5 or D6 decides. Sort the\ncomponents by their encodings and **enter the run of least encoding**:\neach of its components has its component run's cursor cells numbered,\nin that run's local-index order, continuing the enclosing run's\nnumbering; components of equal encoding are entered in host order (§3,\nlemma C: equal encodings are isomorphic and the result is the same).\nRemove the entered elements from their groups and return to D3. A group\nwhose elements mention no unnumbered cursor cell holds equal values (the\ncarrier refuses the duplicate at §8 step 5); its elements are entered in\nhost order, which numbers nothing and emits identical rows.\n\n**D5. Conflict, and refusal without D6.** A component **conflicts** when\nit holds two elements of one group. Without D6, a conflict reached by the\nroot run, or by a component run (directly, or through a nested component\nrun of its own), refuses the lift: `:yin.k/non-portable` kind\n`:unresolved-cursor-cells`, `:yin.k/path` the row path of the container\nholding the least conflicting group (for an install child, prefixed\n`[12 <install name> …]`), and `:yin.k/cursor-cells` the sorted unnumbered\ncursor cells of every conflicting component at that fixpoint (so the\ndiagnostic, like the verdict, does not depend on host order). A key run\nnever refuses (§0; F4). A receiver that reaches D5 while recomputing\nrefuses `:yin.k/undecodable` kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`. Neither is\na resource refusal and neither is `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`.\n\n**D6. Residue search (recommended; the repair of F4's regression).** A\nconflict is resolved instead of refused: let `g` be the least conflicting\ngroup of the component and `E` the component's elements of `g`. For each\n`e ∈ E` run a **trial**: a component run over the same `[:component …]`\nrow in which `e` is entered first when its container is keyed, before\nits siblings are keyed (so `e`'s cursor cells take the first local\nindices and its siblings are keyed against them), and which runs to\ncompletion by D1–D6. Each trial charges `:search` one unit. The\ncomponent's encoding is the least trial spelling, and entering the\ncomponent adopts that trial's numbering (D4). Equal least spellings are\nisomorphic and either may be adopted (§3, lemma E). With D6 no\nstructural refusal remains: the only refusals of the procedure are the\nresource refusals of \"Work\" below, and `:unresolved-cursor-cells` is not\ndefined. With D6 in force, 6a's `:search` budget key stays and counts\ntrials; without it, the key is deleted.\n\n**Totality.** The root run ends with an empty worklist or a refusal.\nBefore emission the implementation asserts that the numbering is a\nbijection from the census's cursor cells onto `1 … n`; a violation is a\ndefect (an exception naming the missing or extra cursor cells), never a\nrefusal, and never a partially numbered carrier.\n\n**Memoization (implementation note, not part of the definition).** A\nlocal run's result is a function of its item, of the enclosing numbering\nrestricted to the item's cursor cells, of the enclosing local counter and\n(D6) of the forced element; an implementation may memoize on that key and\nmust return the numbering restricted to the item's own cursor cells (the\nprototype's one bug during round 2 was returning the whole numbering of\nanother element's context, which the decode round trip caught). The memo\nholds only spellings that were paid for under `:work`, so it adds no\nunaccounted bytes. No bound on the number of distinct memo contexts is\nclaimed; the memo is an optimization and the budget is the bound.\n\n**Validation and decode.** Unchanged in form: the receiver decodes, runs\nthe root run, re-emits and compares root ids (`:noncanonical-cursor-cells`\non mismatch). In 6a's `decode*` the recomputation is `emit` itself\n(`rows.cljc:570-575`), which numbers the root *and every install child*\n(`emit-node`, `:185-189`); the **whole** of that call is wrapped so that\nonly a lift-side `:yin.k/non-portable` of kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`\n(D5, from the root or from a child) is translated to `:yin.k/undecodable`\nkind `:unresolved-cursor-cells` with `:yin.k/path` prefixed by the\ncarried root id (a child's path then reads `[<root id> 12 <name> …]`);\na resource refusal (`:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit`) propagates as\nitself; no other non-portable kind can arise there, because step 5 has\nalready checked literals and markers. The second review's escape (an\ninstall child's D5 leaving `decode*` as `:yin.k/non-portable` with path\n`[:child 15]`) came from translating only the root's numbering; the\nprototype now wraps the whole recomputation and the case is in §6. The\nvalidator order stays: step 6 (numbering) after step 5 and before\n`validate-frames` (`rows.cljc:576-578`; test\n`numbering-precedes-frame-defect`, `rows_test.cljc:206`). With D6 the\ntranslation has nothing to translate and is omitted.\n\n**Work.** No polynomial bound is claimed. The second review showed the\nround-2 bound counted positions but not bytes (a 65536-character stream\ndescriptor multiplied key bytes by 170 at fixed `P`, `d`, `n`, `m`) and\nthat memoization's benefit across differing local contexts is unproved.\nThe procedure is instead *accounted*, as 6a already accounts refinement:\nthe `:work` budget is charged one unit per position visited (D1, D3,\nevery local run) and one unit per position spelled plus one unit per\nbyte of literal or structural datum copied into a spelling, before the\ncopy; `:search` (D6) is charged one unit per trial; `:depth` bounds the\nstructural depth as today, `:cursor-cells` bounds `n`, `:rows` the\ncarried rows and `:bytes` the carried bytes. Exceeding any budget is the\nresource refusal of carrier §10.10 (`:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit\n[<key>]`), never a canonicality verdict, exactly as 6a's `:label-bytes`\nwas. The budget charges every allocated spelling byte before allocation,\nso the bytes materialized at any time are bounded by `:work` plus the\ncarried bytes. What the rank spelling guarantees is narrower and exact:\na key's bytes are a function of the element's expanded structure and of\nthe number of distinct bodies, not of the bodies' sizes (§6: 1396 key\nbytes at both 128 and 65536 descriptor characters). The crude bound\nwithout memoization is `O(P · (n + 1)^(d + 1) · log m)` positions and\n`O(B)` bytes per spelling (`P` expanded positions counted per reference,\n`B` expanded bytes, `d` the unordered-container nesting depth, `n` cursor\ncells, `m` the largest container), which the budget enforces rather than\nthe analysis. Measured: every fixture of §6 charges under 1000 `:work`\nunits, the 2300 generated states at most 1470, against 6a's default\n200000; the seven-cell state charges 488 and 11 trials under D6. The\n`:label-bytes` budget disappears with the growing labels.\n\n## 3. Proof of canonicality\n\nCall two configurations (abstract task, partial numbering, worklist)\n**isomorphic** when a bijection `π` of cursor cells carries one to the\nother: the structures agree up to renaming by `π` and up to the host\norder of every unordered container and of the census, numbers agree\n(`N'(π(c)) = N(c)`), and worklists correspond group by group in order.\nThe proof is a simultaneous induction over runs, ordered by (number of\nunnumbered cursor cells of the run's item, nesting depth of the item):\na key run, a component run or a trial is over an item with no more\nunnumbered cells than its enclosing run and strictly fewer nested\nunordered containers, or (a trial) strictly fewer unnumbered cells; so\nthe lemmas below may assume themselves for every local run a run spawns.\n\n**Lemma A (keys are canonical).** The key of an element is the spelling\nof a key run over it, and a key run reads only structure, body ranks\n(the rank of a body is a function of the census up to isomorphism) and\nnumbers; its own listed-order inputs are (i) element order inside nested\ncontainers, which it never uses except through keys, by the induction,\n(ii) the host order of components of equal encoding in its D4 step,\ncovered by lemma C, and (iii) nothing else: a key run that reaches a\nconflict does not choose, it spells the residue `[:tie K … K]`, which is\na function of the group's common key and size. So isomorphic items under\ncorresponding numberings have byte-identical keys, and the sorted order\nof a container's elements is a function of the configuration up to\nisomorphism.\n\n**Lemma B (ties are exactly what keys cannot separate).** By\nconstruction: elements are deferred iff their keys are equal bytes.\n\n**Lemma C (components).** At a fixpoint let `C₁ … Cₘ` be the components\nof one run of equal encodings, each encoding complete, and let\n`U₁ … Uₘ` be their unnumbered cursor cells. The `Uᵢ` are pairwise\ndisjoint and each `Uᵢ` is confined to `Cᵢ` (every occurrence of an\nunnumbered cell lies in a tied element, hence in its own component).\nEqual, complete encodings of `[:component g₁ i₁ …]` rows are a faithful\nspelling (literals as themselves, rows expanded, every cursor cell by\nindex and body rank, groups by sequence number, a group's several\nelements as a sorted set), so they exhibit, for each pair `Cᵢ, Cⱼ`, a\nbijection `Uᵢ → Uⱼ` under which the components are the same structure\ngroup by group. Entering the run in host order `σ` gives the cells of\n`C_{σ(t)}` the numbers `b + (t−1)·|U| + 1 …` by local index; in order\n`σ'` the cells of `C_{σ'(t)}` receive the same numbers. The block-swap\nbijection composed from the pairwise bijections is an isomorphism of the\ntwo resulting configurations: numbers agree by construction, structures\nagree because the swapped components are isomorphic and nothing outside\nthem mentions their cells, and the worklists agree because the same\ngroups remain, with the same elements removed. ∎\n\n**Lemma D (the D5 verdict is invariant).** Whether the root run, or a\ncomponent run it requires, reaches a fixpoint with a conflicting\ncomponent depends only on which tied elements share unnumbered cursor\ncells at that fixpoint, which is a function of the configuration up to\nisomorphism (lemma A gives the ties, the sharing relation is structural);\nand whether a component run is complete is the same question one level\ndown, by the induction. Key runs do not enter the verdict: their\nconflicts are spelled, not refused, and the spelling is canonical (lemma\nA iii).\n\n**Lemma E (D6 trials).** At a conflicting component the set `E` of\nelements of its least conflicting group is a function of the\nconfiguration up to isomorphism (lemma D names the component and its\ngroups canonically). Each trial is a deterministic function of (the\nconfiguration up to isomorphism, the chosen element), by the induction\non the trial run, which has strictly fewer unnumbered cells than the\nenclosing run (its forced element holds at least one, being linked into\nthe component by a shared unnumbered cell). So the multiset of trial\nspellings is canonical and its least member is canonical. Two trials\nwith equal least spellings have produced isomorphic numberings of the\ncomponent (faithful spelling, as in lemma C), so adopting either yields\nisomorphic configurations. ∎\n\n**Theorem.** The procedure's output on isomorphic configurations is the\nsame: both refuse at corresponding points, or both emit byte-identical\nrows. *Proof.* By the induction above. D1 and D2 decisions are functions\nof the configuration up to isomorphism (lemma A); D3 only repeats D1 on\ndeferred groups in a canonical order; D4's choice of the least run is\ncanonical (lemma A on component encodings, which are complete by D5/D6)\nand its only listed-order input is harmless (lemma C); D5 is invariant\n(lemma D); D6 is invariant (lemma E). Emission spells cursor cells by\nnumber only and sorts every emitted unordered slot by canonical bytes\nunder the final numbering (carrier §5), so isomorphic final\nconfigurations emit the same rows. ∎\n\nConsequences:\n\n1. *Function of structure, renaming-invariant, enumeration-invariant*:\n   the theorem with `π` a renaming, a permutation of the containers, or\n   both. No decision reads a source id or a host order except at the\n   point lemma C covers.\n2. *Terminating.* The structural edge family is acyclic (carrier §8 step\n   3), so every traversal is finite. In any run each D3 round that is not\n   the last numbers a cursor cell; each D4 step numbers at least one\n   cursor cell or removes a duplicate group; each local run is over an\n   item with fewer unordered containers nested below it than its\n   spawning run's item, or (a trial) with fewer unnumbered cells, so the\n   induction measure of §3 decreases and there are finitely many local\n   runs. The budget of §2 \"Work\" is what bounds the count.\n3. *Deterministic*, hence two lifts of one state are byte-identical and\n   the receiver's recomputation is well defined.\n4. *Sharing survives*: the numbering is a bijection on reached cursor\n   cells (D2 never renumbers; totality is asserted), every occurrence of\n   one cursor cell spells one number, and two cursor cells at one\n   position get two numbers.\n5. *Cross-engine law*: two engines lifting one canonical frame produce\n   abstract tasks equal up to renaming (the frame is engine-independent,\n   continuation §2.1) and therefore, by the theorem, equal rows.\n6. *D6 moves no D1–D4 id.* Trials run only where D4 found a conflict,\n   which without D6 is a refusal; every numbering D1–D4 assign is\n   identical with and without D6 (§6 checks the pins both ways).\n\n## 4. Amendments\n\n**A1 — carrier §6, from \"The procedure.\" to the end of step 5.** Replace\nwith §2 above (vocabulary, the spelling, D1–D6, totality, work). Keep the\nsection's first paragraph (the counterexample) and amend \"What is\ndefined, and what is not claimed\" to: \"The canonical numbering is defined\nas the output of the discovery-order procedure below, which is a function\nof the state up to renaming of cursor cells and host enumeration of guest\nsets, maps and the census (proof in\n`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md` §3). It is not the\nminimum of the root bytes over all bijections. The one choice the\ntraversal cannot make by itself, between two equal-keyed elements of one\nguest set or map that are linked by shared unnumbered cursor cells at a\nfixpoint of the root run or of a component run, is resolved by a search\nconfined to that component (D6) [or, in the D5 variant: is refused (D5),\nnot searched; the refusal certifies nothing about interchangeability and\ncan be narrowed later without changing any numbering assigned today].\"\nState in step 2's replacement that `[:n k]` and `[:u j r]` carry exact\nCBOR integers, that `r` is the body rank, and that `[:child]` replaces\n`[:child <child root id>]` (reason in §2 \"Spelling\").\n\n**A2 — carrier §6 \"Validation\".** Replace the paragraph's last two\nsentences, from \"Every labeling that yields the selected commitment is\nthereby accepted, and no encounter-order tie-break ever decides validity\"\nto the end, with: \"Every labeling that yields the selected commitment is\nthereby accepted. The emitted commitment and the acceptance decision are\ninvariant under the host's enumeration order of every set, map and\ncensus (proof in the numbering note, §3); the procedure's one use of host\norder, entering components of equal encoding in the order listed, cannot\nchange the rows (lemma C), and a receiver never decides validity by its\nown enumeration order. This is a lower-side structural refusal, not a\nlift kind. A receiver whose work bound (§10.10) is exceeded before the\nprocedure completes refuses with the structured resource refusal before\nany attachment, never labels the state `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`, and\nnever accepts an unfinished check [D5 variant: ; a receiver that reaches\nD5 refuses `:yin.k/undecodable` kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`].\"\n\n**A3 — carrier §5 preamble.** Replace \"The class-erased skeletons of §6\nare used only inside the numbering procedure and never decide the order\nof an emitted row\" with \"The spellings (content encodings) of §6 are\nused only inside the numbering procedure and never decide the order of\nan emitted row\".\n\n**A4 — carrier §8 step 6.** \"Recompute §6 under the work bound, for the\nroot and every install child, and compare (`:yin.k/undecodable`, kind\n`:noncanonical-cursor-cells`; or the resource refusal of §10.10 if the\nbound is exceeded, which propagates untranslated).\" D5 variant: add\n\"`:yin.k/undecodable` kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells` if D5 is reached\nin the root or in a child, translated from the recomputation's lift-side\nrefusal of that kind only, its path prefixed by the carried root id\";\nand in the diagnostics paragraph after step 12 add\n`:unresolved-cursor-cells` to the `:yin.k/undecodable` kinds, change\n\"the two kinds this carrier adds to UCF §7.5.4's set: `:byte-string` and\n`:metadata`\" to \"the three kinds …, `:metadata` and\n`:unresolved-cursor-cells`\", and in §11 \"two lift kinds and two\n`:yin.k/undecodable` kinds are added\" to \"three … and three …\". With D6\nthese counts are unchanged.\n\n**A5 — carrier §10.** Answer 9: \"a renaming-invariant\nrefinement-and-search procedure\" → \"the discovery-order procedure, which\nis invariant under renaming and host enumeration\". Answer 10: \"a bound\non decoded bytes, rows, cursor cells, traversal depth, refinement work\nand search nodes for §6\" → \"a bound on decoded bytes, rows, cursor cells,\ntraversal depth, traversal-and-spelling work and (D6) residue-search\ntrials for §6 (its `:work` unit counts positions and copied bytes, so\nspellings are paid for before they are allocated)\"; and \"never falls\nback to encounter order\" → \"never substitutes host enumeration order for\nthe canonical traversal\". Proposals list: \"as defined by its procedure\"\n→ \"as defined by discovery order [D5 variant: , and the D5 refusal\nclass]\".\n\n**A6 — carrier §3, identity rows.** After \"minted by the lift under §6's\ncanonical numbering\" add \"(the n-th cursor cell met in §6's canonical\ntraversal)\".\n\n**A7 — continuation §2.2.** Replace \"numbered by the draft's §6\nprocedure\" with \"numbered by discovery order (draft §6 as amended by\n`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md`)\".\n\n**A8 — continuation §5.5.** Three replacements. (i) \"Draft §6 is\ninvariant under renaming of the **cursor cells** (its labels never encode\nan incoming cursor-cell id), and the only other identity it consumes is\nthe stream marker in each cursor cell's initial label\n(`[:body <stream-marker-bytes> <position-bytes>]`, draft §6 step 1) and\nin cursor-cell bodies\" → \"Draft §6 is invariant under renaming of the\n**cursor cells** (it reads no incoming cursor-cell id), and the only\nother identity it consumes is the stream marker in each cursor cell's\nbody label (`[:body [:stream identity descriptor] position]`, draft §6\nas amended, spelled by rank inside keys) and in cursor-cell bodies\".\n(ii) \"Unordered containers (`:set`, `:map`, `:store`, `:bindings`, the\nparked table) are encoded by sorting their elements' encodings bytewise\n(draft §6 step 2), never by host traversal order\" → \"Guest sets and maps\nare entered in the bytewise order of their elements' spellings, and\n`:store`, `:bindings` and the parked table in the byte order of their\nstructural keys (draft §6 D1), never in host enumeration order\". (iii)\nLeave \"never derived from traversal order\" but qualify it: \"never\nderived from *host* traversal order (the canonical traversal of draft §6\nD1 is a function of the state and is what the procedure reads)\".\n\n**A9 — continuation §8.1 acceptance.** Item 10 stands; append items 12–22\nof §9 below as the acceptance of the rewritten numbering.\n\n**A10 — UCF §7.5.3.** No change: \"minted by the emitter (stable within\none lift; remapped on lowering) and not content-addressed\" remains true.\n\n## 5. The alternative: portable ids minted in the VM\n\nThe owner asked whether a cursor cell could carry a portable id from\ncreation so the carrier sends it as is. To work across engines the id\nmust be computable by every engine from shared inputs. The candidates\n(branch `srvm-phase6a`, `src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc`) are: the task's id\ncounter (`gen-id`, `:227-230`; `gensym`, `:2312-2318`, which lower adopts\nas `max(local, carried)`), the creation sequence of `:stream/cursor`\nexecutions (`handle-cursor-unminted`'s `seq-n`, `:557-568`, minted at the\n`:stream/cursor` effect, `:2595-2602`, with the id from `gensym` and the\nsealed reference from `issue-ref`, `:311-323`), a hash of (occurrence,\ncounter), or a random id. Every one of them names *history*, not\nstructure:\n\n- *Same structure, same hash fails.* Hold every carried field equal:\n  task A executes `:stream/cursor` three times (ids `cursor-0`,\n  `cursor-1`, `cursor-2`; counter 3) and drops the second; task B executes\n  `:stream/cursor` twice and then consumes one unrelated fresh name\n  (`gensym` for a non-cursor id; counter 3) and keeps both. The two\n  snapshots are structurally equal (two cursor cells with equal bodies at\n  equal positions, the same id counter, the same everything else), yet A\n  carries `{cursor-0, cursor-2}` and B `{cursor-0, cursor-1}`, so the root\n  ids differ. Any id recomputed from the snapshot's *structure* instead\n  is the canonicalization problem of §2 again, not an alternative to it.\n- *The cross-engine harness law* would hold only if both engines agree\n  on the minting history, which makes an execution trace part of the\n  frame's identity; a random or occurrence-bound id breaks it outright\n  (a fork halt has no occurrence; a child has none of its own).\n- *The VM grows a second name.* Lower mints a fresh private resource\n  entry per cursor cell (carrier §8 step 12) and must keep the carried\n  id beside it; a relift after further execution must mint ids that\n  cannot collide with carried ones, which needs an (origin, counter)\n  pair: a global naming scheme for a thing that only needs a name\n  relative to its state.\n\nDiscovery order answers the owner's observation differently: the\nidentity stays local and the carrier gives it a *structural* name, the\nn-th cursor cell met, exactly as a de Bruijn index names a binder by\nposition rather than by a global symbol. Recommend against portable\nids for immutable creation-history ids carried unchanged; that is the\nonly kind proposed.\n\n## 6. Prototype\n\n`collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj` (not repo code; a\nscratch project under `collab/`, never committed) implements D1–D6 as\none schema-driven run procedure (root run, key runs, component runs and\ntrials share the code; a local run differs only in its number tag, in\nspelling residues as `[:tie]` and, for a trial, in its forced element),\nmemoized as §2 describes, charging `:work` and `:search` as §2 \"Work\"\ndescribes, with the totality assertion, an emitter that numbers install\nchildren by the same procedure, a decode path that runs 6a's `decode*`\nwith its numbering check off and then wraps the whole discovery\nrecomputation (root and children) in the narrow translation, and two\nswitches: `:residue-search` (D6; off is the D5 variant) and\n`:permissive` (diagnostic: a conflict is resolved by one host-first\ntrial, to test whether a refused state is in fact interchangeable). Run\nfrom the probe directory (JVM only; `@#'rows/…` reaches 6a's private\nhelpers); the `clojure` launcher was not available to this session, so\nthe run below used the launcher's cached classpath directly:\n\n```\njava @java.args clojure.main -e \"(require 'probe.discovery) (probe.discovery/-main)\"\n```\n\nResults of 2026-10-12, round 3 (full log `collab/numbering-probe/run.log`;\n6a's own tests pass unchanged in the same project):\n\n| State | Discovery order (D5 variant) | With D6 | 6a |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `fixture :s1 :s2 :x :y` (`rows_test.cljc:28`) | `x cc-1, y cc-2`, root `fab223…` | same | same root |\n| review counterexample `'a #{X Y}`, `'b X` | `x cc-1, y cc-2`, root `a3713a5e…` | same | root `2d9873a8…` |\n| symmetric pair `#{X Y}` | component rule, root `07eda35d…` | same | same root |\n| `#{[X X] [Y Y]}` / `{X 1, Y 1}` | roots `da69c679…` / `49d4478c…`; equal after reversal and after renaming plus shuffling | same | same roots |\n| `[x x y]` / `[x y y]` (`rows_test.cljc:159`) | roots `4993b5b1…` / `536c810b…` | same | `71543fe2…` / same |\n| **review A** `#{[X X] [Y Z]}` | `X cc-1, Y cc-2, Z cc-3` for both enumerations, root `65ba4530…` (the review's digest) | same | `387f294a…` |\n| **review B** `{X X, Y Z}` | `X cc-1, Y cc-2, Z cc-3` for both enumerations, root `409548a7…` (the review's digest) | same | `be62ba43…` |\n| **review C** `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}` | total numbering, inner set listed first gets `cc-1, cc-2`; root `18b70de6…` for the state and for its renaming | same | same root |\n| `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` | numbered (two components, each one element per group), root `92747c0f…` | same | same root |\n| `[#{X Y} X]` | `X cc-1, Y cc-2`, root `7dc3d5c4…` | same | `a850fce6…` |\n| `[#{#{X Y} #{Z W}} X]` | `X cc-1, Y cc-2, Z cc-3, W cc-4`, root `6ea03f9b…` | same | `e61d3709…` |\n| `#{[#{X Y} #{X Y}] [#{P Q} #{R S}]}` | keys differ (two cursor cells against four); root `b35b8e12…` | same | refused `[:label-bytes]` |\n| **review r2 witness** `[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]` | key run conflicts, root run numbers `A cc-1, B cc-2`; root `9600fe01…` (the review's digest); 1 root over 24 renamings plus shuffles; decodes | same | `01933121…` |\n| loop plus 2-cycle (`rows_test.cljc:312`) | `L cc-1`, then refused `:unresolved-cursor-cells`, path `[15]`, cells `[A B]` | `L cc-1, A cc-2, B cc-3`, 2 trials, root `858a891e…`, 1 root over 24 | root `55ff168e…` (`L cc-3`) |\n| 2-cycle `#{[A B] [B A]}` | refused; permissive mode gives 1 root over 24 shuffles (interchangeable, not certified) | `A cc-1, B cc-2`, 2 trials, root `0fe898cc…` **= 6a's root** | numbered |\n| 3-cycle `#{[A B] [B C] [C A]}` | refused; permissive 1 root | 3 trials, root `0b181cd4…` **= 6a's root** | numbered |\n| path `#{[A B] [B C]}` | refused; permissive mode gives 2 roots (not interchangeable) | 2 trials, root `726a9fbb…`, 1 root over 24 | `76117652…` |\n| seven-cell triangle plus square (`:392`) | refused; permissive 1 root over 24 (the 4 of round 2 came from listed-order entry) | 11 trials, 488 `:work`, root `d76d787a…`, 1 root over 24; decode through 6a's `decode*` stops at 6a's own `[:label-bytes]` (probe limitation) | refused `[:label-bytes]` |\n| ten cells in a vector (`:331`) | `cc-1 … cc-10` in vector order | same | same root |\n| frame fixture: wait window `x`, K-frame window `y`, environment `y`, parked K-frame window `#{x y}` | `y cc-1` (free environment, slot 5), `x cc-2` (parked table, slot 8), `:test/cursor cc-3`; root `0b9ef61e…`; decodes | same | `fd3513dd…` |\n| frame fixture: wait window `#{x y}`, parked K-frame window `y` | `y cc-1`, `x cc-2`, `:test/cursor cc-3`; root `ff124bfc…`; decodes | same | `e1558410…` |\n| parent with two installs sharing the review-counterexample child | child `X cc-1, Y cc-2`; one child row shared; root `9544fcd7…`; decodes | same | — |\n| **install child holding the 2-cycle** | lift refuses path `[12 a 15]`; a carrier of it built with `emit-numbered` decodes to `:yin.k/undecodable :unresolved-cursor-cells`, path `[<root> 12 a 15]`, cells `[cc-1 cc-2]` | numbers, root `734c33f3…`, decodes | — |\n| budgets | loop plus 2-cycle under `{:work 1}`: `:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit [:work]`; a valid carrier decoded under `{:work 1}` propagates the same | 3-cycle under `{:search 1}`: `:resource-limit [:search]` | — |\n| key bytes, 4 cells over 2 bodies in `#{#{X Y} #{Z W} #{X Z}}` | label spelling: 4364 bytes at a 128-character descriptor, 920118 at 65536; **rank spelling: 1396 at both**; 13 local runs, 78 `:work`, same root either spelling | — | — |\n| 2000 random value states | 1975 numbered, 25 refused (14 witnessed non-interchangeable by 12 shuffles, 11 not distinguished), **0 invariance failures**, 1783 decode round trips ok, 0 decode failures, 192 not decodable through 6a's own recompute budget (probe limitation); max `:work` 1254 | **2000 numbered, 0 refused**, 0 invariance failures, 1808 decoded, 0 failures, 192 at 6a's budget; max trials 13, max `:work` 1470 | — |\n| 200 frame-bearing states | 192 numbered, 8 refused (5 witnessed, 3 not distinguished), 0 failures, 175 decoded, 17 at 6a's budget; max `:work` 767 | 200 numbered, 0 refused, 0 failures, 183 decoded, max trials 5 | — |\n| 100 states with children | 98 numbered, 2 refused (not distinguished), 0 failures, 75 decoded, 23 at 6a's budget; max `:work` 916 | 100 numbered, 0 refused, 0 failures, 76 decoded, max trials 4 | — |\n\nHand derivations, verified against the codec above (the digest comes\nfrom `emit-numbered` under the hand-derived numbering; where 6a or the\nreview reached the same numbering independently the digest agrees).\nFixture `:s1 :s2 :x :y`: the store's `'payload` map is entered by the\nkeys of its entries; whichever of `'one` (`[:cursor-cell x]`), `'both`\n(`#{x y}`) or `'cursors` (`{x :x, y :y}`) sorts first, `x` is met before\n`y`, since `x` is alone under `'one` and its body (`:s1`) ranks before\n`y`'s (`:s2`) in the set and the map; so `x = cc-1`, `y = cc-2`. Review\ncounterexample: `'a` before `'b`; `#{X Y}` ties (`[u0 r]` twice) and is\ndeferred; `'b` meets `X = cc-1`; the fixpoint re-keys the set, `[:n 1]`\nagainst `[:u 0 r]`, and `Y = cc-2`. Review A: keys `[:vec [u0 r] [u0 r]]`\nand `[:vec [u0 r] [u1 r]]` differ at the second cell's index, so `[X X]`\nis entered first: `X = cc-1`, then `Y = cc-2`, `Z = cc-3`. Review B: the\nentries `[:entry [u0 r] [u0 r]]` and `[:entry [u0 r] [u1 r]]` likewise.\nReview C: both inner sets key as `[:set [u0 r] [u1 r]]` (each key run\nresolves its own `{X, Y}` tie by D4 with two one-element components),\nthe outer tie is deferred, the fixpoint changes nothing, the two\ncomponents are isomorphic, and the one listed first gets `cc-1, cc-2`:\neither order emits `#{#{1 2} #{3 4}}`. `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]`: both sets tie\nand defer; components `{X¹, X²}` and `{Y¹, Y²}` (one element per group,\nequal encodings `[:component 1 [u0 r] 2 [u0 r]]`): `X = cc-1`, `Y = cc-2`\nin host order, same rows either way. `[#{X Y} X]`: the set ties, index 1\nmeets `X = cc-1`, the fixpoint splits the set, `Y = cc-2`. **Witness**\n`[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]`: F4 above; the key run's residue `[:set [:tie K\nK]]` is the outer set's only key, so the outer element is entered, the\ninner tie deferred, `A = cc-1` at index 1, and the fixpoint gives\n`B = cc-2`. Loop plus 2-cycle: `[L L]` keys as `[u0 r] [u0 r]`, the two\nothers as `[u0 r] [u1 r]`, so `L = cc-1` by key order; `{[A B], [B A]}`\nties, the fixpoint changes nothing, and the component `{[A B], [B A]}`\nholds two elements of one group: D5 refuses; **D6** runs two trials\nover `[:component 1 [:set [A B] [B A]]]`, one entering `[A B]` first\n(`A = u0, B = u1`, then `[B A]` keys `[u1 u0]` and is entered), one the\nmirror image; the two spellings are equal, the first is adopted:\n`A = cc-2`, `B = cc-3`. 2-cycle alone: the same two trials from the empty\nnumbering, `A = cc-1`, `B = cc-2`, and 6a's search reaches the same\nrows. 3-cycle: three trials, each a rotation of the others, equal\nspellings, `A, B, C = cc-1, cc-2, cc-3` in the first trial's order, again\n6a's rows. Path `#{[A B] [B C]}`: two trials, `[A B]` first gives\n`[:set [u0 u1] [u1 u2]]`, `[B C]` first gives `[:set [u2 u0] [u0 u1]]`;\nthe least is adopted. The example state of\n`collab/example-suspended-state.clj` has one cursor cell, first met in\nthe act's environment under `c`, so `cc-1`; a one-cell state has the\nsame rows under both procedures.\n\nPins: none of the round-1 or round-2 pins change (`fab223…`, `c4a7c7…`,\n`a3713a5e…`, `07eda35d…`, `da69c679…`, `49d4478c…`, `4993b5b1…`,\n`536c810b…`, `65ba4530…`, `409548a7…`, `18b70de6…`, `92747c0f…`,\n`7dc3d5c4…`, `6ea03f9b…`, `b35b8e12…`, the ten-cell root `6e7c7a6b…`),\nwith the rank spelling and with D6 on. New pins: the witness\n`9600fe01…`; under D6 the loop plus 2-cycle `858a891e…` (6a's `55ff168e…`\nstays removed), the 2-cycle `0fe898cc…` and 3-cycle `0b181cd4…` (6a's\nown roots), the path `726a9fbb…`, the seven-cell state `d76d787a…`.\n\n## 7. Recommendation and cost\n\n**Adopt discovery order with D6** (owner decision: adopt; D6 is this\nnote's recommendation within it). 6b has not started and 6a is\nunmerged, so the cost is one implementation slice of `rows.cljc` and its\ntests (§8) plus the amendments of §4. Size, from the prototype and the\nsecond review's count rather than the round-2 guess: the prototype's\nschema-to-procedure region is about 330 nonblank lines, of which about\n30 are D6, the diagnostic mode, tracing and measurement; in place it\nreuses 6a's `descriptors`, `bytes-key`, `compare-bytes`, `spend!`,\n`refuse!` and `emit-node`, so expect **250–300 added or replacement\nlines and 135–150 deleted** (`check-label-bytes!`, labels, `ce`,\n`positions`, `classes`, `refine`, `search`), **about 100–160 net**,\nexcluding tests; the second review's independent estimate was 100–195\nnet. Review cost: one implementation round and one review round; the\nreview reads §3 (five lemmas, one induction) and §8's cut list rather\nthan re-deriving the design, and the generator of §9 item 18 runs in CI.\nWhat the owner gains over 6a: a definition that can be stated and proved\n(the n-th cell met, with one bounded tie-break), no growing labels, a\nsearch confined to the residue (two trials for a 2-cycle; 6a's search\nover the seven-cell state exceeded a megabyte of labels, D6 numbers it in\n11 trials), and 6a's own roots on the 2-cycle and 3-cycle. Risk: a\ncanonicality bug, mitigated by §9 item 18's generator in CI (it caught\nthe prototype's memo bug), by the decode round trip in that generator,\nand by the proof; and the unproved cost bound, which the `:work` and\n`:search` budgets enforce as 6a's budgets did.\n\n**D5 variant (no search at all).** Same slice minus D6 (about 25 lines),\nplus the `:unresolved-cursor-cells` kind, its decode translation and the\ndocumented refusal class of F4; refuses 2-cycles and 3-cycles that 6a\nnumbers. Acceptable if the owner wants no search in the codec; the\nrefusal can be replaced by D6 later without changing any id numbered\nunder D1–D4 (§3 consequence 6).\n\n**Keep 6a.** Zero rounds now, but 6b, the cross-engine harness and every\nreviewer of a pinned id inherit an exhaustive search over the whole\nstate, labels that grow until the seven-cell state exceeds a megabyte,\nand a definition that is \"whatever the procedure outputs\". Not\nrecommended; the owner has decided against it.\n\n**Portable ids.** Changes the VM, the census, the laws and the harness\n(§5); three or more rounds across two designs. Against.\n\n## 8. Replacing 6a: what is deleted, rewritten and kept\n\nAgainst branch `srvm-phase6a` (`d77da9f0`), `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc`\n(line numbers of that file) and `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc`. An\nimplementation brief can be cut from this list.\n\n**rows.cljc, deleted.**\n- `check-label-bytes!` (`:212-230`): the label-size bound; nothing grows\n  any more.\n- Inside `canonical-numbering` (`:236-355`): the label seeding\n  (`:252-255`), `ce-slot`/`ce` (`:257-284`), `positions` (`:285-325`),\n  `classes` (`:326-330`), `refine` (`:331-337`), `search` (`:338-354`),\n  and the `checked-encode`/`checked-key`/`checked-sort` wrappers\n  (`:245-251`). The `[:child (checked-encode id)]` spelling (`:273-275`)\n  goes with `ce`.\n- `default-budget` (`:200-202`): the `:label-bytes` key; the `:search`\n  key too in the D5 variant (with D6 it stays and counts trials).\n\n**rows.cljc, rewritten.**\n- `canonical-numbering` (`:236-355`), same signature and contract\n  (`task`, optional `budget` → `{source-id :yin.k/cc-n}`; empty census →\n  `{}`), now the root run of §2: `descriptors` extended by the two\n  synthetic kinds `:entry` and `:component`; body ranks from the census;\n  `enter`/`unordered`/`fixpoint`/`components`/`component-encoding`/\n  `adopt`/`resolve`/`local-run`/`spell` as in the prototype\n  (`collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj`, sections \"runs\" and\n  \"spelling\"), with `spend!` charged as §2 \"Work\" says; the totality\n  assertion before returning; D5 raising `:yin.k/non-portable\n  :unresolved-cursor-cells` (D5 variant) or D6 charging `:search`.\n  Portable `.cljc` per continuation §12: no `@#'` reflection, `mapv`/\n  `into`/`reduce` (not `for`) over sequences that may exceed 32 elements\n  (the prototype's `components` uses `for`; replace), `contains?` for\n  membership, `(assoc {} …)` never `(assoc nil …)`, atoms are fine on all\n  three hosts.\n- `emit-node` (`:181-197`): thread a row path so a child's D5 refusal\n  reports `[12 <install name> …]` (D5 variant only; otherwise unchanged).\n- `decode*` recompute (`:570-575`): wrap the `emit` call (which numbers\n  the root and every child) so that only `:yin.k/non-portable\n  :unresolved-cursor-cells` becomes `:yin.k/undecodable\n  :unresolved-cursor-cells` with the path prefixed by `root`; a\n  `:resource-limit` propagates (D5 variant only; with D6 the recompute\n  is unchanged apart from the budget keys).\n- Namespace docstring (`:1-17`): \"canonical cursor-cell numbering\" →\n  \"discovery-order cursor-cell numbering (design note §2)\".\n\n**rows.cljc, kept unchanged.** `row-table`, `pending-table`, `node`,\n`refuse!`/`bad!`, the marker checks, `bytes-key`/`compare-bytes`/\n`byte-compare`/`sorted-bytes`, `descriptors`, the census order\n(`:137-156`), `emit-slot`/`emit-node` apart from the path, `spend!`\n(`:205-209`), `emit-numbered` (`:358-363`), `emit` (`:366-372`),\n`structural?`, `literal?`, `check-abstract!`, all of `decode*` before\n`:570` and after `:575` (the order numbering → `validate-frames`,\n`:576-578`, stays), `decode`, `canonical-under`, `frame->rows`,\n`rows->frame`, `validate-frames`, and the `:bytes`, `:rows`, `:depth`,\n`:cursor-cells`, `:work` budget keys with their defaults.\n\n**rows_test.cljc, kept as fixtures and tests.** `task-node`,\n`cursor-cell-body`, `fixture` (`:16-37`), `portable-frame` and the\n`bijection` test, `row-ids-are-jing-default`, `replace-row`,\n`window-sorted-and-unique`, `canonical-under-is-renaming-invariant`\n(`:143-156`), `alias-topology-and-census` (`:159-168`),\n`gates-and-graph-before-hashes`, `numbering-precedes-frame-defect`\n(`:206-213`), `schema-and-literal-canonicality`,\n`child-scope-and-shared-child-dag` (`:240-249`),\n`pending-envelope-and-nil-literal-are-preserved`,\n`task-root-checks-carried-code-and-frame`, `reversed` and\n`unordered-containers-are-order-free` (`:285-304`),\n`census-is-numeric-from-ten-cursor-cells` (`:331-338`), the marker tests\n(`:341-381`), `cursor-cell-and-identity-shaped-literal-are-distinct`\n(`:384-389`), and the fixture `seven-cursor-cell-triangle-square`\n(`:392-397`). Every counterexample of §6 becomes a fixture (items 12–16\nbelow), including the refused ones.\n\n**rows_test.cljc, rewritten.**\n- `refinement-search-on-symmetric-cursor-cells-is-bounded` (`:171-178`)\n  → `symmetric-cursor-cells-number-by-the-component-rule`: same two\n  assertions on equal roots and two numbers; the `{:search 1}`\n  assertions become `{:work 1}` → `:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit`.\n- `canonical-roots-are-pinned` (`:307-328`): `fab223…` and `c4a7c7…`\n  stay; the loop plus 2-cycle block loses the `{:search 2}` assertion and\n  the pin `55ff168e…`; under D6 it pins `858a891e…` with `L = cc-1`\n  (and the renamed task equal); under the D5 variant it asserts the\n  `:unresolved-cursor-cells` refusal with path `[15]` and cells `[A B]`.\n- `intermediate-label-bytes-are-bounded` (`:400-403`) →\n  `seven-cursor-cells-number-by-residue-search` (D6: root `d76d787a…`,\n  11 trials, `{:search 10}` → `:resource-limit [:search]`) or, in the D5\n  variant, `seven-cursor-cells-are-refused` (`:unresolved-cursor-cells`,\n  cells `[a b c d e f g]`).\n- `input-row-bodies-do-not-spend-label-bytes` (`:406-421`) →\n  `large-literals-do-not-spend-work-beyond-their-bytes`, same body: a\n  400000-element literal still emits and decodes under the default\n  budget.\n\n## 9. Acceptance for the rewritten 6a numbering\n\n12. `rows-test/discovery-order-numbering`: for `fixture :s1 :s2 :x :y`,\n    the review counterexample, `[x x y]`, `[x y y]`, `[#{X Y} X]`, the\n    witness `[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]` and the two frame fixtures of §6,\n    `canonical-numbering` returns exactly the maps of §6.\n13. `rows-test/review-counterexample-resolves-by-fixpoint`: `'a #{X Y}`,\n    `'b X` numbers `X cc-1, Y cc-2`; the task renamed and with the set\n    reversed gives the same root; the root is pinned `a3713a5e…`.\n14. `rows-test/component-rule`: `#{X Y}`, `#{[X X] [Y Y]}`, `{X 1, Y 1}`,\n    `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}` and `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` number without refusal;\n    element reversal and renaming give equal roots; pins `07eda35d…`,\n    `da69c679…`, `49d4478c…`, `18b70de6…`, `92747c0f…`.\n15. `rows-test/alias-pattern-is-part-of-the-key`: `#{[X X] [Y Z]}` and\n    `{X X, Y Z}` give one root for both enumerations and after renaming,\n    pinned `65ba4530…` and `409548a7…`; `#{[#{X Y} #{X Y}] [#{P Q} #{R S}]}`\n    numbers and is pinned `b35b8e12…`; the witness `[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]`\n    numbers `A cc-1, B cc-2`, is pinned `9600fe01…` and decodes.\n16. D6: `rows-test/residue-search`: the 2-cycle, the 3-cycle, the path,\n    the loop plus 2-cycle and the seven-cell state number with the pins\n    of §6 (`0fe898cc…`, `0b181cd4…`, `726a9fbb…`, `858a891e…`,\n    `d76d787a…`), renaming plus shuffling gives equal roots, every\n    carrier decodes, and the 3-cycle under `{:search 1}` refuses\n    `:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit [:search]`.\n    D5 variant instead: `rows-test/unresolved-cursor-cells-refuse`: the\n    same five states refuse at `emit` with `:yin.k/non-portable`, kind\n    `:unresolved-cursor-cells`, path `[15]` and the component's sorted\n    cursor cells; a carrier of each built with `emit-numbered` decodes to\n    `:yin.k/undecodable :unresolved-cursor-cells` with the path prefixed\n    by the root, never `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`; a parent whose\n    install `'a` holds the 2-cycle refuses at `emit` with path\n    `[12 a 15]` and its `emit-numbered` carrier decodes to the\n    `:yin.k/undecodable` kind with path `[<root> 12 a 15]`.\n17. `rows-test/canonical-roots-are-pinned`: `fab223…` and `c4a7c7…`\n    unchanged; `a3713a5e…`, `4993b5b1…`, `536c810b…`, `7dc3d5c4…`,\n    `6ea03f9b…`, `9600fe01…` added; `55ff168e…` removed.\n18. `rows-test/counterexample-search`: a seeded generator (one to six\n    cursor cells over two bodies; nested `:vec`, `:set`, `:map` with\n    cursor-cell keys, sets of cursor-cell pairs including `[X X]`, sets of\n    sets, under keyed bindings and the result; unique elements and keys\n    under decoded equality) over at least 2000 states, plus at least 200\n    frame-bearing states (cursor cells in an act window, a K-frame\n    window, environments matched to the free environment, and a parked\n    state at a park site) and at least 100 states with install children:\n    every numbered state's carrier decodes and re-emits to its root, and\n    renaming every cursor cell plus shuffling every set, map and census\n    gives an equal root id; refusals are counted and reported (0 under\n    D6); the largest `:work` and `:search` charged are reported.\n19. `rows-test/numbering-is-total`: a task whose census declares a\n    cursor cell the traversal does not reach raises the totality defect\n    (not a refusal) before any row is emitted.\n20. `numbering-precedes-frame-defect`, `alias-topology-and-census`,\n    `canonical-under-is-renaming-invariant`,\n    `unordered-containers-are-order-free`, `child-scope-and-shared-child-dag`\n    and `census-is-numeric-from-ten-cursor-cells` pass unchanged; the\n    rewritten tests of §8 pass.\n21. `rows-test/work-is-charged-per-byte`: a state whose cursor-cell\n    bodies carry a 65536-character descriptor numbers under the default\n    budget with the same `:work` as under a 128-character descriptor\n    (rank spelling), and `{:work 1}` refuses `:yin.k/unsupported\n    :resource-limit [:work]` at `emit` and, for a valid carrier, at\n    `decode`.\n22. Budgets and docs: `:label-bytes` is gone (and `:search` in the D5\n    variant); A1–A9 applied in the variant chosen; kondo clean;\n    `bb test:sub yin.vm` green on three hosts; Node log shows\n    `Testing yin.vm.ucf.rows-test`.\n\n## 10. Owner questions\n\n1. **D6 or the D5 variant?** Recommended: D6 (a search confined to one\n   conflicting component, measured at 2–13 trials, 6a's roots on the\n   2-cycle and 3-cycle, no structural refusal class to document). The D5\n   variant is the same slice without about 25 lines, with the\n   `:unresolved-cursor-cells` kind and the F4 regression (2-cycles and\n   3-cycles refused) as a documented limitation that D6 can lift later\n   without moving any D1–D4 id.\n2. If the D5 variant: the refusal kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells` as\n   proposed, or the orchestrator's `:resource-limit` (recommended\n   against: it is not a budget, and carrier §10.10 says a resource\n   refusal never stands in for a canonicality verdict)?\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":17,"newStart":1,"newLines":22,"lines":[" # Cursor-cell numbering by discovery order (companion to the row carrier)"," ","-Status: **Proposed, not landed.** Written 2026-10-12 (Architect,","-claude-fable-5-1) for the owner's observation that \"the problem is not","-that the cursor cell has identity, but that the identity is local\", and","-the orchestrator's proposal to number cursor cells by **discovery order**","-in a canonical traversal. **Revised 2026-10-12** after the independent","-review (gpt-6.1-sol, `collab/1792140000000-…findings.md`), which broke","-the first draft in two places: the orbit lemma was unsound (equal","-content encodings did not imply interchangeable elements: `#{[X X] [Y Z]}`)","-and nested tie groups were never processed (`#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}`). The","-rule, the proof, the refusal class, the decode path, the work bound, the","-amendments and the prototype are rewritten below; every pinned id that","-moved is listed in §6. Subordinate to","+Status: **Adopted by the owner 2026-10-12 (replaces carrier §6's","+refinement and search); not yet implemented.** Written 2026-10-12","+(Architect, claude-fable-5-1) for the owner's observation that \"the","+problem is not that the cursor cell has identity, but that the identity","+is local\", and the orchestrator's proposal to number cursor cells by","+**discovery order** in a canonical traversal. Revised twice the same day","+after independent reviews (gpt-6.1-sol,","+`collab/1792140000000-…findings.md` and `collab/1792170000000-…findings.md`).","+Round 2 repaired the orbit lemma (`#{[X X] [Y Z]}`) and nested tie groups","+(`#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}`). Round 3 (this text) fixes what the second review","+found: which local conflicts refuse (§2 D5, witness","+`[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]`), the install-child refusal escaping decode, the","+work bound (now an explicit budget, with body labels spelled by rank so","+key bytes no longer grow with a stream descriptor), the A2 wording, the","+size estimate, and the coverage regression against 6a (2-cycles and","+3-cycles), for which §2 D6 specifies and §6 measures the repair. §8 lists","+what an implementation deletes, rewrites and keeps in 6a. Subordinate to"," [`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md)"," (the carrier, body version 3) and"," [`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md`](./yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md);"]},{"oldStart":21,"oldLines":12,"newStart":26,"newLines":17,"lines":[" (branch `srvm-phase6a` at `d77da9f0`), and a prototype run against that"," code (§6). Nothing here is implemented."," ","-**Recommendation in one line:** adopt discovery-order numbering now (§7,","-option a): a function of structure, renaming- and enumeration-invariant,","-provable by hand (§3), with work bounded and accounted before allocation","-(§2 \"Work\"); it deletes carrier §6's refinement and search, keeps every","-pinned root id except the one state only the search could number, and","-refuses a precisely described class (D5) that it cannot certify.","+**In one line:** number cursor cells in the order a canonical traversal","+meets them (D1–D2); order the elements of a guest set or map by a key","+that is the spelling of a speculative local run over the element (so","+alias patterns such as `[X X]` versus `[Y Z]` are part of the key);","+defer equal keys and re-examine them to a fixpoint (D3); at a fixpoint,","+link tied elements that share an unnumbered cursor cell into components","+and enter the component of least complete encoding (D4); a component","+holding two equal-keyed elements of one container is the one case this","+cannot certify: refuse it (D5), or resolve it by a bounded residue search","+over that component alone (D6, recommended). Proof in §3, budget in §2","+\"Work\", results in §6, the cut against 6a in §8."," "," ## 0. Vocabulary used below"," "]},{"oldStart":34,"oldLines":9,"newStart":44,"newLines":9,"lines":["   the abstract task alone. **Host enumeration order** (also \"listed","   order\") is the order in which a host happens to list the elements of a","   guest set or the entries of a guest map, or the census; it is an","-  accident of the host and the procedure must never let it decide a","-  number. The first draft's amendments blurred the two; §4 keeps them","-  apart.","+  accident of the host. The procedure reads it in exactly one place, D4's","+  entry of components of equal encoding, where lemma C shows it cannot","+  change the result."," - An **element** is a `:set` element or a `:map` entry `[key value]`","   (the key is a guest value and may be a cursor cell)."," - A **numbering** `N` is a partial map from the task's cursor cells to"]},{"oldStart":45,"oldLines":9,"newStart":55,"newLines":20,"lines":[" - A **run** is one execution of the traversal-and-resolution procedure","   over one item (a row or a value) from a starting numbering. The **root","   run** is the run over the `:task` row from the empty numbering; its","-  numbers are the output. A **local run** is a run over one element (or","-  one component, D4) from the enclosing run's current numbering; it","-  exists only to compute a sort key and is discarded.","+  numbers are the output. A **local run** is a run over one item from","+  the enclosing run's current numbering. There are three kinds of local","+  run, and the distinction decides what refuses (D5):","+  - a **key run** is a local run over one element, made to compute the","+    element's sort key; it is *speculative*: it never refuses, and","+    whatever it cannot resolve (a tie, a conflict) is spelled in its","+    key as `[:tie …]`;","+  - a **component run** is a local run over a component's synthetic","+    `[:component …]` row (D4); it must be *complete*, else D5;","+  - a **trial** (D6 only) is a component run that enters one chosen","+    element before its siblings.","+- A **body label** is `[:body [:stream identity descriptor] position]`,","+  a cursor cell's census body as plain data; its **rank** is its index","+  among the census's distinct body labels in bytewise order."," "," ## 1. Findings"," "]},{"oldStart":67,"oldLines":14,"newStart":88,"newLines":12,"lines":[" | `:map` entries | **unordered** (keys are `V`) | none |"," | `:cursor-cells` census | not a position (carrier §6 step 2, §8 step 3) | — |"," ","-So the environment's key order is canonical (bytes of the symbol as","-spelled, the order `:bindings` rows are emitted in), and the only","-unordered positions are guest `:set` elements and guest `:map` entries.","-The review confirmed the inventory complete. The example state in","-`collab/example-suspended-state.clj` holds its one cursor cell","-(`:cursor-1`) in three ordered positions: the act's environment under","-`c`, the window under register 2, and the `:next` pending's","-`cursor-cell-C` slot.","+So the only unordered positions are guest `:set` elements and guest","+`:map` entries. Both reviews confirmed the inventory complete. The","+example state in `collab/example-suspended-state.clj` holds its one","+cursor cell (`:cursor-1`) in three ordered positions: the act's","+environment under `c`, the window under register 2, and the `:next`","+pending's `cursor-cell-C` slot."," "," **F2. Discovery order subsumes the positions step.** The carrier's §6"," step 3 (positions) exists to let a cursor cell's number depend on where"]},{"oldStart":87,"oldLines":79,"newStart":106,"newLines":109,"lines":[" "," **F3. Unordered containers are ordered by a content encoding that"," carries the alias pattern.** The first draft spelled an unnumbered cursor","-cell by its body alone, so `[X X]` and `[Y Z]` encoded alike and the","-review's counterexample followed. The repaired encoding (§2 \"Spelling\")","-numbers the cursor cells *inside* an element provisionally, in the","-element's own discovery order, relative to the enclosing numbering: `[X X]`","-spells `[(u0) (u0)]`, `[Y Z]` spells `[(u0) (u1)]`, so alias topology","-inside an element is part of its sort key, as a de Bruijn index is part","-of a term. The key of an element is the spelling produced by a *local","-run* over it, which applies the same rules recursively (nested","-containers sorted, nested ties deferred and re-examined, D4 inside) and","-spells what it cannot resolve as `[:tie …]`. Equal keys form a **tie","-group**, deferred to the run's worklist and re-examined to a fixpoint","-(D3) as other positions assign numbers. No key ever grows beyond the","-size of its element's expansion.","+cell by its body alone, so `[X X]` and `[Y Z]` encoded alike. The","+repaired encoding (§2 \"Spelling\") numbers the cursor cells *inside* an","+element provisionally, in the element's own discovery order, relative to","+the enclosing numbering: `[X X]` spells `[(u0) (u0)]`, `[Y Z]` spells","+`[(u0) (u1)]`, so alias topology inside an element is part of its sort","+key, as a de Bruijn index is part of a term. The key of an element is the","+spelling produced by a *key run* over it, which applies the same rules","+recursively (nested containers sorted, nested ties deferred and","+re-examined, D4 inside) and spells what it cannot resolve as `[:tie …]`.","+Equal keys form a **tie group**, deferred to the run's worklist and","+re-examined to a fixpoint (D3) as other positions assign numbers. A","+provisional cell is spelled with its body *rank*, not its body label, so","+a key's size depends on the element's structure and not on the length","+of a stream descriptor (§2 \"Work\")."," ","-**F4. The residue, the component rule, and the refusal predicate.** What","-survives the fixpoint is a worklist of tie groups whose unnumbered cursor","-cells occur only inside tied elements. Link two tied elements when they","-share an unnumbered cursor cell; a connected set of tied elements is a","+**F4. What refuses, and what is lost against 6a.** What survives a run's","+fixpoint is a worklist of tie groups whose unnumbered cursor cells occur","+only inside tied elements. Link two tied elements when they share an","+unnumbered cursor cell; a connected set of tied elements is a"," **component**. Two cases:"," - *Certified*: no component holds two elements of one group. Every","-  component is then encoded by a local run over its elements in group","-  order (so a component spanning two groups, `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]`, spells its","-  shared cell once with one index), components are sorted by that","-  encoding, and the least run of equal encodings is entered in host","-  order. Equal encodings here mean *isomorphic* components, and the","-  entered state is the same up to isomorphism whichever order the host","-  lists them in (§3, lemma C). `#{X Y}`, `#{[X X] [Y Y]}`, `{X 1, Y 1}`,","-  `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}` and `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` are this case.","-- *Not certified* (D5): some component holds two elements of one tie","-  group, at some fixpoint of some run. Then entering either element first","-  is a choice this procedure cannot show to be harmless, and it refuses.","-  This is **\"not certified by this procedure\"**, not \"non-interchangeable\":","-  `#{[A B] [B A]}` and the 3-cycle `#{[A B] [B C] [C A]}` are refused","-  although every entering order gives the same rows (the swap and the","-  rotation are automorphisms); the path `#{[A B] [B C]}` and the","-  seven-cell triangle plus square (`rows_test.cljc:392-404`) are refused","-  and genuinely need a tie-break (§6 shows two orders with different","-  roots). The loop plus 2-cycle `#{[L L] [A B] [B A]}`","-  (`rows_test.cljc:312-329`) now numbers `L = cc-1` by its key and","-  refuses on `{A, B}`.","-  The predicate, in one sentence: *a state is refused iff, at a","-  fixpoint, two elements of one guest set or map have equal keys and are","-  connected by a chain of shared unnumbered cursor cells through tied","-  elements.* Such a state needs two or more cursor cells with equal bodies","-  that are held *only* through a guest set or map whose elements mention","-  more than one of them each. How often real states do that is **not","-  measured**: the repository has no version-3 task corpus yet (the Stage E","-  scenarios and the v2 handoff census tests hold cursor cells but no v3","-  lift exists before 6b), so §6 reports only the fixture inventory (two","-  of the repository's fixtures hit D5, both built to) and the random","-  generator's rate under a tie-biased distribution. The first draft's","-  rarity claim is withdrawn. D5 can be narrowed later by a bounded search","-  over the residue without changing the numbering of any state D1–D4","-  number today, because D1–D4's decisions precede it.","+  component is then encoded by a component run over its elements in","+  group order (so a component spanning two groups, `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]`,","+  spells its shared cell once with one index), components are sorted by","+  that encoding, and the least run of equal encodings is entered in host","+  order. Equal encodings here mean *isomorphic* components (§3, lemma C).","+  `#{X Y}`, `#{[X X] [Y Y]}`, `{X 1, Y 1}`, `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}` and","+  `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` are this case.","+- *Conflict*: some component holds two elements of one tie group. Then","+  entering either element first is a choice D1–D4 cannot show to be","+  harmless. **Which runs refuse.** Only the *root run* and *component","+  runs* refuse on a conflict (D5). A *key run* never refuses: it is a","+  speculation made to sort, its unresolved residue is spelled `[:tie …]`","+  in the key, and the enclosing run may resolve the same elements later.","+  The second review's witness is exactly this: in `[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]`","+  the key run over the inner set reaches a conflict (`[A B]` and `[B A]`","+  tie and share both cells), the outer set has one element and is","+  entered, the inner tie is deferred to the root worklist, index 1 meets","+  `A = cc-1`, and the root fixpoint separates `[A B]` (`[(n 1) (u0)]`)","+  from `[B A]` (`[(u0) (n 1)]`), so `B = cc-2`; the lift numbers and the","+  receiver recomputes the same root (`9600fe01…`, the review's digest).","+  The earlier text's \"at any fixpoint of any run\" was wrong and is","+  replaced in D5, lemma D and A1.","+  The predicate, in one sentence: *a state is refused under D5 iff the","+  root run, or a component run it requires, reaches a fixpoint at which","+  two elements of one guest set or map have equal keys and are connected","+  by a chain of shared unnumbered cursor cells through tied elements.*"," ","+  **Exactly which structures that is.** A D5 state needs two or more","+  cursor cells with *equal bodies* (same stream identity and descriptor,","+  same position) that are held *only* through guest sets or maps, in","+  elements that each mention more than one of them, with equal keys. The","+  smallest are the directed 2-cycle `#{[A B] [B A]}` and the directed","+  3-cycle `#{[A B] [B C] [C A]}` (both interchangeable: the swap and the","+  rotation are automorphisms, so any entering order gives the same rows,","+  and 6a numbers them); the path `#{[A B] [B C]}` and the seven-cell","+  triangle plus square (`rows_test.cljc:392-404`) are refused and","+  genuinely need a tie-break (§6: two and four distinct roots under","+  host-order entry); the loop plus 2-cycle `#{[L L] [A B] [B A]}`","+  (`rows_test.cljc:312-329`) numbers `L = cc-1` by its key and conflicts","+  on `{A, B}`. One register, environment binding, pending slot or vector","+  position holding any one of the cells breaks the tie for the whole","+  component (`[#{X Y} X]`, the witness above). **Are they common in","+  realistic suspended tasks?** Unmeasured: the repository has no","+  version-3 task corpus (the Stage E scenarios and the v2 handoff census","+  tests hold cursor cells but no v3 lift exists before 6b). The structural","+  argument is that a cursor cell is created into a register","+  (`:stream/cursor` delivers to `rd`, engine `:2595-2602`) and reaches a","+  guest set only by guest code that builds a set of tuples of cursors on","+  one stream at one position and then drops every other reference; of","+  the repository's own fixtures only the two built for the purpose hit","+  D5, and under a tie-biased generator the rate is 35 of 2300 (§6).","+  Because the rate is unmeasured and 6a numbers the 2-cycle and 3-cycle,","+  this note does not leave the regression as a documented limitation:","+  **D6** (§2) is the minimum extra rule that repairs it, a search over","+  the conflicting component only (two trials for a 2-cycle, three for a","+  3-cycle, eleven for the seven-cell state), prototyped and measured in","+  §6: with D6 every one of the 2300 generated states numbers, the 2-cycle","+  and 3-cycle reach 6a's exact roots, and no D1–D4 numbering changes.","+  The owner's choice between D5 (refuse, no search anywhere) and D6","+  (bounded residue search) is §10 question 1; this note recommends D6.","+"," **F5. The scheme is canonical.** Proved in §3: the output (rows or"," refusal) is a function of the abstract task up to isomorphism (renaming"," of cursor cells, permutation of every unordered container and of the","-census), terminating, with work bounded by §2 \"Work\".","+census) and terminating; the work is accounted by the budget of §2","+\"Work\"."," "," **F6. Prototype evidence (§6).** 2000 random value states (one to six"," cursor cells over two bodies, so ties are common; nested vectors, sets,"," maps with cursor-cell keys, sets of cursor-cell pairs including aliases"," such as `[X X]`, sets of sets, cells aliased across containers), 200"," frame-bearing states (act window, K-frame window, environments, a parked","-state) and 100 states with install children: the prototype gave equal","-root ids after renaming every cursor cell and shuffling every set, map","-and census, with no failure; every numbered carrier decoded through 6a's","-decoder and recomputed to its own root; it refused 35 of 2300. The","-review's three counterexamples number canonically; the pinned fixture","-root `fab223…` and the ten-cell census root are unchanged; the new pins","-for the counterexamples equal the digests the review computed","-independently for the same numbering.","+state) and 100 states with install children, each compared with itself","+renamed and shuffled and decoded through 6a's decoder: 0 invariance","+failures and 0 decode failures in either mode; D5 refuses 35 of 2300, D6","+refuses none; the largest `:work` charged is 1470 of 6a's default","+200000 and the largest trial count 13 of 10000. Every pin of rounds 1","+and 2 is unchanged (including under the rank spelling and with D6 on);","+the second review's digest for the witness is reproduced; the key bytes","+of one state under a 65536-character descriptor fell from 920118 (label","+spelling) to 1396 (rank spelling), the same as under a 128-character","+descriptor."," "," **F7. Portable ids minted in the VM are the wrong fix.** §5."," "]},{"oldStart":171,"oldLines":28,"newStart":220,"newLines":27,"lines":[" refusal. Install children are numbered as their own tasks by the same"," rule (task boundary, unchanged), in any order, since scopes are disjoint."," ","-**Body label.** `[:body [:stream identity descriptor] position]`, the","-cursor cell's census body as plain data.","-"," **Spelling (the content encoding `CE`).** A run spells the item it has"," traversed under its final numbering. The root run spells a cursor cell"," numbered `k` as `[:cursor-cell [:n k]]`, `k` an exact integer; a local"," run spells a cursor cell it numbered itself (index `j`, counted from the","-enclosing run's local counter) as `[:cursor-cell [:u j <body label>]]`","-and a cursor cell numbered by an enclosing run as that run spelled it.","-Nothing else changes from carrier §6 step 2: `[:lit d]` as itself,","-`[:row B]` expanded in place, structural data as itself, the census as","-`[:cursor-cells]`, an install child as `[:child]` (this deliberately","-replaces step 2's `[:child <child root id>]`: a child sits only under a","-name-keyed install entry, never inside an unordered container, so its","-content never decides an order, and the encoding stays hash-free). Keyed","-structural rows are spelled in their canonical key order, `:window` in","-numeric register order; a `:set` or `:map` is spelled as the bytewise","-sorted vector of its elements' spellings (an entry as `[:entry K V]`),","-duplicates retained, where a tie group the run could not resolve is","-spelled in place as `[:tie K … K]` (its common key, once per element).","-All comparisons are bytewise on canonical CBOR of these synthetic","-vectors. `CE` is hash-free and linear in the expansion of its argument.","+enclosing run's local counter) as `[:cursor-cell [:u j r]]`, `r` the","+cell's body rank (an exact integer; rank order equals body-label order,","+so no comparison and no numbering changes against spelling the label,","+and the key's size no longer depends on the descriptor), and a cursor","+cell numbered by an enclosing run as that run spelled it. Nothing else","+changes from carrier §6 step 2: `[:lit d]` as itself, `[:row B]`","+expanded in place, structural data as itself, the census as","+`[:cursor-cells]`, an install child as `[:child]` (replacing step 2's","+`[:child <child root id>]`: a child sits only under a name-keyed install","+entry, never inside an unordered container, so its content never decides","+an order, and the encoding stays hash-free). Keyed structural rows are","+spelled in their canonical key order, `:window` in numeric register","+order; a `:set` or `:map` is spelled as the bytewise sorted vector of its","+elements' spellings (an entry as `[:entry K V]`), duplicates retained,","+where a tie group the run could not resolve is spelled in place as","+`[:tie K … K]` (its common key, once per element). All comparisons are","+bytewise on canonical CBOR of these synthetic vectors. `CE` is hash-free."," "," **D1. Traversal.** Depth-first, pre-order, from the run's root item. A"," row's slots are visited in table order. Ordered repeated slots"]},{"oldStart":200,"oldLines":12,"newStart":248,"newLines":11,"lines":[" keys (`:bindings`, `:store`, `:parked-table`, the root's `installs` and"," `module-stores`) in the Jing canonical byte order of the key, the order"," §5 emits; `:window` in ascending numeric register order as the carrier","-specifies (the same as byte order for `N`, but the rule is numeric). The","-census is not entered; an install child's `:task` row is not entered. A","-`:set`'s elements and a `:map`'s entries are each given a **key**: the","-spelling of a local run over the element under the numbering at the","-moment the container is entered (every element keyed from that one","-numbering). Elements are then taken in bytewise key order: a run of","+specifies. The census is not entered; an install child's `:task` row is","+not entered. A `:set`'s elements and a `:map`'s entries are each given a","+**key**: the spelling of a key run over the element under the numbering","+at the moment the container is entered (every element keyed from that","+one numbering). Elements are then taken in bytewise key order: a run of"," equal keys is a **tie group**, appended to the run's worklist (deferral"," order is canonical: containers in traversal order, groups within one"," container in key order); every other element is entered in key order,"]},{"oldStart":219,"oldLines":47,"newStart":266,"newLines":69,"lines":[" enclosing run). An already numbered cursor cell is passed over. There is"," no first-occurrence spelling: every occurrence emits"," `[:cursor-cell :yin.k/cc-n]` and the census row (carrier §3) stays the","-contents table, so content rows keep sharing by Merkle id whether or not","-they hold a cursor cell's first occurrence.","+contents table."," "," **D3. Fixpoint.** After the traversal, the worklist's groups are"," re-examined in deferral order: each group's elements are re-keyed under"," the current numbering and taken as in D1 (singletons entered, equal runs"," re-deferred in order). Repeat while a round numbers at least one cursor"," cell and groups remain. Entering an element inside a group runs D1 on","-it, so a group discovered *inside* an entered element (the review's","-`#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}`) joins the same worklist and is re-examined in the","-next round; there is one worklist per run, and the root run's worklist","-collects deferred groups from every depth.","+it, so a group discovered *inside* an entered element (`#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}`)","+joins the same worklist and is re-examined in the next round; there is","+one worklist per run, and the root run's worklist collects deferred","+groups from every depth."," "," **D4. Components.** When a fixpoint is reached with groups remaining,"," link two tied elements (of any groups) that share an unnumbered cursor"," cell; a connected set of tied elements is a **component**. (At a"," fixpoint every unnumbered cursor cell occurs only inside tied elements,","-so all of its occurrences lie in one component.) If no component holds","-two elements of one group: encode each component by a local run over the","-synthetic ordered row `[:component g₁ e₁ g₂ e₂ …]` of its elements in","-group order (`gᵢ` the group's deferral sequence number, a datum), require","-that run to be complete (no `[:tie]` in its spelling, else D5), sort the","-components by their encodings, and **enter the run of least encoding**:","-each of its components has its local run's cursor cells numbered, in that","-run's local-index order, continuing the enclosing run's numbering;","-components of equal encoding are entered in host order (§3, lemma C:","-equal encodings are isomorphic and the result is the same). Remove the","-entered elements from their groups and return to D3. A group whose","-elements mention no unnumbered cursor cell holds equal values (the","+so all of its occurrences lie in one component.) Encode each component","+by a component run over the synthetic ordered row","+`[:component g₁ i₁ g₂ i₂ …]` in group order, `gᵢ` the group's deferral","+sequence number (a datum) and `iᵢ` the component's one element of that","+group, or, when the component holds several elements of group `gᵢ`, a","+synthetic `:set` of them (the D5/D6 case). A component run must be","+complete (no `[:tie]` in its spelling); it is incomplete only when it","+reaches a conflict of its own, which D5 or D6 decides. Sort the","+components by their encodings and **enter the run of least encoding**:","+each of its components has its component run's cursor cells numbered,","+in that run's local-index order, continuing the enclosing run's","+numbering; components of equal encoding are entered in host order (§3,","+lemma C: equal encodings are isomorphic and the result is the same).","+Remove the entered elements from their groups and return to D3. A group","+whose elements mention no unnumbered cursor cell holds equal values (the"," carrier refuses the duplicate at §8 step 5); its elements are entered in"," host order, which numbers nothing and emits identical rows."," ","-**D5. Refusal.** If at any fixpoint of any run a component holds two","-elements of one group (directly, or inside a component's local run), the","-lift refuses `:yin.k/non-portable` kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`,","-`:yin.k/path` the row path of the container holding that group, and a","-`:yin.k/cursor-cells` list of the component's unnumbered cursor cells.","-A receiver that reaches D5 while recomputing refuses `:yin.k/undecodable`","-kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`. Neither is a resource refusal and neither","-is `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`.","+**D5. Conflict, and refusal without D6.** A component **conflicts** when","+it holds two elements of one group. Without D6, a conflict reached by the","+root run, or by a component run (directly, or through a nested component","+run of its own), refuses the lift: `:yin.k/non-portable` kind","+`:unresolved-cursor-cells`, `:yin.k/path` the row path of the container","+holding the least conflicting group (for an install child, prefixed","+`[12 <install name> …]`), and `:yin.k/cursor-cells` the sorted unnumbered","+cursor cells of every conflicting component at that fixpoint (so the","+diagnostic, like the verdict, does not depend on host order). A key run","+never refuses (§0; F4). A receiver that reaches D5 while recomputing","+refuses `:yin.k/undecodable` kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`. Neither is","+a resource refusal and neither is `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`."," ","+**D6. Residue search (recommended; the repair of F4's regression).** A","+conflict is resolved instead of refused: let `g` be the least conflicting","+group of the component and `E` the component's elements of `g`. For each","+`e ∈ E` run a **trial**: a component run over the same `[:component …]`","+row in which `e` is entered first when its container is keyed, before","+its siblings are keyed (so `e`'s cursor cells take the first local","+indices and its siblings are keyed against them), and which runs to","+completion by D1–D6. Each trial charges `:search` one unit. The","+component's encoding is the least trial spelling, and entering the","+component adopts that trial's numbering (D4). Equal least spellings are","+isomorphic and either may be adopted (§3, lemma E). With D6 no","+structural refusal remains: the only refusals of the procedure are the","+resource refusals of \"Work\" below, and `:unresolved-cursor-cells` is not","+defined. With D6 in force, 6a's `:search` budget key stays and counts","+trials; without it, the key is deleted.","+"," **Totality.** The root run ends with an empty worklist or a refusal."," Before emission the implementation asserts that the numbering is a"," bijection from the census's cursor cells onto `1 … n`; a violation is a"]},{"oldStart":268,"oldLines":48,"newStart":337,"newLines":62,"lines":[" "," **Memoization (implementation note, not part of the definition).** A"," local run's result is a function of its item, of the enclosing numbering","-restricted to the item's cursor cells, and of the enclosing local","-counter; an implementation may memoize on that key and must return the","-numbering restricted to the item's own cursor cells (the prototype's one","-bug during revision was returning the whole numbering of another","-element's context, which the decode round trip caught).","+restricted to the item's cursor cells, of the enclosing local counter and","+(D6) of the forced element; an implementation may memoize on that key and","+must return the numbering restricted to the item's own cursor cells (the","+prototype's one bug during round 2 was returning the whole numbering of","+another element's context, which the decode round trip caught). The memo","+holds only spellings that were paid for under `:work`, so it adds no","+unaccounted bytes. No bound on the number of distinct memo contexts is","+claimed; the memo is an optimization and the budget is the bound."," "," **Validation and decode.** Unchanged in form: the receiver decodes, runs"," the root run, re-emits and compares root ids (`:noncanonical-cursor-cells`"," on mismatch). In 6a's `decode*` the recomputation is `emit` itself","-(`rows.cljc:570-575`); it must be wrapped so that **only** a lift-side","-`:yin.k/non-portable` of kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells` raised by the","-recomputation is translated to `:yin.k/undecodable` kind","-`:unresolved-cursor-cells` with `:yin.k/path` prefixed by the root id;","+(`rows.cljc:570-575`), which numbers the root *and every install child*","+(`emit-node`, `:185-189`); the **whole** of that call is wrapped so that","+only a lift-side `:yin.k/non-portable` of kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`","+(D5, from the root or from a child) is translated to `:yin.k/undecodable`","+kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells` with `:yin.k/path` prefixed by the","+carried root id (a child's path then reads `[<root id> 12 <name> …]`);"," a resource refusal (`:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit`) propagates as"," itself; no other non-portable kind can arise there, because step 5 has","-already checked literals and markers. The validator order stays: step 6","-(numbering) after step 5 and before `validate-frames` (`rows.cljc:576-578`;","-test `numbering-precedes-frame-defect`, `rows_test.cljc:206`).","+already checked literals and markers. The second review's escape (an","+install child's D5 leaving `decode*` as `:yin.k/non-portable` with path","+`[:child 15]`) came from translating only the root's numbering; the","+prototype now wraps the whole recomputation and the case is in §6. The","+validator order stays: step 6 (numbering) after step 5 and before","+`validate-frames` (`rows.cljc:576-578`; test","+`numbering-precedes-frame-defect`, `rows_test.cljc:206`). With D6 the","+translation has nothing to translate and is omitted."," ","-**Work.** Let `P` be the number of expanded positions of the task (the","-tree after expanding every `[:row B]` and structural reference in place;","-a shared row counts once per reference, so `P` can exceed the row count","-and the implementation must bound it, not the row count), `d` the","-container nesting depth, `n` the number of cursor cells, `m` the largest","-element count of one container. Then, with memoized local runs, the","-procedure visits each position at most `(n + 1)` times per nesting level","-(a local run over an element is recomputed only when the numbering","-restricted to that element's cursor cells changes, at most `n` times),","-every encoding is linear in its element's expansion, and each container","-sort costs `O(B · log m)` compare work for `B` key bytes; so","-`W = O(P · (d + 1) · (n + 1) · log m)` positions-and-bytes, and the bytes","-materialized at any time are `O(W)` plus the memo, itself `O(P · (d + 1)","-· (n + 1))`. Without memoization the bound is `O(P · (n + 1)^(d+1) · log m)`.","-Both are accounted before allocation by the budgets that stay: `:work`","-is charged one unit per position visited or spelled **and** one unit per","-literal byte copied into a spelling (so every allocated spelling byte","-has been paid for), `:depth` bounds `d` and the structural depth as","-today, `:cursor-cells` bounds `n`, `:rows` bounds the carried rows and","-`:bytes` the carried bytes, which together bound every literal a spelling","-can copy. The `:search` and `:label-bytes` budgets disappear with the","-search and the growing labels. Exceeding a budget is the resource refusal","-of carrier §10.10, never a canonicality verdict.","+**Work.** No polynomial bound is claimed. The second review showed the","+round-2 bound counted positions but not bytes (a 65536-character stream","+descriptor multiplied key bytes by 170 at fixed `P`, `d`, `n`, `m`) and","+that memoization's benefit across differing local contexts is unproved.","+The procedure is instead *accounted*, as 6a already accounts refinement:","+the `:work` budget is charged one unit per position visited (D1, D3,","+every local run) and one unit per position spelled plus one unit per","+byte of literal or structural datum copied into a spelling, before the","+copy; `:search` (D6) is charged one unit per trial; `:depth` bounds the","+structural depth as today, `:cursor-cells` bounds `n`, `:rows` the","+carried rows and `:bytes` the carried bytes. Exceeding any budget is the","+resource refusal of carrier §10.10 (`:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit","+[<key>]`), never a canonicality verdict, exactly as 6a's `:label-bytes`","+was. The budget charges every allocated spelling byte before allocation,","+so the bytes materialized at any time are bounded by `:work` plus the","+carried bytes. What the rank spelling guarantees is narrower and exact:","+a key's bytes are a function of the element's expanded structure and of","+the number of distinct bodies, not of the bodies' sizes (§6: 1396 key","+bytes at both 128 and 65536 descriptor characters). The crude bound","+without memoization is `O(P · (n + 1)^(d + 1) · log m)` positions and","+`O(B)` bytes per spelling (`P` expanded positions counted per reference,","+`B` expanded bytes, `d` the unordered-container nesting depth, `n` cursor","+cells, `m` the largest container), which the budget enforces rather than","+the analysis. Measured: every fixture of §6 charges under 1000 `:work`","+units, the 2300 generated states at most 1470, against 6a's default","+200000; the seven-cell state charges 488 and 11 trials under D6. The","+`:label-bytes` budget disappears with the growing labels."," "," ## 3. Proof of canonicality"," "]},{"oldStart":318,"oldLines":56,"newStart":401,"newLines":82,"lines":[" other: the structures agree up to renaming by `π` and up to the host"," order of every unordered container and of the census, numbers agree"," (`N'(π(c)) = N(c)`), and worklists correspond group by group in order.","-Let `S` be an abstract task.","+The proof is a simultaneous induction over runs, ordered by (number of","+unnumbered cursor cells of the run's item, nesting depth of the item):","+a key run, a component run or a trial is over an item with no more","+unnumbered cells than its enclosing run and strictly fewer nested","+unordered containers, or (a trial) strictly fewer unnumbered cells; so","+the lemmas below may assume themselves for every local run a run spawns."," "," **Lemma A (keys are canonical).** The key of an element is the spelling","-of a local run over it, and a local run reads only structure, body","-labels and numbers; its own listed-order inputs are (i) element order","-inside nested containers, which it never uses except through keys, by","-induction on nesting depth, and (ii) the host order of components of","-equal encoding in its D4 step, covered by lemma C. So isomorphic items","-under corresponding numberings have byte-identical keys, and the sorted","-order of a container's elements is a function of the configuration up","-to isomorphism.","+of a key run over it, and a key run reads only structure, body ranks","+(the rank of a body is a function of the census up to isomorphism) and","+numbers; its own listed-order inputs are (i) element order inside nested","+containers, which it never uses except through keys, by the induction,","+(ii) the host order of components of equal encoding in its D4 step,","+covered by lemma C, and (iii) nothing else: a key run that reaches a","+conflict does not choose, it spells the residue `[:tie K … K]`, which is","+a function of the group's common key and size. So isomorphic items under","+corresponding numberings have byte-identical keys, and the sorted order","+of a container's elements is a function of the configuration up to","+isomorphism."," "," **Lemma B (ties are exactly what keys cannot separate).** By"," construction: elements are deferred iff their keys are equal bytes."," "," **Lemma C (components).** At a fixpoint let `C₁ … Cₘ` be the components","-of one run of equal encodings, none holding two elements of one group,","-and let `U₁ … Uₘ` be their unnumbered cursor cells. The `Uᵢ` are","-pairwise disjoint and each `Uᵢ` is confined to `Cᵢ` (every occurrence of","-an unnumbered cell lies in a tied element, hence in its own component).","-Equal, complete encodings of `[:component g₁ e₁ …]` rows are a faithful","+of one run of equal encodings, each encoding complete, and let","+`U₁ … Uₘ` be their unnumbered cursor cells. The `Uᵢ` are pairwise","+disjoint and each `Uᵢ` is confined to `Cᵢ` (every occurrence of an","+unnumbered cell lies in a tied element, hence in its own component).","+Equal, complete encodings of `[:component g₁ i₁ …]` rows are a faithful"," spelling (literals as themselves, rows expanded, every cursor cell by","-index and body, groups by sequence number), so they exhibit, for each","-pair `Cᵢ, Cⱼ`, a bijection `Uᵢ → Uⱼ` under which the components are the","-same structure group by group. Entering the run in host order `σ` gives","-the cells of `C_{σ(t)}` the numbers `b + (t−1)·|U| + 1 …` by local index;","-in order `σ'` the cells of `C_{σ'(t)}` receive the same numbers. The","-block-swap bijection composed from the pairwise bijections is an","-isomorphism of the two resulting configurations: numbers agree by","-construction, structures agree because the swapped components are","-isomorphic and nothing outside them mentions their cells, and the","-worklists agree because the same groups remain, with the same elements","-removed. ∎","+index and body rank, groups by sequence number, a group's several","+elements as a sorted set), so they exhibit, for each pair `Cᵢ, Cⱼ`, a","+bijection `Uᵢ → Uⱼ` under which the components are the same structure","+group by group. Entering the run in host order `σ` gives the cells of","+`C_{σ(t)}` the numbers `b + (t−1)·|U| + 1 …` by local index; in order","+`σ'` the cells of `C_{σ'(t)}` receive the same numbers. The block-swap","+bijection composed from the pairwise bijections is an isomorphism of the","+two resulting configurations: numbers agree by construction, structures","+agree because the swapped components are isomorphic and nothing outside","+them mentions their cells, and the worklists agree because the same","+groups remain, with the same elements removed. ∎"," ","-**Lemma D (refusal is invariant).** Whether a component holds two","-elements of one group depends only on which tied elements share","-unnumbered cursor cells, which is a function of the configuration up to","-isomorphism; and whether a component's local run is complete is, by","-lemma A applied to that run, likewise invariant.","+**Lemma D (the D5 verdict is invariant).** Whether the root run, or a","+component run it requires, reaches a fixpoint with a conflicting","+component depends only on which tied elements share unnumbered cursor","+cells at that fixpoint, which is a function of the configuration up to","+isomorphism (lemma A gives the ties, the sharing relation is structural);","+and whether a component run is complete is the same question one level","+down, by the induction. Key runs do not enter the verdict: their","+conflicts are spelled, not refused, and the spelling is canonical (lemma","+A iii)."," ","+**Lemma E (D6 trials).** At a conflicting component the set `E` of","+elements of its least conflicting group is a function of the","+configuration up to isomorphism (lemma D names the component and its","+groups canonically). Each trial is a deterministic function of (the","+configuration up to isomorphism, the chosen element), by the induction","+on the trial run, which has strictly fewer unnumbered cells than the","+enclosing run (its forced element holds at least one, being linked into","+the component by a shared unnumbered cell). So the multiset of trial","+spellings is canonical and its least member is canonical. Two trials","+with equal least spellings have produced isomorphic numberings of the","+component (faithful spelling, as in lemma C), so adopting either yields","+isomorphic configurations. ∎","+"," **Theorem.** The procedure's output on isomorphic configurations is the"," same: both refuse at corresponding points, or both emit byte-identical","-rows. *Proof.* By induction on the number of steps. D1 and D2 decisions","-are functions of the configuration up to isomorphism (lemma A); D3 only","-repeats D1 on deferred groups in a canonical order; D4's choice of the","-least run is canonical (lemma A on component encodings) and its only","-listed-order input is harmless (lemma C); D5 is invariant (lemma D).","-Emission spells cursor cells by number only and sorts every emitted","-unordered slot by canonical bytes under the final numbering (carrier §5),","-so isomorphic final configurations emit the same rows. ∎","+rows. *Proof.* By the induction above. D1 and D2 decisions are functions","+of the configuration up to isomorphism (lemma A); D3 only repeats D1 on","+deferred groups in a canonical order; D4's choice of the least run is","+canonical (lemma A on component encodings, which are complete by D5/D6)","+and its only listed-order input is harmless (lemma C); D5 is invariant","+(lemma D); D6 is invariant (lemma E). Emission spells cursor cells by","+number only and sorts every emitted unordered slot by canonical bytes","+under the final numbering (carrier §5), so isomorphic final","+configurations emit the same rows. ∎"," "," Consequences:"," "]},{"oldStart":376,"oldLines":12,"newStart":485,"newLines":13,"lines":["    both. No decision reads a source id or a host order except at the","    point lemma C covers."," 2. *Terminating.* The structural edge family is acyclic (carrier §8 step","-   3), so every traversal is finite. In the root run each D3 round that","-   is not the last numbers a cursor cell; each D4 step numbers at least","-   one cursor cell or removes a duplicate group; local runs are finite","-   by the same argument one level down and there are finitely many","-   nesting levels. So the procedure ends after at most `n` numbering","-   events per run plus one terminal round, within the bound of §2 \"Work\".","+   3), so every traversal is finite. In any run each D3 round that is not","+   the last numbers a cursor cell; each D4 step numbers at least one","+   cursor cell or removes a duplicate group; each local run is over an","+   item with fewer unordered containers nested below it than its","+   spawning run's item, or (a trial) with fewer unnumbered cells, so the","+   induction measure of §3 decreases and there are finitely many local","+   runs. The budget of §2 \"Work\" is what bounds the count."," 3. *Deterministic*, hence two lifts of one state are byte-identical and","    the receiver's recomputation is well defined."," 4. *Sharing survives*: the numbering is a bijection on reached cursor"]},{"oldStart":391,"oldLines":44,"newStart":501,"newLines":46,"lines":[" 5. *Cross-engine law*: two engines lifting one canonical frame produce","    abstract tasks equal up to renaming (the frame is engine-independent,","    continuation §2.1) and therefore, by the theorem, equal rows.","+6. *D6 moves no D1–D4 id.* Trials run only where D4 found a conflict,","+   which without D6 is a refusal; every numbering D1–D4 assign is","+   identical with and without D6 (§6 checks the pins both ways)."," ","-What is given up: the procedure does not number every state. D5 names","-the refused class exactly (§1 F4); a later bounded search over that","-residue would not change the numbering of any state D1–D4 number today,","-so the refusal can be narrowed without churning ids.","-"," ## 4. Amendments"," "," **A1 — carrier §6, from \"The procedure.\" to the end of step 5.** Replace","-with §2 above (vocabulary, the body label, the spelling, D1–D5, totality,","-work). Keep the section's first paragraph (the counterexample) and amend","-\"What is defined, and what is not claimed\" to: \"The canonical numbering","-is defined as the output of the discovery-order procedure below, which is","-a function of the state up to renaming of cursor cells and host","-enumeration of guest sets, maps and the census (proof in","+with §2 above (vocabulary, the spelling, D1–D6, totality, work). Keep the","+section's first paragraph (the counterexample) and amend \"What is","+defined, and what is not claimed\" to: \"The canonical numbering is defined","+as the output of the discovery-order procedure below, which is a function","+of the state up to renaming of cursor cells and host enumeration of guest","+sets, maps and the census (proof in"," `yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md` §3). It is not the","-minimum of the root bytes over all bijections. A state in which two","-equal-keyed elements of one guest set or map are linked by shared","-unnumbered cursor cells at a fixpoint is refused (D5), not searched; the","-refusal certifies nothing about interchangeability and can be narrowed","-later without changing any numbering assigned today.\" State in step 2's","-replacement that `[:n k]` is an exact CBOR integer and that `[:child]`","-replaces `[:child <child root id>]` (reason in §2 \"Spelling\").","+minimum of the root bytes over all bijections. The one choice the","+traversal cannot make by itself, between two equal-keyed elements of one","+guest set or map that are linked by shared unnumbered cursor cells at a","+fixpoint of the root run or of a component run, is resolved by a search","+confined to that component (D6) [or, in the D5 variant: is refused (D5),","+not searched; the refusal certifies nothing about interchangeability and","+can be narrowed later without changing any numbering assigned today].\"","+State in step 2's replacement that `[:n k]` and `[:u j r]` carry exact","+CBOR integers, that `r` is the body rank, and that `[:child]` replaces","+`[:child <child root id>]` (reason in §2 \"Spelling\")."," "," **A2 — carrier §6 \"Validation\".** Replace the paragraph's last two"," sentences, from \"Every labeling that yields the selected commitment is"," thereby accepted, and no encounter-order tie-break ever decides validity\""," to the end, with: \"Every labeling that yields the selected commitment is","-thereby accepted. The procedure reads the canonical traversal order of","-D1, which is a function of the state; it never reads the host's","-enumeration order of a set, map or census, and a receiver never decides","-validity by its own enumeration order. This is a lower-side structural","-refusal, not a lift kind. A receiver whose work bound (§10.10) is","-exceeded before the procedure completes refuses with the structured","-resource refusal before any attachment, never labels the state","-`:noncanonical-cursor-cells`, never substitutes host enumeration order,","-and never accepts an unfinished check; a receiver that reaches D5","-refuses `:yin.k/undecodable` kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`.\"","+thereby accepted. The emitted commitment and the acceptance decision are","+invariant under the host's enumeration order of every set, map and","+census (proof in the numbering note, §3); the procedure's one use of host","+order, entering components of equal encoding in the order listed, cannot","+change the rows (lemma C), and a receiver never decides validity by its","+own enumeration order. This is a lower-side structural refusal, not a","+lift kind. A receiver whose work bound (§10.10) is exceeded before the","+procedure completes refuses with the structured resource refusal before","+any attachment, never labels the state `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`, and","+never accepts an unfinished check [D5 variant: ; a receiver that reaches","+D5 refuses `:yin.k/undecodable` kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`].\""," "," **A3 — carrier §5 preamble.** Replace \"The class-erased skeletons of §6"," are used only inside the numbering procedure and never decide the order"]},{"oldStart":436,"oldLines":31,"newStart":548,"newLines":33,"lines":[" used only inside the numbering procedure and never decide the order of"," an emitted row\"."," ","-**A4 — carrier §8 step 6.** \"Recompute §6 under the work bound and","-compare (`:yin.k/undecodable`, kind `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`;","-`:yin.k/undecodable` kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells` if D5 is reached,","-translated from the recomputation's lift-side refusal of that kind only;","-or the resource refusal of §10.10 if the bound is exceeded, which","-propagates untranslated).\" In the diagnostics paragraph after step 12,","-add `:unresolved-cursor-cells` to the `:yin.k/undecodable` kinds named,","-and change \"the two kinds this carrier adds to UCF §7.5.4's set:","-`:byte-string` and `:metadata`\" to \"the three kinds this carrier adds to","-UCF §7.5.4's set: `:byte-string`, `:metadata` and","-`:unresolved-cursor-cells`\". In §11, \"two lift kinds and two","-`:yin.k/undecodable` kinds are added\" becomes \"three lift kinds and three","-`:yin.k/undecodable` kinds are added\".","+**A4 — carrier §8 step 6.** \"Recompute §6 under the work bound, for the","+root and every install child, and compare (`:yin.k/undecodable`, kind","+`:noncanonical-cursor-cells`; or the resource refusal of §10.10 if the","+bound is exceeded, which propagates untranslated).\" D5 variant: add","+\"`:yin.k/undecodable` kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells` if D5 is reached","+in the root or in a child, translated from the recomputation's lift-side","+refusal of that kind only, its path prefixed by the carried root id\";","+and in the diagnostics paragraph after step 12 add","+`:unresolved-cursor-cells` to the `:yin.k/undecodable` kinds, change","+\"the two kinds this carrier adds to UCF §7.5.4's set: `:byte-string` and","+`:metadata`\" to \"the three kinds …, `:metadata` and","+`:unresolved-cursor-cells`\", and in §11 \"two lift kinds and two","+`:yin.k/undecodable` kinds are added\" to \"three … and three …\". With D6","+these counts are unchanged."," "," **A5 — carrier §10.** Answer 9: \"a renaming-invariant"," refinement-and-search procedure\" → \"the discovery-order procedure, which"," is invariant under renaming and host enumeration\". Answer 10: \"a bound"," on decoded bytes, rows, cursor cells, traversal depth, refinement work"," and search nodes for §6\" → \"a bound on decoded bytes, rows, cursor cells,","-traversal depth, and traversal-and-spelling work for §6 (its `:work`","-unit counts positions and copied literal bytes, so spellings are paid","-for before they are allocated)\"; and \"never falls back to encounter","-order\" → \"never substitutes host enumeration order for the canonical","-traversal\". Proposals list: \"as defined by its procedure\" → \"as defined","-by discovery order, and the D5 refusal class\".","+traversal depth, traversal-and-spelling work and (D6) residue-search","+trials for §6 (its `:work` unit counts positions and copied bytes, so","+spellings are paid for before they are allocated)\"; and \"never falls","+back to encounter order\" → \"never substitutes host enumeration order for","+the canonical traversal\". Proposals list: \"as defined by its procedure\"","+→ \"as defined by discovery order [D5 variant: , and the D5 refusal","+class]\"."," "," **A6 — carrier §3, identity rows.** After \"minted by the lift under §6's"," canonical numbering\" add \"(the n-th cursor cell met in §6's canonical"]},{"oldStart":479,"oldLines":19,"newStart":593,"newLines":19,"lines":[" **cursor cells** (it reads no incoming cursor-cell id), and the only"," other identity it consumes is the stream marker in each cursor cell's"," body label (`[:body [:stream identity descriptor] position]`, draft §6","-as amended) and in cursor-cell bodies\". (ii) \"Unordered containers","-(`:set`, `:map`, `:store`, `:bindings`, the parked table) are encoded by","-sorting their elements' encodings bytewise (draft §6 step 2), never by","-host traversal order\" → \"Guest sets and maps are entered in the bytewise","-order of their elements' spellings, and `:store`, `:bindings` and the","-parked table in the byte order of their structural keys (draft §6 D1),","-never in host enumeration order\". (iii) Leave \"never derived from","-traversal order\" but